window.RV_LOADED['ib-mathematics-aa-sl'] = {"name":"IB Mathematics AA SL","topics":[{"name":"Functions","subtopics":[{"name":"Composite & Inverse Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1060","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1060\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe table below shows some values of the domain and range for the function $f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/aa1060a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1060a\">\n\nThe graph below shows $y=g(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/aa1060b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1060b\">\n\n1. Evaluate\n\n    1. $f(2)$\n\n    2. $g(f(2))$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Find $f^{-1}(g(2))$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Using the table for $f(x)$ we can write\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &\\boxed{f(2)=5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n    2. Using our previous answer and the point $(5, 3)$ on $g(x)$ we can write\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &g(f(2))=g(5) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{g(f(2))=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. We can evaluate $g(2)=4$ using the graph.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall the definition of an inverse function if $f(a)=b$ then $f^{-1}(b)=a$.\n\n    From the table we can see that $f(3)=4$, therefore \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f^{-1}(g(x))=f^{-1}(4)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\boxed{f^{-1}(4)=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA125","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA125\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{2x+1}$, for\n$-14 \\leq x \\leq 13$.\n\n1.  Write down the range of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for $f^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the domain and range of $f^{-1}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    1 $f$ is continuous and increasing;\n\n    2 $f(-14) = -3$ and $f(13) = 3$.\n\n    Hence the range of $f$ is\n     $[-3,3]$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[13pt]\n    f\\left(y\\right) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sqrt[3]{2y+1} &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    2y+1 &= x^3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x^3 - 1}{2} \\hspace{1.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence an\n    expression for $f^{-1}$ is\n     $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x^3 - 1}{2}$\n\n3.  The domain and range of $f^{-1}$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain}: & \\hspace{0.75em} \\hspace{0.525em}[-3,3] \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{range}: & \\hspace{0.75em} \\hspace{0.525em}[-14,13]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1269","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1269\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ for $-3\\leq x\\leq 4$ is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-q\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f(2)$. [1 mark]\n\nLet $g(x)=2f(x)-1$ for $-3\\leq x\\leq 4$. \n\n2.  On the axes above, sketch the graph of $g$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence determine the value of $(g\\circ f)(2) $. [1 mark]\n\n4.  Hence solve the equation $(f\\circ g)(x)=0$ when $x>0$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x)$ when $x=2$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a1\">\n\n    Hence $\\boxed{f(2)=1}$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n       \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  The function $f(x)$ is mapped to $g(x)$ by two transformations.\n\n    By considering $g(x)=\\textcolor{blue}{2}f(x)\\textcolor{green}{-1}$, we can see there is a $\\text{\\textcolor{blue}{vertical stretch by a scale factor of}}\\,\\,\\textcolor{blue}{2}$ and a $\\text{\\textcolor{green}{vertical shift down}}\\,\\textcolor{green}{1}\\,\\text{\\textcolor{green}{unit}}$.\n\n    The vertical stretch ($\\textcolor{blue}{y=2f(x)}$) is shown in blue below. \n\n    Note that the vertical distance from the $y$-axis of every point on the curve is doubled. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a2\">\n\n    Then the vertical shift is shown in green ($\\textcolor{green}{y=2f(x)-1}$) .\n\n    Note that every point on the blue curve is shifted vertically down $1$ unit.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a3\">\n\n    Correct local minimum at $(-2,1)$\n\n    $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Correct $y$-intercept at $(0,5)$\n\n    $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  From part (a) we know that $f(2)=1$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (g\\circ f)(2)&=g(f(2))\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=g(1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence $\\boxed{(g\\circ f)(2)=3}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Here is the graph of $g(x)$ showing that $g(1)=3$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a4\">\n\n4.  We have the equation $(f\\circ g)(x)=0$. The L.H.S. is a composite function which can be rewritten\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (f\\circ g)(x)&= f(\\textcolor{red}{g(x)})\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Here we can see that $\\textcolor{red}{g(x)}$ is the inner function and $f(x)$ is the outer function, meaning that the output of $g$ will be the input of $f$.\n\n    By considering the graph of $f(x)$ we see that when $x=3$ then $f(x)=0$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a5.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a5\">\n\n    This means in order to have an output of $0$, the input must be $3$.\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(\\textcolor{red}{g(x)})=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        f(\\textcolor{red}{3})=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we need to find the value of $x$, where $x>0$, such that $g(x)=3$. \n\n    From part (c), we know that $g(1)=3$, and we can see that this is the only possible solution when $x>0$.\n\n    Hence if $x>0$ and $(f\\circ g)(x)=0$ then $x=1$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA130","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA130\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x) = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{6+2x}{6-2x}}$, for\n$-3 \\leq x < 3$.\n\nFind the inverse function of $f$, stating its domain and range.","markScheme":"If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for $y = f^{-1}(x)$,\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[16pt]\nf(y) &= x \\\\[11pt]\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6+2y}{6-2y}} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{6+2y}{6-2y} &= x^2 \\\\[12pt]\n6 + 2y &= 6x^2-2x^2y \\\\[16pt]\n3 + y &= 3x^2-x^2y \\\\[16pt]\n(x^2 + 1)y &= 3x^2 - 3 \\\\[12pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{3x^2 - 3}{x^2 + 1} \\hspace{3em}  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the\ninverse function of $f$ is\n $f^{-1}(x) =  \\dfrac{3x^2 - 3}{x^2 + 1}$\n\nThe domain of $f^{-1}$ is\n $[0,\\infty)$\nand range of $f^{-1}$ is\n $[-3,3)$"},{"ref":"AA132","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA132\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 5x^2 + 3x + 2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the coordinates of the local\n    maximum point. Round your answers to **three significant figures**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nThe domain of $f$ is now restricted to $[a,b]$ where\n$a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Write down the smallest value of $a < 0$ and the largest value\n        of $b > 0$ for which $f$ has an inverse. Give your answers\n        correct to **three significant figures**.\n\n    2.  For these values of $a$ and $b$, sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$\n        and $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same set of axes, showing clearly the\n        coordinates of the end points of each curve.\n\n    3.  Solve the equation $f^{-1}(x) = -1$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/9444e78d503e38688d4c8ecfe5c29f2d0705556f.svg\" alt=\"9444e78d503e38688d4c8ecfe5c29f2d0705556f.svg\">\n\n    From the diagram, we see that $f$ is positive on the intervals\n    $(-\\infty,-2)$, $(-0.414,1)$ and $(2.41,\\infty)$, and negative on\n    $(-2,-0.414)$ and $(1,2.41)$.\n\n    Hence the set of solutions to the inequality $f(x) < 0$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (-2,-0.414)\\cup(1,2.41)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  From the diagram, we see that the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a local\n    maximum at point B.\n\n    Hence the coordinates of the local maximum point are\n     $(0.285, 2.43)$\n\n3.\n    1.  From the diagram, we see that $f$ is a bijection if its domain\n        is restricted to $[-1.14,0.285]$ or $[0.285,1.87]$.\n        Hence $f$ has an inverse if\n         $a = -1.41$\n        and  $b = 0.285$\n\n    2.  The graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$ across the line $y = x$.\n\n        Hence, if we sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = f^{-1}(x)$\n        on the same set of axes, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/b6185b0f42f1522008f7c0707456fd536c854029.svg\" alt=\"b6185b0f42f1522008f7c0707456fd536c854029.svg\">\n\n    3.  If we solve the equation $f^{-1}(x) = -1$ for $x$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(f^{-1}(x)) &= f(-1) \\hspace{4em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{10.7em} x = -4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(f^{-1}(x)) = x$}]\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA160","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA160\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3 + 1$ and $g(x) = x-2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Solve $(f\\circ g)(x) = 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x) = x^3 + 1$ for $x = 2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} f(2) &= 2^3 + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8 + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for $y = f^{-1}(x)$,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y^3 + 1 &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y^3 &= x - 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    y &= \\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2em} f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(f\\circ g)(x) = 0$ for $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(g(x)) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    f(x-2) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4.6em} (x-2)^3 + 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x-2 &= \\sqrt[3]{-1} \\hspace{3em} [=-1] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.7em} x = 1\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA161","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA161\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ and $g(x) = x - 3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f(3)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $(g\\circ f)(3)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $g^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ for $x = 3$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} f(3) &= 3^2 + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9 + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $(g\\circ f)(x) = g(f(x))$ for $x = 3$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.4em} (g\\circ f)(3) &= g(f(3)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(11) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11 - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    g(y) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 3 &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= x + 3 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g^{-1}(x) = x+3 \\hspace{0.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA165","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA165\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3$ and $g(x) = 2x - 1$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $g^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $(g\\circ f)(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Solve the equation $(g\\circ f)(x) = 0$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    g(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    2y - 1 &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    2y &= x + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x + 1}{2} \\hspace{1.9em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{2} \\hspace{0.6em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ f)(x) &= g(f(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(x^3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2x^3-1 \\hspace{1.1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(g\\circ f)(x) = 0$ for $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} 2x^3-1 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{12.2em} x \\approx 0.794 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA169","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA169\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sqrt{x-3}$, for $x \\geq 3$.\n\n1.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = x^2 +1$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Find $(g\\circ f)(28)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the range of:\n    1.  $f^{-1}$;\n\n    2.  $g$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[7pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\\\[5pt]\n    \\sqrt{y - 3} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 3 &= x^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= x^2 + 3 \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    f^{-1}(x) = x^2 + 3 \\hspace{0.4em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 3}$ for $x = 28$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} f(28) &= \\sqrt{28-3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ f)(28) &= g(f(28)) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= g(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5^2 +1 \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 26\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.\n        $[3,\\infty)$ or $y \\geq 3$\n\n    2.\n        $[1,\\infty)$ or $y \\geq 1$"},{"ref":"AA170","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA170\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = 4x - 3$ and $g(x) = 2x$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $g(5)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $(f\\circ g)(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $(f\\circ g)^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $g(5) = 10$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ g)(x) &= f(g(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(2x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4(2x) - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8x - 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $h(x) = (f\\circ g)(x)$.\n    Hence $h^{-1}(x) = (f\\circ g)^{-1}(x)$.\n\n    If we solve the equation $(h\\circ h^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = h^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(h^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    h(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y - 3 &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y &= x + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x + 3}{8} \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    (f\\circ g)^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x + 3}{8} \\hspace{2.7em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA171","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA171\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sqrt{x+7}$, for $x \\geq -7$.\n\n1.  Find $f^{-1}(3)$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g$ be a function such that $g^{-1}$ exists for all real numbers.\n\n2.  Given that $g(9) = 4$, find $(f\\circ g^{-1})(4)$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(3) = 3$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(3)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(3)) &= 3 \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y) &= 3 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\sqrt{y + 7} &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y + 7 &= 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 2 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    f^{-1}(3) = 2 \\hspace{2.4em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4 &= g(9) \\\\[6pt]\n    g^{-1}(4) &= g^{-1}(g(9)) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{11em} g^{-1}(4) &= 9 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{since $(g^{-1}\\hspace{-0.1em}\\circ g)(9) = 9$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ g^{-1})(4) &= f(g^{-1}(4)) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= f(9) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{9+7} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{16} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA174","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA174\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x+2)^3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g$ be a function so that $(f\\circ g)(x) = 27x^6$.\n\n2.  Find $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[7pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    (y+2)^3 &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y+2 &= \\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\sqrt[3]{x} - 2 \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.3em} f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x} - 2\n    $$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ g)(x) = 27x^6$ for $y = g(x)$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(g(x)) &= 27x^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y) &= 27x^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    (y+2)^3 &= 27x^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    y+2 &= 3x^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 3x^2 - 2 \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.5em} g(x) = 3x^2 - 2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA175","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA175\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$, for\n$-4 \\leq x \\leq 5$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/8dc73726e7704b5f5e31e8ab0e8d2ebbdc109321.svg\" alt=\"8dc73726e7704b5f5e31e8ab0e8d2ebbdc109321.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $f(1)$;\n\n    2.  $f^{-1}(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the domain of $f^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same grid above.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $f(1) = 3$\n\n    2.\n        $f^{-1}(2) = 0$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain of $f^{-1}$} &= \\text{range of $f$} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [-2,4]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$ across the line $y = x$.\n\n    Hence, if we sketch the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/90bb640268e36201860be09ad192280e600a64a2.svg\" alt=\"90bb640268e36201860be09ad192280e600a64a2.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA197","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA197\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nTwo functions, $f$ and $g$, are defined in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/f16cb92d520a6fc6d08cc849fc6b256348e1335f.svg\" alt=\"f16cb92d520a6fc6d08cc849fc6b256348e1335f.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f(2)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $(g\\circ f)(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $g^{-1}(5)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $f(2) = 0$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ f)(2) &= g(f(2)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ g^{-1})(5) &= 5 \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    g(g^{-1}(5)) &= 5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.7em} g^{-1}(5) = -4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $g(-4) = 5$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA200","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA200\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2x$, $g(x) = 4x + 6$ and $h(x) = (f\\circ g)(x)$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $h(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $h^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.3em} h(x) &= (f\\circ g)(x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(g(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(4x+6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(4x+6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8x+12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(h\\circ h^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = h^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(h^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    h(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y+12 &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y &= x-12 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x-12}{8} \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    h^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x-12}{8}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA207","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA207\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe functions $f$ and $g$ are defined such that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{3}$\nand $g(x) = 12x+4$.\n\n1.  Show that $(g\\circ f)(x) = 4x-4$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $(g\\circ f)^{-1}(a) = 10$, find the value of $a$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= g\\hspace{0.05em}(f(x)) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= g\\Big(\\dfrac{x-2}{3}\\Big) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 12\\Big(\\dfrac{x-2}{3}\\Big)+4 \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 4\\hspace{0.05em}(x-2) + 4 \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 4x-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.25em} (g\\circ f)^{-1}(a) &= 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    (g\\circ f)(10) &= a \\\\[6pt]\n    4(10)-4 &= a \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part $(a)$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    a = 36\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA742","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA742\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe area, $A$, of a given square can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(P) &= \\dfrac{P^2}{16}, \\hspace{0.5em} P \\geq 0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere\n$P$ is the perimeter of the square.\n\nThe graph of the function $A$, for $0 \\leq P \\leq 20$, is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/8c2c1a298c1b79e92e64f94fe2b4a4d452e488ad.svg\" alt=\"8c2c1a298c1b79e92e64f94fe2b4a4d452e488ad.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $A(20)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  On the grid above, draw the graph of the inverse function, $A^{-1}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  In the context of the question, explain the meaning of\n    $A^{-1}(4) = 8$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $A(P)$ for $P = 20$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A(P) &= \\dfrac{20^2}{16} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of the inverse function, $A^{-1}$, on the grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/508588bd8662a40575516587374d1e3379be6249.svg\" alt=\"508588bd8662a40575516587374d1e3379be6249.svg\">\n\n3.\n    $\\text{For a given square, when the area is \\(4\\), the perimeter is \\(8\\)}$"},{"ref":"AA743","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA743\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe circumference, $C$, of a given circle can be represented by the\nfunction\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(A) &= 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{A}{\\pi}}, \\hspace{0.5em} A \\geq 0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is the area of the circle.\n\nThe graph of the function $C$, for $0 \\leq A \\leq 24$, is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/3388e210bb4d6f02426816cb2651396f2822c4c1.svg\" alt=\"3388e210bb4d6f02426816cb2651396f2822c4c1.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $C(24)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  On the grid above, draw the graph of the inverse function, $C^{-1}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  In the context of the question, explain the meaning of\n    $C^{-1}(2\\pi) = \\pi$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $C(A)$ for $A = 24$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    C(A) &= 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{24}{\\pi}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 17.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of the inverse function, $C^{-1}$, on the grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/fae26733051cc1a7c15c3478abb0d0697e69d7fa.svg\" alt=\"fae26733051cc1a7c15c3478abb0d0697e69d7fa.svg\">\n\n3.\n    $\\text{For a given circle, when the circumference is \\(2\\pi\\), the area is \\(\\pi\\)}$"},{"ref":"AA746","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA746\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA town is planning to construct a jogging path in a grass field\n$170$ m long and $70$ m\nwide. The path is to be the shape of a rectangle with two semicircles of\nradius $x$, as shown in the diagram. The sides of the rectangle\nconnecting the circles are to be $100$ m long.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/22448faee8b9c6574b3fdefa8505f67030a94583.svg\" alt=\"22448faee8b9c6574b3fdefa8505f67030a94583.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down a function, $P$, (in metres) for the perimeter of the\n    jogging path, in terms of the radius, $x$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Determine the domain and range of $P$, taking into consideration the\n    dimensions of the grass field.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find an equation for the inverse function $P^{-1}(x)$. Express your\n    answer in the form $P^{-1}(x)=mx+c$.[2 marks]\n\nThe designers of the path are deciding whether the total length of the\npath should be $300$ m,\n$400$ m, or $500$ m. The\ndesigners want to maximise the perimeter of the path, but fit the path\nin the grass field.\n\n4.  Determine which length is most suitable, given the\n    dimensions of the grass field.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The perimeter for the path is the sum of the two straight sides\n    connecting the circles and the perimeter of a complete circle of\n    radius $x$. We get\n    $$\n    P(x) = 200+2\\pi x\n    $$\n\n2.  The length of the path will be $100+2x$ and its width $2x$.\n    Therefore we require $100+2x\\leq 170$ and $2x\\leq 70$. Hence\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Domain: } 0 < x \\leq 35.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    For\n    the range, the function $P(x)$ is increasing and $P(0)=200$ and\\\n    $P(35)=200+70\\pi$. We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Range: } 200\\leq P(x) \\leq 200+70\\pi.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let's denote $P(x)$ by $y$. To find the inverse we can interchange\n    the variables $x$ and $y$ and rearrange for $y$, as follows\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 200+2\\pi x\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    x &= 200+2\\pi y\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    y &= \\dfrac{x-200}{2\\pi}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-3.2em}P^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}x - \\dfrac{100}{\\pi}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5.5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  For each of the proposed perimeters $P$, we can find the radius\n    value $x$ as $x=P^{-1}(P)$, then the length and width of the running\n    track can be calculated, as shown in the following table.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/8985560368df68be350c8eca5afbe5b19a733963.svg\" alt=\"8985560368df68be350c8eca5afbe5b19a733963.svg\">\n\n    From the table, we can see that the largest perimeter of the options\n    that still fit inside the grass field dimensions is\n\n    $$\n    P=400\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}.\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA747","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA747\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nHarry is planning on constructing a glass window in one of the outer\nwalls of his house. The dimensions of the wall space available are 2m x\n2m. Harry wants the window to be in the shape shown in the diagram\nbelow. The bottom section is a rectangle and the top part is a\nsemicircle of radius $x$ m. Harry wants the\nheight of the rectangle to be fixed at 1 m.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/4b81f49038bd3a70fed3ed55e752da67f426bdf2.svg\" alt=\"4b81f49038bd3a70fed3ed55e752da67f426bdf2.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down a function $P$ (in metres) for the perimeter of the\n    window in terms of the radius, $x$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Determine the domain and range of $P$, taking into consideration the\n    dimensions of the available wall.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find an equation for the inverse function $P^{-1}(x)$.[2 marks]\n\nHarry wants to maximise the size of the window, however the window frame\nthat covers the perimeter of the window can only be 5, 6, or 7 metres\nlong, due to manufacturing restrictions.\n\n4.  Determine which perimeter length is the best option for\n    Harry.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The perimeter for the window is the sum of the three sides of the\n    rectangle and the semicircle, thus we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    P(x) &= \\text{Rectangle width}+2\\times\\text{Rectangle height}+\\text{Semicircle} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &= 2x+2(1)+\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}\\pi x}{\\cancel{2}} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-2.15em}P(x) = 2+(2+\\pi)x\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The width of the window will be $2x$ and the height $1+x$.\n    Therefore, we require $2x\\leq 2$ and $1+x\\leq 2$.\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Domain: } 0\\leq x \\leq 1.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now\n    for the range, the function $P(x)$ is increasing and $P(0)=2$ and\n    $P(1)=4+\\pi$. Therefore,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Range: } 2\\leq P(x) \\leq 4+\\pi.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let's denote $P(x)$ by $y$. To find the inverse we can interchange\n    the variables $x$ and $y$ and rearrange for $y$, as follows\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 2+(2+\\pi)x\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    x &= 2+(2+\\pi)y\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    y &= \\dfrac{x-2}{(2+\\pi)}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-3.2em}P^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{(2+\\pi)}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  For each of the proposed perimeters $P$, we can find the radius\n    value $x$ as $x=P^{-1}(P)$, then the length and width of the window\n    can be calculated, as shown in the following table\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/95900dceeb57876cf3bec3ce67dc138811471933.svg\" alt=\"95900dceeb57876cf3bec3ce67dc138811471933.svg\">\n\n    From the table, we can see that the perimeter that maximizes the\n    dimension of the window that fits in the wall is\n\n    $$\n    P=7\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}.\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Exponential and Logarithmic Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA044","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA044\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIt has been suggested that the number of visits (the number of hits) to\na newly launched website can be modelled by an equation of the form\n$H = aN^b$, where $H$ is the number of hits in each month,\n$N$ is the month number, $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nTo test this model, a new website was created and the number of hits in\neach of the first $10$ months were recorded.\n\nThe results are shown in the following table for $N = 1$ and $N = 2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/bc28e82540342d957840e8bc6479dc4995148f25.svg\" alt=\"bc28e82540342d957840e8bc6479dc4995148f25.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $a$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $b$, giving your answer to five significant\n        figures. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Use this model to estimate the number of hits in the\n    $8$th month. Give\\\n    your answer to the nearest integer. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Substituting $H = 32$ and $N = 1$ in the equation $H = aN^b$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        32 &= a(1)^b \\hspace{2.45em}  \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = 32 \\hspace{3.2em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Substituting $H = 100$ and $N = 2$ in the equation $H = 32N^b$\n        and solving the resulting equation for $b$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        100 &= 32(2)^b \\\\[6pt]\n        b &= \\log_2\\left(\\dfrac{100}{32}\\right) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        b \\approx 1.6439 \\hspace{1.3em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $H = 32\\hspace{0.05em}Y^{1.6439}$ for $N = 8$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.25em} H &= 32(8)^{1.6439} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 977\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1042","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1042\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe brightness of a star can be measured in terms of its apparent magnitude as seen from Earth. The apparent magnitude $M$ of a star is given by the formula\n$$\nM = -2.5 \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F}{F_0}\\right)\n$$\nwhere $F$ is the observed flux density and $F_0$ is a reference flux density.\n\n1. Solve for $F$ in terms of $M$.[2 marks]\n\n2. Suppose that $M(F_1)=1$ and $M(F_6)=6$. Find the value of $\\dfrac{F_1}{F_6}$.[1 mark]\n\nNow, let's look at the difference in magnitude of two stars. Suppose that star $1$ has observed flux density of $F_1$ with magnitude $M_1$ while star $2$ has flux density $F_2$ with magnitude $M_2$.\n\n3. Find an expression for the difference in magnitude $M_2-M_1$.[2 marks]\n\nThe apparent magnitude of the sun is $M_s=-26.8$ and the apparent magnitude of the full moon is $M_m=-12.7$.\n\n4. Their brightness factor is $\\dfrac{F_s}{F_m}=10^{\\tfrac{a}{b}}$ where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Q^+}$. Find $a$ and $b$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. By applying logarithm and exponential properties, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    M &= -2.5\\big[\\log(F)-\\log(F_0)\\big] \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[10pt]\n    M &= -2.5\\log(F)+2.5\\log(F_0) \\\\[11pt]\n    2.5\\log(F) &= 2.5\\log(F_0) - M \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log(F) &= \\log(F_0)- \\dfrac{M}{2.5} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.2em}F = F_0 \\cdot 10^{-\\frac{M\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Using the previous answer, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{F_1}{F_6} &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{F_0} \\cdot 10^{-\\frac{1\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}}{\\cancel{F_0}\\cdot  10^{-\\frac{6\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10^{-\\frac{1\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}}{10^{-\\frac{6\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 10^{\\frac{6\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}-\\frac{1\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}\\\\[10pt]\n    &= 10^{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{F_1}{F_6} &=100 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Using logarithm properties again, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    M_2-M_1 &= -2.5\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\right) - \\left[-2.5\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -2.5\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -2.5\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\div\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -2.5\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\div\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    M_2-M_1 &=-2.5 \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_1}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. From part (c), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2.5 \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_s}{F_m}\\right) &= M_s-M_m \\\\[10pt]\n    \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_s}{F_m}\\right) &= \\dfrac{-26.8-(-12.7)}{-2.5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{F_s}{F_m} &=10^{\\tfrac{-14.1}{-2.5}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2.8em}a=14.1 \\text{ and }b=2.5 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1055","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1055\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe temperature of a chocolate is given by the equation\n$$\n%60 \\hspace{0.1em} e^{-0.1t} + 20\nT(t) = A \\left( 2^{-kt} \\right) + B\n$$\nwhere $A$ and $B$ and $k$ are constants, $k>0$, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t$ is the number of minutes since the chocolate was served.\n\nIt is known that the initial temperature is $84^\\circ$ and the temperature at time $t=\\dfrac{1}{k}$ is $52^\\circ$.\n\n1. Show that $A=64$ and $B=20$. [2 marks]\n\nIt took $40$ minutes to get a temperature of $23^\\circ$.\n\n2. Find the value of $k$.[2 marks]\n\n3. Find the number of minutes it takes to get a temperature of $21^\\circ$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Since the initial temperature is $84^\\circ$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    T(0) &= A \\left(2^{-k(0)}\\right) +  B \\\\[7pt]\n    84 &= A + B \\hspace{2em}\\textcolor{red}{[1]} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $T$ at $t=\\dfrac{1}{k}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    T\\left(\\dfrac{1}{k}\\right) &= A \\left(2^{-k/k}\\right) +  B \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[7pt]\n    52 &= \\dfrac{A}{2} + B \\hspace{2em}\\textcolor{red}{[2]} \\\\[-10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find $A$ take the difference $\\textcolor{red}{[2]}-\\textcolor{red}{[1]}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-0.75em} A = 64  \\\\[-10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, using $\\textcolor{red}{[1]}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em} B = 20\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Solving for $k$ in the equation $T(40)=23$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    64 \\left(2^{-k(40)}\\right) + 20 &= 23 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n    &=0.11037...\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{ k = 0.110} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Solving for $t$, $T(t)=21$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    64 \\left( 2^{-0.110t} \\right) + 20 &= 21 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[9pt]\n    &=54.545...\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{ t = 54.5\\,\\text{minutes}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1169","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1169\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=6x^2-7x+2$.\n\n1.  Fully factorise $f(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n\\\nAnother function is defined as $g(x)=\\ln x$.\n\nA third function is defined as $h(x)=(g \\circ f)(x)$.\n\n2.  Hence, determine the domain of $h(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n\\\nThe value $a$ is added to $f(x)$, where $a \\in \\mathrm{R}$.\n\nFor the domain of $h(x)$ to be $x \\in \\mathrm{R}$ then $a>k$.\n\nThe minimum value of $k$ is $a_{\\text{min}}$.\n\n3.   Find $a_{\\text{min}}$ [3 marks]\n\n\\\nNow the value $b$ is added to $f(x)$, where $b \\in \\mathrm{R}$ and $b > a_{\\text{min}}$.\n\n4.  Find the value of $b$ such that the vertex of $h(x)$ lies on the $x-$axis. Give your answer to $4$ significant figures. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We will use the grouping method to factorise.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a \\times c &= 2 \\times 6\\\\[6pt]\n         &=12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we need to find two numbers that sum to $-7$ and multiply to give $12$. These are $-3$ and $-4$.\n\n    Therefore, we can then rewrite $-7x$ as $-3x-4x$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        6x^2-7x+2&=6x^2-3x-4x+2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We then factorise the resulting expression into two groups\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=3x(2x-1)-2(2x-1)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=(2x-1)(3x-2) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  This is a composite function in which we form $h(x)$ by substituting $f(x)$ into $g(x)$. Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        h(x)&=\\ln{\\left(6x^2-7x+2\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now sketch $h(x)$ on our G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_a%20%282%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_a (2)\">\n\n    $h(x)$ has two vertical asymptotes and these occur when $h(x)=\\ln{0}$. Hence $f(x)=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the answer from part (a) we can find their equations.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (2x-1)(3x-2)&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=\\tfrac{1}{2}, \\tfrac{2}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The red arrows on the image above show the domain of $h(x)$.\n\n    Therefore the $\\text{domain of }h(x)\\text{ is }x<\\tfrac{1}{2}\\text{ and }x>\\tfrac{2}{3}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The following image shows $f(x)$ and $h(x)$ together on the same axes.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_c%20%283%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_c (3)\">\n\n    Notice that $h(x)$ is undefined when $f(x)<0$, this is the dark blue part of $f(x)$.\n\n    Hence we need to shift $f(x)$ vertically up $a$ units such that the vertex of $f(x)$ touches the $x-$axis at a single point.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To do this we must find the minimum value of $f(x)$.\n\n    Using a G.D.C. we can find the minimum point of $f(x)$ to be $(0.5833...,-0.04166...)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $f(x)+0.04166...$ will ensure $f(x)$ intersects the $x-$axis at one point. Ensuring that $f(x)\\geq 0$ for all $x$. This is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_d.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_d\">\n\n    Therefore if $a>0.04166...$ then $f(x)>0$ for all $x$ and the domain of $h(x)$ is $x \\in \\mathrm{R}$.\n\n    Hence, $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{a_{\\text{min}}=0.0417}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Notice from the image in the previous part, that the vertex of $h(x)$ lies on the same vertical line as $f(x)$.\n\n    Therefore the equation of the vertical line is $x=0.5833...$ and the final position of the vertex of $h(x)$ must be on the $x-$axis. This is shown below.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_d2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_d2\">\n\n    Therefore we need to solve this equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        h(0.5833...)&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        (g \\circ (f(0.5833...)+b))&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        \\ln{(f(0.5833...)+b)}&=0 \\hspace{2em} (\\ln{1}=0) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        f(0.5833...)+b&=1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    From part (c) we know that $f(0.5833...)=-0.04166...$ at the vertex of $f(x)$ therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        b&=1-(-0.04166...)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1.04166...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{b=1.042}\\hspace{0.525em}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1180","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1180\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x)=3e^{\\frac{x}{2}}$ where $x \\in \\mathrm{R}$ and is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1180_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1180_q\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation of the asymptote of $f(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Write down the $y-$intercept of $f(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Hence, or otherwise, sketch the inverse of $f(x)$ on the image above. Make sure any asymptotes and/or intercepts are clearly marked. [2 marks]\n\n4. Determine the domain of the inverse of $f(x)$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  $y=0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Occurs when $x=0$, $f(0)=3\\times e^0$ therefore $3$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The inverse is a reflection in the line $y=x$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1180_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1180_a\">\n\n    __A1__ Correct shape/asymptotes\\\n    __A1__ Correct intercept\n\n4.  $\\text{The domain of the inverse is }x>0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1284","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1284\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe amount, in milligrams, of a medicinal drug in the body $t$ hours after it is injected is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    D(t)=240e^{-kt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $k>0$ and $t\\geq 0$. Before the injection, it is assumed the amount of drug in the body is zero.\n\nA patient is to be injected with the drug and, for this patient, it is known that it takes $5$ hours for the amount of drug remaining in the body to have decreased by $65\\%$ of the initial dose.\n\nThe patient is regularly checked and is allowed to go home when the amount of drug remaining in the body is $10\\%$, or less, of the initial dose.\n\nThe initial dose is given to the patient at $9:00\\,$am.\n\nUse this model to estimate, to the nearest hour, the earliest time the patient will be allowed to go home.","markScheme":"This model has a fixed value of $k$. In order to use the model we must find $k$.\n\nFirst, we can find the initial amount of the drug injected into the patient, this occurs when $t=0$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(0)&=240e^{-k(0)}\\\\[6pt]\nD(0)&=240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFive hours later, $t=5$, we are told that $100\\%-65\\%=35\\%$ of the initial $240\\text{ mg}$ remains.\n\nWe can use this to find $k$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.35\\times240&=240e^{-5k}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n0.35&=e^{-5k}\\\\[6pt]\n\\ln{0.35}&=\\ln e^{-5k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the log law $\\log_ax^m=m\\log_ax$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln{0.35}&=-5k\\ln e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\ln e=1$, we can simplify the equation and solve for $k$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln{0.35}&=-5k\\\\[6pt]\nk&=\\frac{\\ln{0.35}}{-5}\\\\[6pt]\n&=0.2099\\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write out the model with out value of $k$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    D(t)&=240e^{-0.2099...t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $t$ when the drug remaining in the body is equal to $0.10\\times240=24\\text{ mg}$.\n\nHence we need to solve the following equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24&=240e^{-0.2099\\dots t}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can do this graphically by sketching the function $y=D(t)$ and the horizontal line $y=24$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1284.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1284\">\n\nThe $x-$coordinate of the intersection point is the solution.\n\nAlthough a calculator method is preferred, an algebraic approach is also included in case you attempted it that way\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.1&=e^{-0.2099\\dots t}\\\\[6pt]\n\\ln{0.1}&=-0.2099\\dots t\\\\[6pt]\nt&=\\frac{\\ln{0.1}}{-0.2099\\dots}\\\\[6pt]\nt&=10.96\\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\nt&=11 \\hspace{1em}\\text{to the nearest hour}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the answer to a time we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{9:00 + 11 hours = 20:00}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the earliest time, to the nearest hour, the patient will be allowed to go home is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{20:00 or 8:00\\,pm}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA151","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA151\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Write down the domain and range of the logarithmic function\n    $y = \\log_a x$\\\n    where $a > 0$ and $a \\neq 1$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\log_{x^2} y = 9 \\log_y (x^2)$, find all the possible\n    expressions\\\n    of $y$ as a function of $x$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain}:\\hspace{0.75em}&\\hspace{0.525em}(0,\\infty) \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{range}:\\hspace{0.75em}&\\hspace{0.525em}\\mathbb{R}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{x^2} y &= 9 \\log_y (x^2) \\\\[11pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{\\ln y}{\\ln (x^2)} &= \\dfrac{9\\ln (x^2)}{\\ln y} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\ln y}{2\\ln x} &= \\dfrac{18\\ln x}{\\ln y} \\\\[12pt]\n    (\\ln y)^2 &= (6\\ln x)^2 \\\\[16pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\pm 6\\ln x \\\\[16pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln (1/x^6)\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.25em}\\ln (x^6) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.2em} y = \\dfrac{1}{x^6}\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.25em}x^6\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA178","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA178\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^x - 2$ and $g(x) = 3x + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$k$ is a constant.\n\n1.  Find $(g\\circ f)(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that\n    $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} (g\\circ f)(x) = -4$,\n    find the value of $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= g(f(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(e^x - 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(e^x - 2) + k \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3e^x - 6 + k\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} (g\\circ f)(x) &= -4 \\hspace{6.9em} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\big[3e^x - 6 + k\\big] &= -4 \\\\[4pt]\n    3(0) - 6 + k &= -4 \\\\[9pt]\n    - 6 + k &= -4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.2em} k = 2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA182","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA182\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = 0.2e^{x+2} - 4$, for $-3 \\leq x \\leq 2$.\n\n1.  On the following grid, sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/219ede634589aee775d4d590dd79b30ff5c63914.svg\" alt=\"219ede634589aee775d4d590dd79b30ff5c63914.svg\">\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ is reflected in the $x$-axis, then translated 1 unit in the direction of the positive $x$-axis and 2 units in the direction of the positive $y$-axis to obtain the graph of a function $g$.\n\n3.  Find $g(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y = f(x)$ for $-3 \\leq x \\leq 2$ \\[by using\n    G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/a0ce7feb5a9182c2d088b3711cfba75a324b8033.svg\" alt=\"a0ce7feb5a9182c2d088b3711cfba75a324b8033.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.2em} 0.2e^{x+2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &\\approx 0.996 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are\n         $(0.996,0)$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.2e^{0+2} - 4 \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[7pt]\n        &\\approx -2.52\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the\n        $y$-intercept are\n         $(0,-2.52)$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} g(x) &= -f(x-1) + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\big[0.2e^{(x-1)+2} - 4\\big] + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -0.2e^{x+1} + 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA202","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA202\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA population of endangered snow leopards, $P$, can be modelled by the\nequation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_t = P_0e^{kt},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial population,\nand $t$ is measured in years.\n\n1.  After one year, it is estimated that $\\dfrac{P_1}{P_0} = 0.93$.\n    1.  Find the value of $k$.\n\n    2.  Interpret the meaning of the value of $k$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the least number of **whole** years for which\n    $\\dfrac{P_t}{P_0} < 0.50$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Substituting $t = 1$ in the equation $P_t = P_0e^{kt}$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        P_1 &= P_0e^{k(1)} \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{P_1}{P_0} &= e^k \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{0.1em} 0.93 &= e^k \\\\[11pt]\n        k &= \\ln 0.93 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{0.8em} k \\approx -0.0726\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $\\text{The rate at which the population of leopards exponentially decrease}$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{P_t}{P_0} &< 0.50 \\\\[8pt]\n    e^{-0.0726t} &< 0.50 \\\\[12pt]\n    -0.0726t &< \\ln 0.50 \\\\[6pt]\n    t &> -\\dfrac{\\ln 0.50}{0.0726} \\\\[8pt]\n    t &> 9.55 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the least number of whole years\n    for which $\\dfrac{P_t}{P_0} < 0.50$ is\n     $t = 10$"},{"ref":"AA734","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA734\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAtmospheric pressure, $P$, in kPa, decreases exponentially with\nincreasing height above sea level, $h$. The atmospheric pressure can be\nmodelled by the function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(h) &= 101\\times\\bigg(\\dfrac{25}{22}\\bigg)^{-h},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere\n$h$ is the height above sea level in kilometres.\n\n1.  Write down the exact atmospheric pressure at sea level, in kPa.\n    [1 mark]\n\nMount Kosciuszko is the highest mountain in Australia with a height of\n$2228$ $\\text{metres}$ above sea level at the top.\n\n2.  Calculate the atmospheric pressure at the top of the Mount\n    Kosciuszko. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the height where the atmospheric pressure is\n    equal to $10$ kPa. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $101 \\text{ kPa}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $P(h)$ for $h = 2.228$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} P(2.228) &= 101\\times\\bigg(\\dfrac{25}{22}\\bigg)^{-2.228} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{\\approx 76.0 \\text{ kPa}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Writing the equation $P(h) = 10$ for $h$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    101\\times\\bigg(\\dfrac{25}{22}\\bigg)^{-h} &= 10 \\hspace{8.1em} \\\\\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solve this equation [by using G.D.C.]\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11em} h = 18.1\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km} \\hspace{3em} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA735","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA735\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe number of French words, $N$, that are remembered by students after\nthe completion of a French language course decreases exponentially over\ntime. This data can be modelled by the function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) = a\\times b^{-t} + 450,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are\npositive constants, and $t$ is the time in years since a student\ncompleted the French language course.\n\nImmediately after completion, a student remembers $4200$ French words.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$. [2 marks]\n\nAfter $4$ years a student remembers only $1600$ French words.\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\nThe number of French words a student remembers never decreases below a\ncertain number of words, $n$.\n\n3.  Write down the value of $n$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the equation $N(0) = 4200$ for $a > 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a\\times b^{-0} + 450 &= 4200 \\hspace{5.4em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n    a + 450 &= 4200 \\\\[-16pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    a = 3750 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $N(4) = 1600$ for $b > 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3750\\times b^{-4} + 450 &= 1600 \\hspace{6.9em} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solve this equation [by using G.D.C.]\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{10.6em} \\boxed{b \\approx 1.34} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we draw the graph of $y = N(t)$, for $t \\geq 0$ \\[by using\n    G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/5f0266262d56defec1ffa52f191a9228b568fd3e.svg\" alt=\"5f0266262d56defec1ffa52f191a9228b568fd3e.svg\">\n\n    Hence the value of $n$ is\n    $450$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA736","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA736\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe number of users on a new social network on January 1st $2018$ was $320\\hspace{0.15em}000$. One year later, on January 1st $2019$, the number of users on this network is estimated to be $350\\hspace{0.15em}000$. The number\nof the users on this network, $N$, can be modelled by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) = 320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times b^t, \\hspace{0.5em} t\\geq0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since January 1st $2018$ and $b$ is a constant.\n\n1.  Find the **exact** value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Estimate the number of the users on this network there will be in\n    $2023$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine the year during which the number of the social network users\n    reaches one million. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $N(1) = 350\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $b$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times b &= 350\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\hspace{4.4em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[9pt]\n    b &= \\dfrac{350\\hspace{0.15em}000}{320\\hspace{0.15em}000} \\\\[-9pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{2.7em}\\boxed{b = \\dfrac{35}{32} \\text{ or } 1.09375} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we evaluate $N(t)$ for $t = 5$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} N(5) &= 320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times \\left(\\dfrac{35}{32}\\right)^5 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[4pt]\n    &\\approx 500\\hspace{0.15em}887.6... \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{\\approx 501\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\text{ users}}\\hspace{1em}\\text{(3 s.f.)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Solving the equation $N(t) = 1\\hspace{0.15em}000\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.75em} 320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times \\left(\\dfrac{35}{32}\\right)^t &= 1\\hspace{0.15em}000\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    t &\\approx 12.71... \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we determine the years corresponding to $t = 12$ and $t=13$, we find that the number of network users reaches one million $\\text{during } 2030$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA739","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA739\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA population of goldfish decreases exponentially. The size of the\npopulation, $P$, after $t$ days is modelled by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(t) = 8000\\times2^{-t} + 100, \\hspace{0.5em} t \\geq 0. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Write down the **exact** size of the initial population. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the size of the population after $5$ days. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the time it will take for the size of the population to decrease to $120$.[2 marks]\n\nThe population will stabilize when it reaches a size of $n$.\n\n4.  Write down the value of $n$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $P(t)$ for $t = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.7em} P(0) &= 8000\\times2^0 + 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8000 + 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}= 8100 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $P(t)$ for $t = 5$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.3em} P(5) &= 8000\\times2^{-5} + 100 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}= 350 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $P(t) = 120$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    8000\\times2^{-t} + 100 &= 120 \\hspace{6.1em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{14.2em} t = 8.64 \\text{ days} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.9em} \\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty} P(t) &= \\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty} \\big[8000\\times2^{-t} + 100\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0+100 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $2^{-t} \\rightarrow 0$ as $t \\rightarrow \\infty$}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 100 \\text{ fish}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the value of $n$ is $\\hspace{0.525em}100$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA740","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA740\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA population of $50$ hamsters was introduced to a new town. One month\nlater, the number of hamsters was $62$. The number of hamsters, $P$, can\nbe modelled by the function\n\n$$\nP(t) = 50\\times b^t,\\hspace{0.5em} t\\geq0, \\\\[3pt]\n$$\nwhere $t$ is the\ntime, in months, since the hamsters were introduced to the town.\n\n1.  Find the value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the number of hamsters in the town after $6$ months.\n    [2 marks]\n\nA wildlife specialist estimates that the town has enough water and food\nto support a maximum population of $2000$ hamsters.\n\n3.  Calculate the number of months it takes for the hamster\n    population to reach this maximum. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the equation $P(1) = 62$ for $b$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    50\\times b^1 &= 62 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    b &= \\dfrac{62}{50} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.4em}b = 1.24\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $P(t)$ for $t = 6$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} P(6) &= 50\\times(1.24)^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 182\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $P(t) = 2000$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    50\\times(1.24)^t &= 2000 \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{15.6em} t \\approx 17.1 \\text{ months} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA987","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA987\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA species of bird, thought to be extinct, was rediscovered on 14 March 1973. Through a careful protection and breeding program, the population has continued to increase since then.\n\nThe Department of conservation proposes that the population of birds $t$ years after 14 March 1973 can be modelled by the function $P(t)=10e^{kt}$, where $0 < k < 1$ and $t \\geq 0$.\n\nOn 14 March 1993 the known population of birds was 50.\n\n1. Use the model to estimate the population of birds on 14 March 2023.[4 marks]\n\nIn fact, the known population of birds on 14 March 2023 was 502. A new model is proposed in which\n\n* The population on March 14 1973 was 10.\n\n* The population on March 14 2023 was 502.\n\n* The population increased at a constant $r\\%$ per year.\n\n2. Find the value of $r$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 14 March 1993 is 20 years after 14 March 1973, therefore $P(20)=50$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(20) &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    50 &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^{20k} &= 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $k\\approx 0.08047$. Hence our model is $P(t)=10e^{0.08047t}$.\n\n    14 March 2023 is 50 years after 14 March 1973, hence we calculate $P(50)$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(50) &= 10e^{0.08047 \\times 50} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-2.4em}\\boxed{P(50)\\approx559}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. If something increases at a constant rate, the compound interest formula applies. Using the formula for compound interest with $PV=10$, $FV=502$, $k=1$ and $n=50$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    FV &= PV \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\right)^{kn} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    502 &= 10 \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100}\\right)^{50}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $\\boxed{r\\approx 8.15}$"}]},{"name":"Linear and Quadratic Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1268","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1268\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nConsider the line $L_1$ which passes through the points $A (-4,-4)$ and $B(8,-1)$.\n\n1. Find the gradient of the line $L_1$. [1 mark]\n\nLine $L_2$ is perpendicular to $L_1$ and passes through the point $(-a,2a)$ where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2. Given that $L_2$ intersects the $y$-axis at $y=2a-3$, find the value of $a$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula for the gradient of a line\n\n    $$\n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m&=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    we can find the gradient of $L_1$\n\n    $$\n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m_1&=\\frac{-1-(-4)}{8-(-4)}\\\\[9pt]\n\n       &=\\frac{3}{12}\\\\[9pt]\n\n       &=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  As $L_2$ is perpendicular to $L_1$ we can find the gradient of $L_2$ using the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m_1\\times m_2&=-1\\\\[9pt]\n\n        \\frac{1}{4}\\times m_2&=-1\\\\[9pt]\n\n        m_2&=-4\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can form an equation, using the gradient formula, and solve for $a$.\n\n    Substituting in $m_2$ and the points $(-a,2a)$ and $(0,2a-3)$ we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    m_2&=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    -4&=\\frac{(2a-3)-2a}{0-(-a)}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[9pt]\n\n      -4 &=\\frac{-3}{a}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    -4a&=-3\\\\[6pt] \n\n    a&=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1285","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1285\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the equations of the two tangents to the curve $y=2x^2-x+\\tfrac{9}{2}$ that pass through the origin.","markScheme":"To better understand the scenario we can make an approximate sketch.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/AA1285.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1285\">\n\nThe dashed lines are the two tangent lines whose equations we need to find.\n\nThe equation of a tangent line, through the origin, will be of the form\n\n$$ \n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y&=mx\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTo find the intersection points of the curve and line we can solve both equations simultaneously, hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    mx&=2x^2-x+\\frac{9}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0&=2x^2-x-mx+\\frac{9}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0&=2x^2-(1+m)x+\\frac{9}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nIf the lines are tangent to the curve then there is only one point of intersection i.e. only $1$ solution.\n\nAs we have a quadratic equation then we can say that the discriminant must be equal to zero. Hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\Delta&=b^2-4ac\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0&=[-(1+m)]^2-4(2)\\left(\\tfrac{9}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSolving for $m$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    36&=[-(1+m)]^2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    -(1+m)&=\\pm6\\\\[6pt]\n\n    m&=-7 \\text{ and }5\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore the two equations are \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y=-7x}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y=5x}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA134","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA134\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Express $2x^2 - 8x + 9$ in the form $a(x+b)^2 + c$ where\n    $a,b,c \\in\\mathbb{Z}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $f(x) = x - 2$ and $(g\\circ f)(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + 9$, find\n    $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the method of completing the square, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x^2 - 8x + 9 &= 2\\big[x^2 - 4x\\big] + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[(x-2)^2 - 4\\big] + 9 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x-2)^2 - 8 + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x-2)^2 + 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.3em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= 2x^2 - 8x + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    g(f(x)) &=  2(x-2)^2 + 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    g(x-2) &= 2(x-2)^2 + 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(x) = x - 2$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g(x) = 2x^2 + 1 \\hspace{3.4em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA145","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA145\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe equation $x^2 - kx + 1 = 0$ has two distinct real roots.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Since the quadratic function $q(x) = x^2 - kx + 1$ has two distinct real\nroots, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(-k)^2 - 4(1)(1) &> 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 - 4 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 &> 4 \\\\[7pt]\n|k| &> 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} k < \\hspace{-0.1em}-2 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k > 2  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $k$ is\n $(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$"},{"ref":"AA146","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA146\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe equation $x^2 + (k-3)x - 3k = 0$ has two distinct real solutions.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Since the quadratic function $q(x) = x^2 + (k-3)x - 3k$ has two distinct\nreal roots, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k-3)^2 - 4(1)(-3k) &> 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n(k^2 - 6k + 9) + 12k &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 + 6k + 9 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k + 3)^2 &> 0 \\\\[7pt]\n|k + 3| &> 0 \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.7em} k + 3 < 0 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k + 3 > 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk < \\hspace{-0.1em} -3 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k > -3   \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $k$ is\n $k\\in\\mathbb{R}\\,;\\,k\\neq-3$"},{"ref":"AA147","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA147\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 + kx$ and $g(x) = x + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$k$ is a constant.\n\nThe graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$ intersect at two distinct\npoints.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = g(x)$ in the form\n$ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2 + kx &= x + k \\\\[6pt]\nx^2 + (k-1)x - k &= 0 \\hspace{3.1em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the quadratic\nfunction $q(x) = x^2 + (k-1)x - k$ has two distinct real roots.\n\nTherefore we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k-1)^2 - 4(1)(-k) &> 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n(k^2 - 2k + 1) + 4k &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2  + 2k + 1 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k + 1)^2 &> 0 \\\\[7pt]\n|k+1| &> 0 \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.7em} k + 1 < 0 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k + 1 > 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk < \\hspace{-0.1em} -1 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k > -1   \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $k$ is\n $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{-1\\}$"},{"ref":"AA148","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA148\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = p - \\dfrac{16}{x}$, for $x \\neq 0$, where $p$ is a constant.\n\nThe line $y = x - p$ intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at two distinct\npoints.\n\nFind the possible values of $p$.","markScheme":"If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = x - p$ in the form\n$ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np - \\dfrac{16}{x} &= x - p \\\\[6pt]\n2p - \\dfrac{16}{x} &= x \\\\[11pt]\n2px - 16 &= x^2 \\\\[14pt]\n\\hspace{0.2em} x^2 - 2px + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the quadratic\nfunction $q(x) = x^2 - 2px + 16$ has two distinct real roots.\n\nTherefore we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.8em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(-2p)^2 - 4(1)(16) &> 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n4p^2 - 64 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\np^2 - 16 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\np^2 &> 16 \\\\[7pt]\n|p| &> 4 \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} p < \\hspace{-0.1em}-4 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} p > 4  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $p$ is\n $(-\\infty,-4)\\cup(4,\\infty)$"},{"ref":"AA155","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA155\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Express $3x^2 + 18x + 20$ in the form $a(x+b)^2 + c$ where\n    $a,b,c \\in\\mathbb{Z}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $f(x) = x + 3$ and $(g\\circ f)(x) = 3x^2 + 18x + 20$,\n    find $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the method of completing the square, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3x^2 + 18x + 20 &= 3\\big[x^2 + 6x\\big] + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\big[(x+3)^2 - 9\\big] + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(x+3)^2 - 27 + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(x+3)^2 - 7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.8em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= 3x^2 + 18x + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    g(f(x)) &= 3(x+3)^2 - 7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    g(x+3) &= 3(x+3)^2 - 7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(x) = x + 3$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g(x) = 3x^2 - 7 \\hspace{0.6em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA162","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA162\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 - 2x - 8$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Write down the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Solve the equation $f(x) = 0$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry of the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0^2 - 2(0) - 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{P}(0,-8)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 - 2x - 8 &= 0 \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    (x + 2)(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.8em} x = -2,\\hspace{0.25em} 4\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.7em} f(x) &= x^2 - 2x - 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[(x - 1)^2 - 1\\big] - 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x - 1)^2 - 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the axis of symmetry of the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$ is\n     $x = 1$"},{"ref":"AA163","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA163\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of a quadratic function\n$f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/1964f1685a23a24125bfe5568ac8db4fc2c5ff72.svg\" alt=\"1964f1685a23a24125bfe5568ac8db4fc2c5ff72.svg\">\n\nThe vertex is at point A and the $x$-intercepts are at points B and C.\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = (x - h)^2+k$.\n\n1.  Write down the values of $h$ and $k$. [2 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can also be written in the form\n$f(x) = (x + p)(x - q)$, where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$.\n\n2.  Write down the values of $p$ and $q$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the $y$-intercept of the graph of $f$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $h = 2$  and\n     $k = -9$\n\n2.\n    $p = 1$  and\n     $q = 5$\n\n3.  Evaluating $f(x) = (x+1)(x-5)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= (0+1)(0-5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1)(-5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{D}(0,-5)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $f$."},{"ref":"AA164","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA164\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 1)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nFor the graph of $f$, find:\n\n1.  the $y$-intercept; [2 marks]\n\n2.  the $x$-intercepts; [2 marks]\n\n3.  the coordinates of the vertex. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 1)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} f(0) &= (0 - 3)(0 + 1) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-3)(1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the $y$-intercept is\n     $\\mathrm{A}(0,-3)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (x - 3)(x + 1) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -1,\\hspace{0.25em} 3 \\hspace{1.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the\n    $x$-intercepts are\n     $\\mathrm{B}(-1,0)$\n    and\n     $\\mathrm{C}(3,0)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing the square,\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} f(x) &= (x - 3)(x + 1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= x^2 - 2x - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[(x-1)^2 - 1\\big] - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x-1)^2 - 4 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex\n    are  $(1,-4)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA166","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA166\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find:\n    1.  the $y$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $x$-intercepts. [3 marks]\n\n2.  The function $f$ can be expressed in the form $f(x) = (x-h)^2 + k$.\n    Find\\\n    the value of $h$ and the value of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ on the grid below. Clearly label\\\n    the intercepts with the axes, and the vertex. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/aefa19917a8714e460d7087874916779ea719f3e.svg\" alt=\"aefa19917a8714e460d7087874916779ea719f3e.svg\">","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.8em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0^2 - 4(0) + 3 \\\\[7pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $y$-intercept is\n         $\\mathrm{A}(0,3)$\n\n    2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2 - 4x + 3 &= 0 \\hspace{8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        (x-1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= 1,\\hspace{0.25em} 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $x$-intercepts\n        are\n         $\\mathrm{B}(1,0)$\n        and\n         $\\mathrm{C}(3,0)$\n\n2.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} f(x) &= x^2 - 4x + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[(x-2)^2 - 4\\big] + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x-2)^2 - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $h = 2$\n    and  $k = -1$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ on the grid below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/e4bab816ede5f709b1664609afdc53c6db3be4ab.svg\" alt=\"e4bab816ede5f709b1664609afdc53c6db3be4ab.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA167","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA167\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe equation $x^2 + kx + 4 = 0$ has two equal roots.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Since the quadratic function $q(x) = x^2 + kx + 4$ has two equal roots,\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 - 4(1)(4) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 - 16 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 &= 16 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.9em} k = \\pm 4\n$$"},{"ref":"AA168","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA168\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe vertex of the graph of $f$ is at P$(3,4)$ and the graph passes\nthrough Q$(1,-4)$.\n\n1.  Write down the values of $h$ and $k$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $h = 3$  and\n     $k = 4$\n\n2.  Using the coordinates of Q, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(1) &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} a(1-h)^2 + k &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n    a(1-3)^2 + 4 &= -4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    4a &= -8 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    a = -2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA177","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA177\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider $f(x) = x^2 + bx + c$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. The graph of $f$ has a local minimum when $x = 2.5$.\nThe distance between the two $x$-intercepts of the graph of $f$ is $7$.\n\n1.  Find the coordinates of the two $x$-intercepts. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$ and the value of $c$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The axis of symmetry of the graph of $f$ is $x = 2.5$.\n    Hence the coordinates of the two $x$-intercepts are\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bigg(2.5-\\frac{7}{2},0\\bigg) = \\hspace{0.525em}(-1,0)\\hspace{0.525em} \\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\n    \\bigg(2.5+\\frac{7}{2},0\\bigg) = \\hspace{0.525em}(6,0)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The roots of the quadratic function $f(x) = x^2 + bx + c$ are\n    $x = -1$ and $x = 6$.\n\n    Hence, using the factor theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} f(x) &= (x + 1)(x - 6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= x^2 - 5x - 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $b = -5$\n    and  $c = -6$"},{"ref":"AA179","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA179\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = ax^2 - 24x + c$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$a, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  A horizontal line, $L$, intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $x = 1$ and $x = 7$.\n\n    1.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $a = 3$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  The equation of $L$ is $y = 6$. Find the value of $c$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The equation of the axis of symmetry is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= \\dfrac{1 + 7}{2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        x= 4 \\hspace{2.2em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n        4 &= -\\dfrac{(-24)}{2a} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[12pt]\n        \\hspace{8.8em} 8a &= 24 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = 3 \\hspace{2.1em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Since the line $y = 6$ intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $x = 1$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(1) &= 6 \\hspace{6.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3(1)^2 - 24(1) + c &= 6 \\\\[7pt]\n    3 - 24 + c &= 6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.8em} c = 27\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA186","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA186\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 3x^2 + 12x + 9$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $f$, find:\n    1.  the $y$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $x$-intercepts. [4 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = a(x-h)^2+k$.\n\n2.  Find the values of $a$, $h$ and $k$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  For the graph of $f$, write down:\n    1.  the coordinates of the vertex;\n\n    2.  the equation of the axis of symmetry. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a translation of 4 units in the direction of the positive $y$ axis.\n\n4.  Find $g(x)$, giving your answer in the form\n    $g(x) = px^2 + qx + r$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Evaluating $f(x) = 3x^2 + 12x + 9$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5em} f(0) &= 3(0)^2 + 12(0) + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 9\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $y$-intercept is\n         $\\mathrm{A}(0,9)$\n\n    2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.5em} 3x^2 + 12x + 9 &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2 + 4x + 3 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n        (x+3)(x+1) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n        x &= -3,\\hspace{0.25em}-1 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the\n        $x$-intercepts are\n         $\\mathrm{B}(-3,0)$\n        and\n         $\\mathrm{C}(-1,0)$\n\n2.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} f(x) &= 3x^2 + 12x + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\big[x^2 + 4x\\big] + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\big[(x + 2)^2 - 4\\big] + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(x + 2)^2 - 3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = 3,\\hspace{0.25em} h = -2$\n    and  $k = -3$\n\n3.\n    1.\n        $(-2,-3)$\n\n    2.\n        $x = -2$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} g(x) &= -f(x)+4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -(3x^2 + 12x + 9)+4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -3x^2 - 12x - 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA187","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA187\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2x^2 + 4x + p$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$p \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  The equation $f(x) = 0$ has two equal roots.\n    1.  Write down the value of the discriminant of $f$.\n\n    2.  Show that $p = 2$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $f$, find:\n    1.  the equation of the axis of symmetry.\n\n    2.  the coordinates of the vertex; [4 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the solution to the equation $f(x) = 0$. [1 mark]\n\n4.  The function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k$.\n    Find\\\n    the values of $a$, $h$ and $k$. [5 marks]\n\n5.  The graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a\n    reflection\\\n    in the $x$-axis. Find the coordinates of the vertex of the graph of\n    $g$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $\\triangle  = 0$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\triangle  &= b^2 - 4ac \\\\[4pt]\n        0 &= 4^2 - 4(2)(p) \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[5pt]\n        0 &= 16 - 8p \\\\[5pt]\n        16 &= 8p \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        p = 2 \\hspace{2.9em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -\\dfrac{4}{2(2)} \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        x = -1 \\hspace{2.9em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f(x)=2x^2 + 4x + 2$ for $x = -1$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.2em} f(-1) &= 2(-1)^2 + 4(-1) + 2 \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the vertex are\n         $(-1,0)$\n\n3.\n    $x=-1$\n\n4.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} f(x) &= 2x^2 + 4x + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[x^2 + 2x\\big] + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[(x+1)^2 - 1\\big] + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x+1)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = 2,\\hspace{0.25em} h = -1$\n    and  $k = 0$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em}  g(x) &= -f(x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x+1)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex of\n    the graph of $g$ are\n     $(-1,0)$"},{"ref":"AA188","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA188\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2x^2-8x+6$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f(0)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Solve the equation $f(x) = 0$. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = a(x-h)^2+k$.\n\n3.  Find the values of $a$, $h$ and $k$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  For the graph of $f$, write down:\n    1.  the coordinates of the vertex;\n\n    2.  the equation of the axis of symmetry. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a translation of 1 unit to the right and 3 units up.\n\n5.  Find $g(x)$, giving your answer in the form\n    $g(x) = px^2+qx+r$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $f(0) = 6$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x^2-8x+6 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2-4x+3 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    (x-1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.8em} x = 1,\\hspace{0.25em}3\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing of square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} f(x) &= 2x^2-8x+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[x^2-4x\\big]+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[(x-2)^2-4\\big]+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x-2)^2-2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = 2,\\hspace{0.25em}h=2$\n    and  $k=-2$\n\n4.\n    1.\n        $(2,-2)$\n\n    2.\n        $x = 2$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} g(x) &= -f(x-1)+3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\big[2((x-1)-2)^2-2\\big]+3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x-3)^2+5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x^2-6x+9)+5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2x^2+12x-13\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA190","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA190\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = a(x+1)(x+5)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. The following diagram shows part of the graph of\n$f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/c560b93b90031104353dd8752d2c6976f60f1751.svg\" alt=\"c560b93b90031104353dd8752d2c6976f60f1751.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ has $x$-intercepts at $(p,0)$ and $(q,0)$, and a\n$y$-intercept at $(0,-10)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $p$ and the value of $q$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $a$. [6 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of the vertex. [3 marks]\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a\nreflection in the $y$-axis, followed by a translation of 2 units in the direction of the positive $y$-axis. The point P$(-2,6)$ on the graph of $f$ is mapped to point Q on the graph of\n$g$.\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of Q. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $p = -5$  and\n         $q = -1$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of the $y$-intercept, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(0) &= -10 \\hspace{5.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        a(0+1)(0+5) &= -10  \\\\[6pt]\n        5a &= -10  \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = -2\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} f(x) &= -2(x+1)(x+5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x^2+6x+5) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -2x^2-12x-10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the axis of symmetry\n    formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.5em} x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -\\dfrac{(-12)}{2(-2)} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.2em} x = -3\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} f(x) &= -2x^2-12x-10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2\\big[x^2+6x\\big] - 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2\\big[(x+3)^2 - 9\\big] - 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x+3)^2 + 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex\n    are  $(-3,8)$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} \\text{transformation $1$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{reflection in the $y$-axis} \\\\\n    \\text{transformation $2$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{translation of two units upwards}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we apply the transformations $1$ and $2$ to P, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/e1df7f8211664f00389891c42cbdf0bb7d3cdd9a.svg\" alt=\"e1df7f8211664f00389891c42cbdf0bb7d3cdd9a.svg\">\n\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(2,8)$"},{"ref":"AA191","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA191\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}x^2 - 2$ and $g(x) = x^2 - 4$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that $(f\\circ g)(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}x^4 - 2x^2 + 2$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  On the following grid, sketch the graph of $y = (f \\circ g)(x)$, for\n    $0 \\leq x \\leq 3$. [3 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/b4ef7d37b745cb564b996b9d79dff67b636cf799.svg\" alt=\"b4ef7d37b745cb564b996b9d79dff67b636cf799.svg\">\n\n3.  The equation $(f\\circ g)(x) = k$ has exactly two solutions,\n    for $0 \\leq x \\leq 3$. Find\\\n    the possible values of $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ g)(x) &= f(g(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(x^2 - 4) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} &= \\dfrac{1}{4}(x^2 - 4)^2 - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{4}(x^4 - 8x^2+16) - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{4}x^4 - 2x^2 + 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of $y = (f \\circ g)(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we\n    have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/35e596bf685bd72ab7dad8286b0830ba0c592c52.svg\" alt=\"35e596bf685bd72ab7dad8286b0830ba0c592c52.svg\">\n\n3.  The line $y = k$ intersects the graph of $y = (f \\circ g)(x)$\n    exactly at two points when $-2 < k \\leq 2$.\n\n    Hence the equation $(f \\circ g)(x) = k$ has exactly two solutions\n    when  $-2 < k \\leq 2$"},{"ref":"AA196","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA196\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA quadratic function $f$ can be written in the form\n$f(x) = p(x-1)(x-q)$.\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ has axis of symmetry $x = 2$ and $y$-intercept\nat $(0,-3)$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $p$. [3 marks]\n\nThe line $y = kx + 6$ is a tangent to the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n3.  Find the possible values of $k$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The $x$-coordinates of the $x$-intercepts are $x=1$ and $x=q$.\n    Hence, using the equation of the axis of symmetry, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1+q}{2} &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    1+q &= 4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2em} q = 3\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the coordinates of the $y$-intercept, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= -3 \\hspace{3.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    p(0-1)(0-3) &= -3 \\\\[6pt]\n    3p &= -3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.7em} p = -1\n    $$\n\n3.  If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = kx+6$ in the form\n    $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} -(x-1)(x-3) &= kx+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    -x^2+4x-3 &= kx+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= x^2+(k-4)x+9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the quadratic function\n    $q(x) = x^2+(k-4)x+9$ has two equal roots.\n\n    Therefore we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.2em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (k-4)^2 - 4(1)(9) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n    (k-4)^2 &= 36 \\\\[7pt]\n    k-4 &= \\pm 6 \\\\[7pt]\n    k &= 4 \\pm 6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4.75em} k=-2,\\hspace{0.25em} 10\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA201","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA201\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2(x-1)^2-8$, for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that $f(x) = 2x^2-4x-6$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $f$:\n    1.  write down the coordinates of the vertex;\n\n    2.  write down the $y$-intercept;\n    3.  find both $x$-intercepts. [7 marks]\n\n3.  Hence sketch the graph of $f$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = 6x^2$, for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe graph of $f$ may be obtained from the graph of $g$ using the\nfollowing three transformations:\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{1em} & \\bullet\\text{ a compression of scale factor $a$ in the $y$-direction, followed by} \\\\\n & \\bullet\\text{ a horizontal translation of $h$ units, followed by} \\\\\n & \\bullet\\text{ a vertical translation of $k$ units.}\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n4.  Find the values of $a, h$ and $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} f(x) &= 2(x-1)^2-8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x^2-2x+1)-8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2x^2-4x-6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $(1,-8)$\n\n    2.\n        $\\text{P}(0,-6)$\n\n    3.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        2x^2-4x-6 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2-2x-3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{0.8em} (x+1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -1,\\hspace{0.25em}3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $x$-intercepts\n        are\n         $\\text{A}(-1,0)$\n        and\n         $\\text{B}(3,0)$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $f$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/8374016b8a888aa9fc9466667cfab6ca4babdde2.svg\" alt=\"8374016b8a888aa9fc9466667cfab6ca4babdde2.svg\">\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= ag(x-h)+k \\\\[6pt]\n    2(x-1)^2 - 8 &= a\\big[6(x-h)^2\\big]+k \\\\[6pt]\n    2(x-1)^2 - 8 &= 6a(x-h)^2+k \\hspace{2.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we\n    find\n     $a = 1/3,\\hspace{0.25em} h = 1$\n    and  $k = -8$"},{"ref":"AA725","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA725\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe equation of a line $L_1$ is $2x + 3y = -5$.\n\n1.  Find the gradient of $L_1$. [2 marks]\n\nA second line, $L_2$, is perpendicular to $L_1$.\n\n2.  State the gradient of $L_2$. [1 mark]\n\nThe point P$(4,0)$ lies on $L_2$.\n\n3.  Find the equation of $L_2$, giving your answer in the form\n    $ax + by + d = 0$, where $a, b, d \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [2 marks]\n\nThe point Q is the intersection of $L_1$ and $L_2$.\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of Q. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we rewrite the equation of $L_1$ in the form $y = mx + c$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x + 3y &= -5 \\\\[12pt]\n    3y &= -2x - 5 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}x-\\dfrac{5}{3} \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the gradient of $L_1$ is\n     $m_{L_1} = -\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n2.  The gradient of $L_2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} m_{L_2} &= -\\dfrac{1}{m_{L_1}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $L_2 \\perp L_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{(-2/3)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the point-gradient formula with P$(4,0)$ and $m = 3/2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[5pt]\n    y - 0 &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}(x - 4) \\\\[6pt]\n    2y &= 3x - 12 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.45em} 0 = 3x - 2y - 12\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    L_1&:\\hspace{1.0em} 2x + 3y = -5 \\hspace{3em}{(1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    L_2&:\\hspace{1.0em} 3x - 2y = 12 \\hspace{3.25em}{(2)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving\n    the system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $x$ and $y$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{-0.8em} \\begin{array}{rcl}\n    x &=& 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &=& -3\n    \\end{array}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(2,-3)$"},{"ref":"AA728","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA728\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows the graph of a quadratic function\n$f(x) = 2x^2 + bx + c$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/75e0c507b59edc93cc9f79e429c580e90c045817.svg\" alt=\"75e0c507b59edc93cc9f79e429c580e90c045817.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $c$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$ and write down $f(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the graph of $f$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $c = -30$\n\n2.  The graph of $f$ passes through the point P$(3,0)$.\n    Hence, using the coordinates of P, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(3) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2(3)^2 + b(3) - 30 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    18 + 3b - 30 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    3b - 12 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4em} b = 4\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} f(x) = 2x^2 + 4x - 30\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The axis of symmetry of the graph of $f$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em}  x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -\\dfrac{4}{2(2)} \\\\[8pt]\n    x &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $f(x)$ for $x = -1$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.65em} f(-1) &= 2(-1)^2 + 4(-1) - 30 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex are\n     $(-1,-32)$"},{"ref":"AA729","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA729\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA farmer is going to fence two equal adjacent parcels of land. These\nparcels share one side (which also requires fencing) as shown in the\nfollowing diagram. The farmer has only $80$ metres of fence.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/580f23d360211ad7dd2c45e79d30c7d9c7d24f56.svg\" alt=\"580f23d360211ad7dd2c45e79d30c7d9c7d24f56.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation for the total length of the fence,\n    $80$ m, in terms of $x$ and $y$.\n    [1 mark]\n\n2.  Write down the total area of both parcels of land in terms of $x$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the maximum area, in m$^2$, of one parcel of land. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $80 = 3x + 4y$\n\n2.  The total area of both parcels of land in terms of $x$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} A(x) &= x(2y) \\\\[18pt]\n    &= 2xy \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 2x\\left(\\dfrac{80 - 3x}{4}\\right) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{from part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 40x - \\dfrac{3}{2}x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $y = A(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/d34855549a850484b1041b3153951956e33882fa.svg\" alt=\"d34855549a850484b1041b3153951956e33882fa.svg\">\n\n    Hence the maximum area of one parcel of land is\n    $\\dfrac{266.67}{2} \\approx$\n     $133 \\text{ m}^2$"},{"ref":"AA730","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA730\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe axis of symmetry of the graph of a quadratic function has equation\n$x = \\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\n1.  Draw the axis of symmetry on the grid below. [1 mark]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/095afe930760d162dfc8b9c36b42836af719a0ca.svg\" alt=\"095afe930760d162dfc8b9c36b42836af719a0ca.svg\">\n\nThe graph of the quadratic function intersects the $x$-axis at the point\nP$(-1,0)$. There is a second point, Q , at\nwhich the graph of the quadratic function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n2.  Mark and label P and Q on the grid above. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of the quadratic function has equation $y = -x^2+bx+c$, where\n$b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Find the value of $b$ and the value of $c$.\n\n    2.  Find the coordinates of the vertex, M.\n    3.  Draw the graph of the quadratic function on the grid above.\n        [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the axis of symmetry on the grid below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/251642e55ba01124027bed7ddba72f11d48d8750.svg\" alt=\"251642e55ba01124027bed7ddba72f11d48d8750.svg\">\n\n2.  The $x$-coordinate of $Q$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\left[\\dfrac{3}{2}-(-1)\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we mark and label P and Q on the grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/1fa563586fbd8e498bbc0f6a90199f2a3a99e7f7.svg\" alt=\"1fa563586fbd8e498bbc0f6a90199f2a3a99e7f7.svg\">\n\n3.\n    1.  Using the coordinates of P, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} 0 &= -(-1)^2 + b(-1) + c \\\\[6pt]\n        0 &= -1 - b + c \\\\[6pt]\n        1 &= -b + c \\hspace{6em}(1)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using the coordinates of\n        Q, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.6em} 0 &= -(4)^2 + b(4) + c \\\\[6pt]\n        0 &= -16 + 4b + c \\\\[6pt]\n        16 &= 4b + c \\hspace{7.5em}(2)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, solving the system\n        of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $b$ and $c$, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{10em} b = 3\\hspace{0.75em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.75em}c = 4\\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = -x^2 + 3x + 4$ for $x = 3/2 = 1.5$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.2em} y &= -(1.5)^2 + 3(1.5) + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 6.25\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of M are\n         $(1.5,6.25)$\n\n    3.  If we draw the graph of $y = -x^2 + 3x + 4$ on the grid below,\n        we obtain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/db963f9fd832653990678f1ba763db2a3974d991.svg\" alt=\"db963f9fd832653990678f1ba763db2a3974d991.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA732","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA732\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph shows the curve of a quadratic function of the form\n$f(x) = ax^2 + bx + 90$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/e02d6931ce58420fa7c9cbe422473aefce55e151.svg\" alt=\"e02d6931ce58420fa7c9cbe422473aefce55e151.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation for the axis of symmetry of the curve.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the curve. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  The axis of symmetry intersects the $x$-axis midway between the\n    $x$-intercepts.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.15em} x &= \\dfrac{-5+9}{2} \\\\\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.4em} x = 2\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substitute the given coordinates into $f(x)=ax^2+bx+90.$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the coordinates of A, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-5) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    a(-5)^2 + b(-5) + 90 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    25a - 5b &= -90 \\hspace{1em} \\hspace{6em}(1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the\n    coordinates of B, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(9) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{0.6em} a(9)^2 + b(9) + 90 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    81a + 9b &= -90 \\hspace{7.5em}(2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the system\n    of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$ [by using G.D.C.].\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.4em} a = -2\\hspace{0.75em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.75em}b = 8\\hspace{3em}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The vertex of the curve lies on the axis of symmetry.\\\n    Hence, evaluating $f(x)$ for $x = 2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.8em} f(2) &= -2(2)^2 + 8(2) + 90 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 98\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA733","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA733\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph of a quadratic function has a $y$-intercept at A$(0,24)$ and\n**one** of its $x$-intercepts is B$(2,0)$.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is $4$.\n\nThe equation of the quadratic function is in the form $y = ax^2+bx+c$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $c$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the coordinates of the second $x$-intercept of the\n    function. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  $c = 24$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.4em} 4 &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\hspace{3em} \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\\n    \\\\\n    b &= -8a \\hspace{7em}(1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the coordinates of B, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0 &= a(2)^2 + b(2) + c  \\\\[6pt]\n    [\\text{from $(1)$ \\& part (a)}] \\hspace{2em} 0 &= a(2)^2 + (-8a)(2) + 24 \\hspace{3.6em} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\boxed{a = 2}   \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting $a = 2$ in the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    b &= -8(2) \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    b = -16 \\hspace{1.7em}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    $(6,0)$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA744","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA744\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe ocean pressure, $P$, under sea level can be modelled by the function\n\n$$\nP(D) = \\dfrac{D}{10}+1\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the distance in metres below sea\nlevel and $P$ is measured in atmospheres.\\\nA submarine cruising near the surface is submerged according to the\nfunction\n$$\nD(t) = 10+5t\n\n$$\nwhere $t$ is measured in minutes and $D$ is\nthe distance the submarine is below sea level, measured in\nmetres.\n\n1.  Find the composite function $P\\circ D$ and explain what it means in\n    the\\\n    context of this question[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find and interpret $(P\\circ D)(10)$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The composite function can be expressed as\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ D) (t) &= P(D(t)) \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &= P(10+5t)\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &= \\dfrac{10+5t}{10}+1 \\rule[-4.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{0.28em}= 2+\\dfrac{t}{2}\\rule[-5.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\hspace{6em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{This composite function represents the ocean pressure (in atmospheres) outside the submarine at time $t$ minutes.}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $t=10$ in (1), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ D)(10)&=2+\\dfrac{10}{2}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{0.28em}=7\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{This result means that after 10 minutes, the ocean pressure outside the submarine is 7 atmospheres.}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA745","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA745\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA tyre manufacturing company has found that the number of tyres it\nproduces, $N$, can be modelled by the function\n$$\nN(t)=3t-9\n\n$$\nwhere $t$\nis the number of hours the factory operates per day, with a minimum of 3\nhours.\\\nThe profit the company makes, $P$, in dollars, depends on the number of\ntyres produced, and is modelled by the function\n$$\nP(N)=60N-850\n\n$$\nwhere\n$N$ is the number of tyres produced.\n\n1.  Find the company's profit or loss if it operates for $6$ hours per\n    day.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the company's profit in terms of the hours of operation per\n    day, $t$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine the number of hours the company needs to operate the\n    factory per day in order to earn a positive profit. Give your answer\n    to the nearest hour.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $t=6$ in $N(t)$, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     N(6) &= 3(6)-9 = 9\\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n    Thus, the profit is given by P(9)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n     P(9) &= 60(9)-850 = -310\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n     Therefore, operating $6$\n    hours a day, the company has a loss:\n    $$\\$\\hspace{0.07em}-310$$\n\n2.  The profit in terms of the hours of operation per day can be found\n    by the composite function $P\\circ N$. Hence, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ N)(t) &= P(N(t)) \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &= P(3t-9) \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    &= 60(3t-9)-850\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-5em}(P\\circ N)(t) = 180\\hspace{0.05em}t-1390\\hspace{5em}{(1)}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The company has a positive profit if (1) is greater than 0. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ N)(t) &>0\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    180t-1390 &> 0 \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    t &> \\dfrac{1390}{180} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    t &> 7.72.\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5.5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, the\n    factory needs to operate\n    $8$\n    hours per day in order for the company to earn a positive profit."},{"ref":"AA759","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA759\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe line $y = x + 1$ touches the graph of the function\n$g(x) = 2x^2 + kx + 3$ at one point. Find the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Letting $g(x) = y$ we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^2 + kx + 3 &= x + 1   \\\\[10pt]\n2x^2 + (k-1)x + 2 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n[\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{discriminant of quadratic above}\\hspace{0.1em}] &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n(k-1)^2 - 4(2)(2) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[10pt]\nk^2 - 2k + 1 - 16 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\nk^2 - 2k - 15 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n(k - 5)(k + 3) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.5em} k = -3, \\, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA763","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA763\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = -6x^2 + bx + c$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  A horizontal line, $L$, intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $(-1,h)$ and $(5,h)$, where $h \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    1.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $b = 24$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given $h=-8$, find the value of $c$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The equation of the axis of symmetry is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= \\dfrac{-1 + 5}{2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}x= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[12pt]\n        \\hspace{10em} 2 &= -\\dfrac{b}{2(-6)} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)(i)}] \\\\[12pt]\n        & \\hspace{-0.5em} b = 24\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Since the line $L$ intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at $(-1,-8)$,\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-1) &= -8  \\\\[12pt]\n    -6(-1)^2 + 24(-1) + c &= -8 \\\\[12pt]\n    -6 - 24 + c &= -8 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.45em} c = 22\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA982","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA982\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x)=4x-2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. The graph of a linear function $g$ is perpendicular to the graph of $f$ and passes through the origin. Find an expression for $g(x)$.[2 marks]\n\n2. The graph of a linear function $h$ is parallel to the graph of $f$ and passes through the point $(3,4)$. Find an expression for $h(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The slope of $f$ is $m_f=4$, then $m_g=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ is the slope of the function $g$ that is perpendicular to $f$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n     y-0&=-\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-0)\\\\[6pt]\n\n     y&=-\\dfrac{1}{4}x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{g(x)=-\\dfrac{1}{4}x}.$\n\n2. The slope of the function $h$ is $m_h=4$ because $h$ is parallel to the graph $f$. We have, \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y-4&=4(x-3)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y-4&=4x-12\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y=4x-8\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{h(x)=4x-8}.$"},{"ref":"AA983","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA983\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe graph of a function $f$ has its vertex at the point $(1,7)$ and intersects the $x$-axis at $x=-3$. \n\n1. Find $f$ in the form $f(x)=a\\left(x-h\\right)^2+k$. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $g$ is defined by $g(x)=mx^2-2nx+\\dfrac{2n^2}{m}$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2. In the case where $g(-2)=g(1)=3$,\n\n    1. find the value of $m$ and the value of $n$;\n\n    2. Find the range of $g$.[7 marks]  \n\nThe linear function $h$ is defined by $h(x)=nx+1$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3. Show that the graphs of $h$ and $g$ have two distinct points of intersection for every possible value of $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{R}$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. In the form $f(x)=a\\left(x-h\\right)^2+k$, $h$ is the $x$-coordinate of the vertex, and $k$ is the $y$-coordinate. Hence $h=1$ and $k=7$, and $f(x)=a\\left(x-1\\right)^2+7$.\n\n    The graph of $f$ passes through the point $(-3,0)$, so we have $f(-3)=0$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(-3) &= a\\left(-3-1\\right)^2+7 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    0 &= 16a + 7 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a &= -\\dfrac{7}{16}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\boxed{f(x)=-\\dfrac{7}{16}\\left(x-1\\right)^2+7}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. 1. We have that $g(-2)=g(1)=3$, so $g$ passes through the points $(-2,3)$ and $(1,3)$. Given that $g$ is a quadratic and hence its graph is a parabola, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/aa983a.PNG\" alt=\"aa983a\">\n\n        The axis of symmetry must be halfway between these points and has equation $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$. Using the formula for the axis of symmetry, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        -\\dfrac{1}{2} &= -\\dfrac{-2n}{2m} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        m &= -2n\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n        Substituting $-2n$ in place of $m$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x) &= -2nx^2 -2nx + \\dfrac{2n^2}{-2n} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= -2nx^2 - 2nx - n\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And given that $g(1)=3$,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(1) &= -2n(1)^2 - 2n(1) - n \\\\[8pt]\n\n        3 &= -5n \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{n = -\\dfrac{3}{5}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And calculating the value of $m$,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m &= -2n \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= -2\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{m = \\dfrac{6}{5}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. As stated in part (b)(i), the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$. The $y$-coordinate will therefore be equal to $g\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x) &= \\dfrac{6}{5}x^2 + \\dfrac{6}{5}x + \\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        g\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) &= \\left(\\dfrac{6}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) + \\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{10} - \\dfrac{6}{10} + \\dfrac{6}{10} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{10}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The vertex is at $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right)$ and the graph of $g$ is as follows:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/aa983b.PNG\" alt=\"aa983b\">\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\boxed{\\text{The range of $g$ is $g(x) \\geq \\dfrac{3}{10}$.}}  \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3. If $g$ and $h$ always have two distinct points of intersection, then the equation $g(x)=h(x)$ must have two unique solutions. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g(x) &= h(x) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    mx^2-2nx+\\dfrac{2n^2}{m} &= nx + 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    mx^2 - 3nx + \\dfrac{2n^2}{m} -1 &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can use the discriminant to check the number of solutions. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\Delta &= b^2 - 4ac \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= (-3n)^2 - 4(m)\\left(\\dfrac{2n^2}{m} -1\\right)\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=(-3n)^2 - 4(m)\\left(\\dfrac{2n^2}{m} -1\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=9n^2 - 8n^2 + 4m \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=n^2 + 4m \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$, $4m>0$. $n^2 \\geq 0$. Hence $\\Delta>0$. Thus\n\n    $$\\boxed{\\text{The graphs of $h$ and $g$ have two distinct points of intersection for all $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.}}$$"}]},{"name":"Rational Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1013","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1013\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x+4}{2x-5}$, for $x\\neq\\dfrac{5}{2}$. \n\n1. For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the coordinates of:\n\n    1. the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2. the $y$-intercept. [3 marks]\n\n2. For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the equation of:\n\n    1. the horizontal asymptote;\n\n    2. the vertical asymptote. [3 marks]\n\n3. Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$.  [2 marks]\n\n4. Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]\n\nThe graphs of $f(x)$ and $f^{-1}(x)$ have two points of intersection.\n\n5. Find the coordinates of the two points of intersection. **Give your answers to two decimal places**. [3 marks]\n\n6. Hence find the equation of the perpendicular bisector to the two points of intersection. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{3x+4}{2x-5} &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        3x+4 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        x &= -\\dfrac{4}{3}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{3},0\\right)$\n\n    \n\n    2. Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{3(0)+4}{2(0)-5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= -\\dfrac{4}{5}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $\\left(0,-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)$\n\n2. 1. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty} f(x) &= \\dfrac{3}{2} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\n    2. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        [\\text{denominator of $f$}] &= 0 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        2x-5 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x &= \\dfrac{5}{2}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = \\dfrac{5}{2}$\n\n3. If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/aa1013a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1013a\">\n\n4. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y &= \\dfrac{3x+4}{2x-5} \\hspace{5.5em} \\left[\\text{where } y = f(x)\\right]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{3y+4}{2y-5} \\hspace{3em} \\left[\\text{where } y = f^{-1}(x)\\right] \\\\[8pt]\n\n    x(2y-5) &= 3y+4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2xy - 5x &= 3y +4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2xy - 3y &= 5x + 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y(2x - 3) &= 5x + 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y &= \\dfrac{5x+4}{2x-3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3em}\\boxed{f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{5x+4}{2x-3}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. Sketching $f(x)$ and $f^{-1}(x)$ on the same axes, we can find the points of intersection using the G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/aa1013b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1013b\">\n\n6. The midpoint of AB is given by\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(\\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2} , \\dfrac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right) &= \\left(\\dfrac{-0.45+4.45}{2} , \\dfrac{-0.45+4.45}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= (2,2)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Point A and point B both lie on the line $y=x$, which has gradient $m=1$. Therefore the perpendicular bisector has gradient $m=-1$.\n\n    The equation of the perpendicular bisector is given by\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y - 2 &= -(x - 2) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{y = -x + 4}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1149","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1149\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the function $\\displaystyle f(x)=b+\\frac{4}{x+a}$, $\\hspace{1em}$ where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThe equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$ is $x=2$ and $f(\\tfrac{2}{3})=0$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $a$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Hence, find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Hence, write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote.[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  The vertical asymptote occurs when the denominator of the fraction is equal to zero.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2+a&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{a=-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  From the information given we know that the point $(\\tfrac{2}{3},0)$ lies on the curve.\n\n    Hence, we can create an equation and solve it for $b$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=b+\\frac{4}{\\tfrac{2}{3}-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        b&=-\\frac{4}{-\\tfrac{4}{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{b=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The horizontal asymptote occurs as $x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty$. We can see that as this happens the fractional part of $f(x)$ will tend to $0$.\n\n    Hence the equation of the horizontal asymptote is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y&=3+\\frac{4}{\\pm \\infty-2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{y=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The answer must be given as an equation, either $y=3$ or $f(x)=3$."},{"ref":"AA126","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA126\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nA rational function is defined by $f(x) = a + \\dfrac{b}{x-c}$, for\n$x \\neq c$, where $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThe following diagram represents the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/174d0bc6234e556e44492ded9dca1f7d93a7be93.svg\" alt=\"174d0bc6234e556e44492ded9dca1f7d93a7be93.svg\">\n\nUsing the information on the graph,\n\n1.  state the value of $a$ and the value of\n    $c$ ; [2 marks]\n\n2.  find the value of $b$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a = -4$  and\n     $c = 4$\n\n2.  The point P lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n    Hence, using the coordinates of P, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} 0 &= f(6) \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= -4 + \\dfrac{b}{6-4} \\\\[2pt]\n    4 &= \\dfrac{b}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b = 8 \\hspace{1.9em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA127","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA127\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is of the form $f(x) = \\dfrac{ax+b}{2x+c}$, for\n$x \\neq -\\dfrac{c}{2}$, where $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\nGiven\n\nthat the graph of $y = f(x)$ has asymptotes $x = -5$ and $y = 2$, and\nthat the point\n\nP$\\left(1,-\\dfrac{1}{12}\\right)$ lies on the graph, find the values of\n$a, b$ and $c$.","markScheme":"Using the vertical asymptote $x = -5$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{[denominator of $f$ at $-5$]} &= 0 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n2(-5) + c &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5em} c = 10\n$$\n\nUsing the horizontal asymptote $y = 2$, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.1em} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\pm\\infty} f(x) &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{a}{2} &= 2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{4.4em} a = 4\n$$\n\nUsing the coordinates of the point P, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= -\\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{3.4em} \\dfrac{4+b}{2+10} &= -\\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\[10pt]\n4+b &= -1 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.2em} b = -5\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1283","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1283\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{3x+4}{2x+k}$.\n\n1.  Write down the domain of $f$ in terms of $k$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$ in terms of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence write down the value of $k$ such that $f(x)$ is a self-inverse function.[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  A rational function is undefined when the denominator is equal to zero.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2x+k&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    x&=-\\frac{k}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Any other value for $x$ is valid.\n\n    Hence we get the domain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{x \\in \\mathbb{R}, x\\neq-\\frac{k}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We are trying to find the function $y=f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n    The definition of an inverse states that\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (f \\circ f^{-1})(x)&=x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we can rewrite this to get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(f^{-1}(x))&=x\\\\[6pt]\n\n        f(y)&=x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(y)&=x\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{3y+4}{2y+k}&=x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall, we want $y=f^{-1}(x)$ therefore we should rearrange this to make $y$ the subject.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x(2y+k)&=3y+4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2xy+kx&=3y+4\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    To isolate $y$, we move all of the $y$ terms to one side and then factorise out $y$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2xy-3y&=-kx+4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y(2x-3)&=-kx+4\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y&=\\frac{-kx+4}{2x-3}\\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3.2em}f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{-kx+4}{2x-3}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  A self-inverse function is such that $f(x)\\equiv f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{3x+4}{2x+k}&\\equiv \\frac{-kx+4}{2x-3}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Comparing either the numerators or denominators,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3x+4&=-kx+4\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{k=-3}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1316","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1316\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=\\dfrac{a-3x}{x-2}$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x\\neq 2$ and $a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.    For the graph of $y=f(x)$, write down the equation of\n\n    1.    the vertical asymptote; \n\n    2.    the horizontal asymptote. [2 marks]\n\nIt is known that $f(1)=f^{-1}(1)$.\n\n2.    Find the value of $a$. [3 marks]\n\n3.    Hence, sketch the graph of $f$ on the axes below. On your sketch, indicate the values of any axis intercepts and label any asymptotes with their equations.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/AA1316q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1316q1\">\n\n     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  $f$ is a rational function. This means it can be written as a fraction with a function of $x$ in the numerator and the denominator.\n\n    1. The vertical asymptote occurs when $f$ is undefined.\n\n        $f$ is undefined for any $x$-value that causes the denominator to equal zero.\n\n        Hence, we can form and solve an equation to find the value of $x$ that makes $f$ undefined.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x-2&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        x&=2\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The answer must be written as an equation. Hence  $x=2$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. The horizontal asymptote of $f$ can be found by considering extremely large positive and negative values.\n\n        Let's consider the numerator $a-3x$ to begin with.\n\n        If $x$ becomes extremely large in the positive or negative direction, then the value of $a-3x$ becomes closer to $-3x$. The value of $a$ is less and less significant to the value of $a-3x$ as the size of $x$ grows.\n\n        \n\n        Similarly, considering the denominator, the value of $x-2$ approaches $x$ as the size of $x$ grows.\n\n        Hence, if we put that together, for large $x$ values we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(x)&=\\frac{-3\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=-3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Notice that, as the $x$'s cancel out, we don't need to worry about $x$ being positively or negatively large.\n\n        Therefore the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. We know that a function and its inverse will be equivalent on the line $y=x$. \n\n    It is known that the inverse functions are equivalent when $x=1$. Hence we also know that $f(x)=1$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can substitute $1$ in place of $x$ and $f(x)$ to solve for $a$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{a-3(1)}{(1)-2}&=1\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    -a+3&=1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a&=2\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. First, we can find the axis intercepts.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y&\\text{-intercept}&&&x&\\text{-intercept}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f(0)&=\\frac{2-3(0)}{(0)-2}&&&0&=\\frac{2-3x}{x-2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=-1&&&&=\\frac{2}{3}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the answers from part (a) we can draw the asymptotes and also plot the intercepts.\n\n    We can then complete the sketch.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/AA1316a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1316a1\">\n\n    $\\text{Correct intercepts and asymptotes}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Correct function shape}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note, the function must not touch the asymptotes."},{"ref":"AA185","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA185\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x-6}{x+1}$, for $x\\neq-1$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the equation of:\n    1.  the horizontal asymptote;\n\n    2.  the vertical asymptote. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{3x-6}{x+1} &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        3x-6 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n        x &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept\n        are  $(2,0)$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.8em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3(0)-6}{0+1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the $y$-intercept\n        are  $(0,-6)$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty} f(x) &= \\dfrac{3}{1} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the equation of the horizontal\n        asymptote is\n         $y = 3$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &= 0 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        x+1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the equation of the vertical\n        asymptote is\n         $x = -1$\n\n3.  Hence, if we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/5aa8339f9ee09bb9f920e735960c8b3bddfc8e2c.svg\" alt=\"5aa8339f9ee09bb9f920e735960c8b3bddfc8e2c.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA189","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA189\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x-a} + b$, for $x \\neq a$.\n\nThe line $x = 1$ is a vertical asymptote of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $a$. [1 mark]\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point P$(0,3)$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a=1$\n\n2.  Using the coordinates of P, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= 3 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{2}{0-1} + b &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    -2+b &= 3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b = 5 \\hspace{0.3em}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty}\\left[\\dfrac{2}{x-1} + 5\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0 + 5 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA192","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA192\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x+1}$, for $x \\neq -1$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $f$, find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept;\n\n    3.  the equation of the vertical asymptote. [5 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = x-3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The graphs of $f$ and $g$\nintersect at points A and B.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of A and B. [5 marks]\n\nLine $L$ is the perpendicular bisector of A and B.\n\n3.  Find the equation of $L$, giving your answer in the form $y = mx + c$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the $x$-intercept of $L$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Since the equation $f(x) = 0$ has no solutions, there are\n         $\\text{no \\(x\\)-intercepts}$\n        $\\\\[-20pt]$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x+1}$ for $x = 0$, we have\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         f(0) &= \\dfrac{5}{0+1} \\hspace{3.6em} \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n         &= 5\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n         Hence the $y$-intercept is\n         $\\mathrm{P}(0,5)$\n        $\\\\[-20pt]$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x+1 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the equation of the vertical\n        asymptote is\n         $x = -1$\n        $\\\\[-20pt]$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = g(x)$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{5}{x+1} &= x-3 \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}} \\\\[12pt]\n        5 &= (x+1)(x-3) \\\\[9pt]\n        5 &= x^2 - 2x - 3 \\\\[9pt]\n        0 &= x^2 - 2x - 8 \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[10pt]\n        0 &= (x+2)(x-4) \\\\[10pt]\n        x &= -2,4 \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $g(x) = x-3$\n    for $x = -2$ and $x = 4$, we find\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.1em} \\begin{array}{rcccl}\n    y &=& g(-2) &=& -5 \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\\\\[9pt]\n    y &=& g(4) &=& 1.\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $\\mathrm{A}(-2,-5)$\n    and\n     $\\mathrm{B}(4,1)$\n\n3.  As A and B lie on $g$ then the gradient of line AB is the gradient of $g$, therefore $m_{AB}=1$.\n\n    Hence, the gradient of $L$, the perpendicular bisector, is $m_L=-1$.\n\n    The midpoint of A$(-2,-5)$ and B$(4,1)$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    M_{AB}&= \\left(\\frac{-2+4}{2},\\frac{-5+1}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= (1,-2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the point-gradient form of the equation of a line (__2.1__)with  $M_{AB}(1,-2)$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y-(-2) &= -1(x-1)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    y+2 &= -x+1\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}y=-x-1\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    At the $x$-intercept, $y=0$.\n\n    Substituting this into the equation of $L$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0 &= -x-1\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -1.\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Transformations","questions":[{"ref":"AA1272","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1272\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider $f(x)=2\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3$ and $g(x)=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2$.\n\nThe function $f$ is mapped onto $g$ \n\n by three transformations.\n\n1.  Fully describe each of the transformations and the order in which they must be applied. [3 marks]\n\nA new function is such that $h(x)=g(x)+k$ where $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Find the minimum value of $k$ such that $h(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  By considering the differences between the functions we can work out the transformations needed to map $f(x)$ to $g(x)$.\n\n    First we notice that the inner functions are different.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x)=2\\cos\\left(x\\textcolor{blue}{\\,-\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\right)+3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    g(x)=4\\cos\\left(x\\textcolor{blue}{\\,+\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\right)+2\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    If we add $\\pi$ units to the inner function of $f(x)$, it will equal the inner function of  $g(x)$.   \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x+\\pi)&=2\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\pi\\right)+3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f(x+\\pi)&=2\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Comparing the function $f(x+\\pi)$ to $g(x)$, we notice that the coefficient of cosine has been doubled.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x+\\pi)=\\textcolor{green}{2}\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    g(x)=\\textcolor{green}{4}\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence, if we multiply $f(x+\\pi)$ by $2$ we would have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)=2(2\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+6\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    If we continue comparing the function $2f(x+\\pi)$ to $g(x)$, we see there is a difference of $4$ units. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\textcolor{red}{\\,+\\,6}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    g(x)&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\textcolor{red}{\\,+\\,2}\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    Hence, if we apply a vertical shift of $-4$ to $2f(x+\\pi)$, we will obtain $g(x)$.    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)-4&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+6-4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=g(x)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n    Summarising the $3$ transformations, there is\n\n    a horizontal shift $\\textbf{left}$ of $\\pi$ units, followed by a vertical stretch by a scale factor of $2$ , followed by a vertical shift of $-4$ units.\n\n    Note: The horizontal shift could also come after the vertical transformations, however the two vertical transformations must be applied in the order given. \n\n2.  In order to apply a vertical translation such that $g(x)>0$ for all $x$, we need to know the minimum value of $g(x)$.\n\n    The minimum value of cosine is $-1$. Hence we can determine the minimum of $g(x)$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x)&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4(-1)+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=-2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence, we need to translate the graph vertically upwards at least $2$ units. \n\n    \n\n    Therefore, the minimum value is $k=2$.    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA133","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA133\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n4x-1 &\\text{if}&  x \\geq 1, \\\\[6pt]\n-x^2+x+3  &\\text{if}&  x<1.\n\\end{array}\\right.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Determine whether or not $f$ is continuous at $x = 1$. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of the function $g$ is obtained by applying the following\ntransformations\\\nto the graph of $f$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\hspace{-5em}\\text{a horizontal translation $2$ units to the left, followed by}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-5em}\\text{a reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-5em}\\text{a vertical stretch by a factor of $3$.}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n2.  Find $g(x)$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rcl}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} f(1) &=& 4(1)-1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=& 3\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{array}{rcl}\n    \\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 1^-} f(x) &=& -1^2 + 1 + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=& 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $\\text{\\(f\\) is continuous at \\(x = 1\\)}$\n\n2.  If we translate the graph of $y = f(x)$ horizontally $2$ units to\n    the left, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} f(x+2) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    4x+7      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    -(x+2)^2+x+5  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we reflect the graph of\n    $y = f(x+2)$ in the $x$-axis, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -f(x+2) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    -4x-7      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    (x+2)^2-x-5  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we stretch the graph of\n    $y = -f(x+2)$ vertically by a factor of $3$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} -3f(x+2) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    -12x-21      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    3(x+2)^2-3x-15  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the function $g$ is defined\n    by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.3em} \\answer{g(x) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    -12x-21      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    3(x+2)^2-3x-15  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA173","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA173\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3$ and $g(x) = 5(x-2)^3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe graph of $g$ can be obtained from the graph of $f$ using two transformations.\n\n1.  Give a full geometric description of each of the transformations. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of $g$ is translated by 4 units in the direction of the positive $x$-axis, and 1 unit in the direction of the negative $y$-axis to give the graph of a\nfunction $h$.\n\nThe point P$(1,1)$ on the graph of $f$ is translated to point Q on the\ngraph of $h$.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of Q. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    Transformation $1$: $$\\text{vertical stretch by a scale factor of $5$}$$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Transformation $2$: $$\\text{translation of 2 units in the direction of the positive $x$-axis.}$$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. If we apply the transformations $1$ and $2$ from part (a), and transformation $3$ from part (b) to P, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Transformations/images/577066d1f46b6880207621796d732b7e8ac43d46.svg\" alt=\"577066d1f46b6880207621796d732b7e8ac43d46.svg\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(7,4)$\n     $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA180","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA180\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$, for\n$-1 \\leq x \\leq 2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Transformations/images/ccc9d5942ccfefc2e6313a34922fed1ada13594f.svg\" alt=\"ccc9d5942ccfefc2e6313a34922fed1ada13594f.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $f(1)$;\n\n    2.  $f^{-1}(-2)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $(f\\circ f)(1)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(-x)$ on the same grid above. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $f(1) = 2$\n\n    2.\n        $f^{-1}(-2) = -1$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ f)(1) &= f(f(1)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The graph of $y = f(-x)$ is a reflection of the graph of $y = f(x)$\n    in the $y$-axis.\n\n    Hence, if we sketch the graph of $y = f(-x)$ on the same grid below,\n    we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Transformations/images/d2b3518aa3484d1ffd19b0a1dd3f20d6f92c6046.svg\" alt=\"d2b3518aa3484d1ffd19b0a1dd3f20d6f92c6046.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA181","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA181\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that $g(x) = 3f(x-2)+1$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe graph of $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ after the following\ntransformations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{a vertical stretch by a factor of $k$, followed by} \\\\\n&\\text{a horizontal translation of $a$ units, followed by} \\\\\n&\\text{a vertical translation of $b$ units}. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $k$;\n\n    2.  $a$;\n    3.  $b$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $h(x) = -2g(x)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe point P$(3,4)$ on the graph of $g$ is mapped to point Q on the graph\nof $h$.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of Q. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $k = 3$\n\n    2.\n        $a = 2$\n\n    3.\n        $b = 1$\n\n2.  The graph of $h$ is obtained from the graph of $g$ using two\n    transformations.\n\n    Their geometric descriptions are:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical stretch by a factor of $2$;} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{reflection in the $x$-axis.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we apply the transformations $1$ and $2$ to P, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Transformations/images/1510a052325892668888784527ddbb81323a45d9.svg\" alt=\"1510a052325892668888784527ddbb81323a45d9.svg\">\n\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(3,-8)$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA1037","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1037\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe arithmetic sequence has the sixth term of $u_6=\\ln{2}$ and the eighth term of $u_8=\\frac{3}{2}\\ln{4}$.\n\n1.  1.  Show the common difference is $\\ln2$.\n\n    2.  Find $u_1$.\n\n    3.  Hence, show that the sum of $n$ terms is given by $S_n=\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}} $. Where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$. [5 marks]\n\nA new function, $f(x)$, is formed using $S_n$ with the discrete variable $n$ being replaced with the continuous variable $x$, such that $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Hence $f(x)=\\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n2.  1.  Write down the equation of the axis of symmetry of $f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Sketch $f(x)$ on the grid below, identify the axis of symmetry and any axis intercepts.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Unassigned/images/q1037.png\" alt=\"q1037\">\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the minimum value of $S_n$ in the form $a\\ln b$. Where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ [5 marks]\n\nConsider the linear function $g(x)=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$.\n\n3.  1.  Solve $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, solve the inequality $f(x)<g(x)$.\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the range of values of $n$ that satisfy $S_n<g(x)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As the sequence is arithmetic we can write $u_8=u_6+2d$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\tfrac{3}{2}\\ln{4}&=\\ln{2}+2d \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            2d&=3\\ln{(\\sqrt{4})}-\\ln2\\\\\n            2d&=2\\ln{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\ln2}\\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We can use $u_6$ to find the first term\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            u_6&=u_1+5d\\\\\n            \\ln2&=u_1+5\\ln2\\\\\n            u_1&=\\hspace{0.525em}-4\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(2\\times -4 \\ln2 +(n-1)\\times \\ln2\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            &=\\frac{n^2}{2}\\ln2-\\frac{9n}{2}\\ln2\\\\\n            &=\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\left(n^2-9n\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln{\\sqrt{2}} \\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  1.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x&=-\\frac{-9\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}\\\\\n            &\\hspace{-0.525em}x=\\tfrac{9}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  Here is the sketch of $f(x)$ with the desired features identified.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Unassigned/images/a1037-1.png\" alt=\"a1037-1\">\n\n        $\\text{Correct intercepts}$ **A1** and $\\text{axis of symmetry}$ **A1**.\n\n    3.  Using the symmetry of $f(x)$ we can identify the minimum value(s) of $f(x)$ which has integer $x$ coordinates.\n\n        Both points are equidistant from the axis of symmetry and they represent the minimum value of $S_n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Unassigned/images/a1037-2.png\" alt=\"a1037-2\">\n\n        We can use the $x-$value from the graph to pick either $n=4$ or $n=5$ as both will yield the same minimum value. This solution will pick $n=4$, hence, the minimum value of $S_n$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_4&=\\left(4^2-9(4)\\right)\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\\n            &=-20\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}-10\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1.  Equate the RHS of each function\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}&=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Cancel out the factor $\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$ and solve for $x$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x^2-9x&=6-3x\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n            0&=x^2-6x-6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can solve this by completing the square.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (x-3)^2-9-6&=0\\\\\n            x-3&=\\pm\\sqrt{15}\\\\\n            x&=\\hspace{0.525em}3\\pm\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  By making a rough sketch of $g(x)$ on the graph we can solve the inequality\\\\\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Unassigned/images/a1037-3.png\" alt=\"a1037-3\">\n\n        We can identify the region the $f(x)<g(x)$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\hspace{0.525em}3-\\sqrt{15}<x<3+\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  $n$ must be a positive integer and lie entirely within the region identified in part (c)(ii).\n\n        We know that $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}$, hence we can identify the minimum and maximum values of $n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        The minimum value must be an integer larger than $3-\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -1<3-\\sqrt{15}<0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The minimum value of $n$ is $1$.\n\n        Similarly, the maximum value must be an integer smaller than $3+\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            6<3+\\sqrt{15}<7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The maximum value of $n$ is $6$.\n\n        Hence\n        $1\\leq n\\leq 6$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA1047","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the range of possible values for $p$ such that $$e^{2x}+2\\ln p = 4e^x$$ has at least one real solution.","markScheme":"The equation is a hidden quadratic. Let $k=e^x$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^2 - 4k + 2 \\ln p &= 0  \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the discriminant we can say a quadratic equation has at least one real solution if $ b^2-4ac\\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, we have at least one real solution for $k$ if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-4)^2-4(2\\ln p) &\\geq 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[7pt]\n16-8\\ln p &\\geq 0 \\\\[7pt]\n8\\ln p &\\leq 16  \\\\[7pt]\n\\ln p &\\leq 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-2.33em}\\boxed{0 < p \\leq e^2} \\hspace{3em} \\text{($\\ln p > 0$)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\\\\[-11pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA199","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA199\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{3\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)}$, for $x > 0$.\n\nThe $k$th maximum point on the graph of $f$ has $x$-coordinate $x_k$,\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.  Given that $x_{k+1} = x_k + d$, find $d$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the value of $n$ such that\n    $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k = 992$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y= f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Functions/Unassigned/images/97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\" alt=\"97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $x$-coordinates of the points A$(2,20.1)$ and\n    B$(10,20.1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} x_2 &= x_1 + d \\\\[6pt]\n    10 &= 2 + d \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 8\n    $$\n\n2.  We have an arithmetic sequence with the first term $x_1 = 2$ and the\n    common difference $d = 8$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2x_1+(n-1)d)$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k &= 992 \\\\[6pt]\n    S_n &= 992 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{with $x_1 = 2$ \\& $d = 8$}] \\\\[13pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(2)+(n-1)(8)) &= 992 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.1em} n = 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA234","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA234\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLet $g(x) = -4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $h(x) = 5\\cos x - 1$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $(h\\circ g)(x) = 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 1$.\n     [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the range of $h\\circ g$.  [2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $(h\\circ g)\\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}\\Big) = 4$, find the next\n    value of $x$, greater than $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}$,\n\n    for which $(h\\circ g)(x)=4$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  The graph of $y = (h\\hspace{0.075em}\\circ\\hspace{0.075em}g)(x)$ can\n    be obtained by applying five $\\text{transformations}$ to the\n    graph of $y = \\cos x$. State what the five transformations represent\n    $\\text{geometrically}$ and give the order in which they are\n    applied. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (h\\circ g)(x) &= h(g(x)) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= h\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} -1 &\\leq \\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\leq 1 \\\\[3pt]\n    -5 &\\leq 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\leq 5 \\\\[3pt]\n    -6 &\\leq 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-1 \\leq 4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the range of $h\\circ g$ is\n     $[-6,4]$\n\n3.  The period of the function $h\\circ g$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{|-4|} \\hspace{1.05em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the next value of $x$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{compression parallel to the $x$-axis by a factor of $1/4$} \\hspace{1.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{reflection in the $y$-axis} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $3$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{stretch parallel to the $y$-axis by a factor of $5$} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $4$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{horizontal translation $\\pi/12$ units to the left} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $5$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{vertical translation $1$ unit down}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA922","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA922\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the functions $f(x)=3\\cos(x)+\\dfrac{9}{2}$ and $g(x)=3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+A$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $A< \\dfrac{9}{2}$.\n\n1. Describe a sequence of two transformations that transforms the graph of $f$ to the graph of $g$. [3 marks]\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the graph $g$ is at the point $\\left(0\\,,\\dfrac{9}{2}\\right)$\n\n2. Find the range of $g$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Applying a translation of $a$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis to a function $f$ is shown as $f(x+a)$, and a translation of $b$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis is shown as $f(x)-b$. \n\n    Therefore a translation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis would be $f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\dfrac{9}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that $A < \\dfrac{9}{2}$, if we then apply a translation of $\\dfrac{9}{2} - A$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) - \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{2} - A\\bigg) &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\dfrac{9}{2} - \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{2} - A\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) + A \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= g(x)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we have the following transformations in any order\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    T_1 &: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{translation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    T_2 &: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{translation of $\\dfrac{9}{2} - A$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We have $g(0)=\\dfrac{9}{2}$. Solving for $A$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3\\cos\\left(0+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+A &= \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) + A & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    A & = 3 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $g(x)=3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3$ , and the following inequalities hold:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    -1\\leq \\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &\\leq 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    -3\\leq 3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &\\leq 3 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    0\\leq 3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3 &\\leq 6 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    0\\leq g(x) &\\leq 6 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the range of $g$ is $0\\leq g(x) \\leq 6$"}]}]},{"name":"Number & Algebra","subtopics":[{"name":"Exponents & Logarithms","questions":[{"ref":"AA029","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA029\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve $\\log_6(x) + \\log_6(x-5) = 2$, for $x > 5$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_6(x) + \\log_6(x-5) &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_6(x(x-5)) &= \\log_6 6^2 \\\\[5pt]\n\\log_6 (x^2-5x) &= \\log_6 36 \\\\[5pt]\nx^2-5x &= 36 \\\\[5pt]\nx^2 - 5x - 36 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(x + 4)(x - 9) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nx - 9 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x + 4 > 0$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = 9 \\hspace{1.6em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA031","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA031\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_3 x - \\log_3 5 = 1 + \\log_3 4$ for $x$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_3 x - \\log_3 5 &= 1 + \\log_3 4 \\\\[10pt]\n\\log_3 x - \\log_3 5 &= \\log_3 3 + \\log_3 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_3\\dfrac{x}{5} &= \\log_3 (3\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot4)  \\\\[5pt]\n\\log_3\\dfrac{x}{5} &= \\log_3 12 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x}{5} &= 12 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.55em}x = 60\n$$"},{"ref":"AA032","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA032\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_5 x - \\log_5 4 = 2 + \\log_5 3$ for $x$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_5 x - \\log_5 4 &= 2 + \\log_5 3 \\\\[10pt]\n\\log_5 x - \\log_5 4 &= \\log_5 5^2 + \\log_5 3 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_5\\dfrac{x}{4} &= \\log_5 (5^2\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot3)  \\\\[5pt]\n\\log_5\\dfrac{x}{4} &= \\log_5 75 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x}{4} &= 75 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.55em}x = 300\n$$"},{"ref":"AA034","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA034\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider\n$a = \\log_{63}64\\times\\log_{62}63\\times\\log_{61}62\\times\\dots\\times\\log_{2}3$.\nGiven that $a\\in\\mathbb{Z}$, find the value of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\log_{63}64\\times\\log_{62}63\\times\\log_{61}62\\times\\dots\\times\\log_{2}3 \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\dfrac{\\log64}{\\cancel{\\log63}}\\times\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\log63}}{\\cancel{\\log62}}\\times\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\log62}}{\\cancel{\\log61}}\\times\\cdots\\times\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\log3}}{\\log2} \\\\[6pt]\n&=  \\dfrac{\\log64}{\\log2} \\\\[6pt]\n&=  \\dfrac{\\log2^6}{\\log2} \\\\[6pt]\n&=  \\dfrac{6\\hspace{0.05em}\\cancel{\\log2}}{\\cancel{\\log2}} \\\\[12pt]\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA035","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA035\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) = 1 + \\log_2(x-1)$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) &= 1 + \\log_2(x-1) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) &= \\log_2 2 + \\log_2(x-1) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) &= \\log_2(2x-2) \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-2x+1 &= 2x-2 \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-4x+3 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(x-1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\nx-3 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{$x\\neq1$, since $\\log_20$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = 3 \\hspace{3.15em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA036","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA036\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) = 1 + \\log_3(x-2)$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) &= 1 + \\log_3(x-2) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) &= \\log_3 3 + \\log_3(x-2) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) &= \\log_3(3x-6) \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-4x+4 &= 3x-6 \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-7x+10 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(x-2)(x-5) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\nx-5 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x - 2 > 0$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx=5 \\hspace{3.15em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA037","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA037\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\log_a 2 = 5$.\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $\\log_a 32$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact value of $\\log_{\\sqrt{a}} 2$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $a$, giving your answer correct to $3$ significant\n    figures. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_a 32 &= \\log_a 2^5 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5 \\log_a 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{\\sqrt{a}} 2 &= \\dfrac{\\log 2}{\\log \\sqrt{a}} \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[2pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log 2}{\\frac{1}{2}\\log a} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_a 2 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2(5) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the definition of logarithm, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_a 2 &= 5 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    a^5 &= 2 \\\\[4pt]\n    a &= 2^\\frac{1}{5} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    a \\approx 1.15 \\hspace{0.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA038","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA038\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\log_b 3 = 10$.\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $\\log_b 81$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact value of $\\log_{b^2} 3$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $b$, giving your answer correct to $3$ significant\n    figures. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_b 81 &= \\log_b 3^4 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4 \\log_b 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4(10) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{b^2} 3 &= \\dfrac{\\log 3}{\\log b^2} \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log 3}{2\\log b} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_b 3 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(10) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the definition of logarithm, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_b 3 &= 10 \\hspace{3.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    b^{10} &= 3 \\\\[4pt]\n    b &= 3^\\frac{1}{10} \\\\[4pt]\n    &=1.116...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b \\approx 1.12 \\hspace{0.6em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA039","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA039\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $15^{4a} = 81^{a+2}$ for $a$. Express your answer in\nterms of $\\ln 3$ and $\\ln 5$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15^{4a} &= 81^{a+2} \\\\[10pt]\n(3\\cdot5)^{4a} &= 3^{4(a+2)} \\\\[9pt]\n\\cancel{3^{4a}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot5^{4a} &= \\cancel{3^{4a}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot3^8 \\\\[10pt]\n5^{4a} &= 3^8 \\\\[10pt]\n\\ln 5^{4a} &= \\ln 3^8 \\\\[10pt]\n4a \\ln 5 &= 8\\ln 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.25em}a = \\dfrac{2\\ln 3}{\\ln 5}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA040","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA040\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $14^{6x} = 64^{x+3}$ for $x$. Express your answer in\nterms of $\\ln 2$ and $\\ln 7$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14^{6x} &= 64^{x+3} \\\\[10pt]\n(2\\cdot7)^{6x} &= 2^{6(x+3)} \\\\[9pt]\n\\cancel{2^{6x}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot7^{6x} &= \\cancel{2^{6x}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot2^{18} \\\\[10pt]\n7^{6x} &= 2^{18} \\\\[10pt]\n\\ln 7^{6x} &= \\ln 2^{18} \\\\[10pt]\n6x\\ln 7 &= 18\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{1.9em}x = \\dfrac{3\\ln 2}{\\ln 7}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA041","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA041\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the values of $x$ when\n$25^{x^2-2x} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{125}\\right)^{4x+2}$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25^{x^2-2x} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{125}\\right)^{4x+2} \\\\[3pt]\n\\left(5^2\\right)^{x^2-2x} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5^3}\\right)^{4x+2} \\\\[9pt]\n5^{2x^2-4x} &= 5^{-12x-6} \\\\[16pt]\n2x^2 - 4x &= -12x-6 \\\\[16pt]\nx^2 - 2x &= -6x - 3 \\\\[16pt]\nx^2 + 4x + 3 &= 0 \\\\[17pt]\n(x+3)(x+1) &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3.45em}x = -3,-1\n$$"},{"ref":"AA042","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA042\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the value(s) of $x$ when\n$27^{x+2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right)^{2x+4}$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27^{x+2} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right)^{2x+4} \\\\[3pt]\n\\left(3^3\\right)^{x+2} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3^2}\\right)^{2x+4} \\\\[9pt]\n3^{3x+6} &= 3^{-4x-8} \\\\[16pt]\n3x+6 &= -4x-8 \\\\[16pt]\n7x &= -14 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = -2 \\hspace{0.5em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA045","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA045\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the integer values of $a$ and $b$ for which\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b\\log_4 7 + 60\\log_{8} 14 &= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b\\log_4 7 + 60\\log_{8} 14 &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\na + \\dfrac{b\\ln 7}{2\\ln 2} + \\dfrac{60\\ln 14}{3\\ln 2} &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\na\\ln 2 + \\dfrac{b}{2}\\ln 7 + 20\\ln (2\\cdot7) &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\na\\ln 2 + \\dfrac{b}{2}\\ln 7 + 20(\\ln 2 + \\ln 7) &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n(a + 20)\\ln 2 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{b}{2}+20\\bigg)\\ln 7 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n $a = -20$\nand  $b = -40$"},{"ref":"AA046","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA046\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the value of each of the following, giving your answer as an\ninteger.\n\n1.  $\\log_6 6$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_6 9 + \\log_6 4$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_6 72 - \\log_6 2$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\log_6 6 = 1$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_6 9 + \\log_6 4 &= \\log_6 (9\\cdot4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\log_6 36 \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\log_6 \\big(6^2\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_6 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_6 72 - \\log_6 2 &= \\log_6 \\bigg(\\dfrac{72}{2}\\bigg) \\hspace{0.15em} \\\\[5pt]\n     &= \\log_6 36 \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA047","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA047\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nFind the value of each of the following, giving your answer as an\ninteger.\n\n1.  $\\log_{10} 100$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_{10} 50 + \\log_{10} 2$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_{10} 4 - \\log_{10} 40$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.65em} \\log_{10} 100 &= \\log_{10} \\big(10^2\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_{10} 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{10} 50 + \\log_{10} 2 &= \\log_{10} (50\\cdot2) \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\log_{10} 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_{10} 4 - \\log_{10} 40 &= \\log_{10} \\bigg(\\dfrac{4}{40}\\bigg) \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[3pt]\n     &= \\log_{10} \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{10}\\bigg) \\\\[9pt]\n     &= \\log_{10} \\big(10^{-1}\\big) \\\\[16pt]\n     &= (-1) \\log_{10} 10 \\\\[9pt]\n     &= -1\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA048","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA048\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $p=\\ln 2$ and $q = \\ln 6$. Write down the following expressions in\nterms of $p$ and $q$.\n\n1.  $\\ln 12$ [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\ln 3$ [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\ln 48$ [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of natural logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln 12 &= \\ln (2\\cdot6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ln 2 + \\ln 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= p + q\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of natural logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\ln 3 &= \\ln \\bigg(\\dfrac{6}{2}\\bigg) \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\ln 6 - \\ln 2 \\\\[10pt]\n    &= q - p\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of natural logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.6em} \\ln 48 &= \\ln \\big(2^3\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot6\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n     &= 3\\ln2 +  \\ln 6 \\\\[6pt]\n     &= 3p + q\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA050","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA050\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $\\log_2 a = p$, $\\log_2 b = q$, $\\log_2 c = r$. Write down the\nfollowing expressions in terms of $p$, $q$ and $r$.\n\n1.  $\\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{ab}{c}\\Big)$ [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{a^2c}{b^3}\\Big)$\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_a b$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{ab}{c}\\Big) &= \\log_2 (ab) - \\log_2 c \\\\[6pt]\n     &= (\\log_2 a + \\log_2 b) - \\log_2 c  \\\\[10pt]\n     &= p + q - r\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.9em} \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{a^2c}{b^3}\\Big) &= \\log_2 \\big(a^2c\\big) - 3\\log_2 b \\\\[6pt]\n     &= (2\\log_2 a + \\log_2 c) - 3\\log_2 b \\\\[10pt]\n     &= 2p + r - 3q\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_a b &= \\dfrac{\\log_2 b}{\\log_2 a} \\hspace{2em}  \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{q}{p}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA052","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA052\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write down the value of\n    1.  $\\log_2 8$;\n\n    2.  $\\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big)$;\n    3.  $\\log_9 3$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence solve\n    $\\log_2 8 + \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big) + \\log_9 3 = \\log_{16} x$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_2 8 &= \\log_2 \\big(2^3\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\\log_2 2 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big) &= \\log_5\\big(5^{-2}\\big) \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (-2)\\log_5 5 \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_9 3 &= \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 9} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{\\ln \\big(3^2\\big)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{2\\ln 3} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_2 8 + \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big) + \\log_9 3 &= \\log_{16} x \\hspace{6.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3 + (-2) + \\dfrac{1}{2} &= \\log_{16} x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2} &= \\log_{16} x \\\\[10pt]\n    16^{3/2} &= x \\\\[14pt]\n    4^3 & = x \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $$\n     \\hspace{2.7em} x = 64\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA053","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA053\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write down the value of\n    1.  $\\log_3 81$;\n\n    2.  $\\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big)$;\n    3.  $\\log_{25} 5$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence solve\n    $\\log_3 81 + \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big) + \\log_{25} 5 = \\log_{9} x$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_3 81 &= \\log_3\\big(3^4\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\\log_3 3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big) &= \\log_2\\big(2^{-3}\\big) \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (-3)\\log_2 2 \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_{25} 5 &= \\dfrac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 25} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 5}{\\ln \\big(5^2\\big)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 5}{2\\ln 5} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_3 81 + \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big) + \\log_{25} 5 &= \\log_{9} x \\hspace{7.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    4 + (-3) + \\dfrac{1}{2} &= \\log_{9} x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2} &= \\log_{9} x \\\\[10pt]\n    9^{3/2} &= x \\\\[14pt]\n    3^3 & = x \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $$\n     \\hspace{2.4em} x = 27\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA055","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA055\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the value of\n\n1.  $\\log_7 98 - \\log_7 2$; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $49^{\\log_7 6}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_7 98 - \\log_7 2 &= \\log_7\\Big(\\dfrac{98}{2}\\Big) \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\log_7 49 \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\log_7\\big(7^2\\big) \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2 \\log_7 7 \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    49^{\\log_7 6} &= \\big(7^2\\big)^{\\log_7 6} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 7^{2\\log_7 6} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7^{\\log_7 (6^2)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 7^{\\log_7 36} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 36\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA056","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA056\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the value of\n\n1.  $\\log_5 75 - \\log_5 3$; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $25^{\\log_5 8}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_5 75 - \\log_5 3 &= \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{75}{3}\\Big) \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\log_5 25 \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\log_5\\big(5^2\\big) \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2 \\log_5 5 \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    25^{\\log_5 8} &= \\big(5^2\\big)^{\\log_5 8} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5^{2\\log_5 8} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5^{\\log_5 (8^2)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 5^{\\log_5 64} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 64\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1037","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1037\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe arithmetic sequence has the sixth term of $u_6=\\ln{2}$ and the eighth term of $u_8=\\frac{3}{2}\\ln{4}$.\n\n1.  1.  Show the common difference is $\\ln2$.\n\n    2.  Find $u_1$.\n\n    3.  Hence, show that the sum of $n$ terms is given by $S_n=\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}} $. Where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$. [5 marks]\n\nA new function, $f(x)$, is formed using $S_n$ with the discrete variable $n$ being replaced with the continuous variable $x$, such that $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Hence $f(x)=\\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n2.  1.  Write down the equation of the axis of symmetry of $f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Sketch $f(x)$ on the grid below, identify the axis of symmetry and any axis intercepts.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/q1037.png\" alt=\"q1037\">\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the minimum value of $S_n$ in the form $a\\ln b$. Where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ [5 marks]\n\nConsider the linear function $g(x)=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$.\n\n3.  1.  Solve $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, solve the inequality $f(x)<g(x)$.\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the range of values of $n$ that satisfy $S_n<g(x)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As the sequence is arithmetic we can write $u_8=u_6+2d$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\tfrac{3}{2}\\ln{4}&=\\ln{2}+2d \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            2d&=3\\ln{(\\sqrt{4})}-\\ln2\\\\\n            2d&=2\\ln{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\ln2}\\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We can use $u_6$ to find the first term\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            u_6&=u_1+5d\\\\\n            \\ln2&=u_1+5\\ln2\\\\\n            u_1&=\\hspace{0.525em}-4\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(2\\times -4 \\ln2 +(n-1)\\times \\ln2\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            &=\\frac{n^2}{2}\\ln2-\\frac{9n}{2}\\ln2\\\\\n            &=\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\left(n^2-9n\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln{\\sqrt{2}} \\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  1.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x&=-\\frac{-9\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}\\\\\n            &\\hspace{-0.525em}x=\\tfrac{9}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  Here is the sketch of $f(x)$ with the desired features identified.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/a1037-1.png\" alt=\"a1037-1\">\n\n        $\\text{Correct intercepts}$ **A1** and $\\text{axis of symmetry}$ **A1**.\n\n    3.  Using the symmetry of $f(x)$ we can identify the minimum value(s) of $f(x)$ which has integer $x$ coordinates.\n\n        Both points are equidistant from the axis of symmetry and they represent the minimum value of $S_n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/a1037-2.png\" alt=\"a1037-2\">\n\n        We can use the $x-$value from the graph to pick either $n=4$ or $n=5$ as both will yield the same minimum value. This solution will pick $n=4$, hence, the minimum value of $S_n$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_4&=\\left(4^2-9(4)\\right)\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\\n            &=-20\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}-10\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1.  Equate the RHS of each function\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}&=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Cancel out the factor $\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$ and solve for $x$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x^2-9x&=6-3x\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n            0&=x^2-6x-6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can solve this by completing the square.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (x-3)^2-9-6&=0\\\\\n            x-3&=\\pm\\sqrt{15}\\\\\n            x&=\\hspace{0.525em}3\\pm\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  By making a rough sketch of $g(x)$ on the graph we can solve the inequality\\\\\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/a1037-3.png\" alt=\"a1037-3\">\n\n        We can identify the region the $f(x)<g(x)$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\hspace{0.525em}3-\\sqrt{15}<x<3+\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  $n$ must be a positive integer and lie entirely within the region identified in part (c)(ii).\n\n        We know that $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}$, hence we can identify the minimum and maximum values of $n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        The minimum value must be an integer larger than $3-\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -1<3-\\sqrt{15}<0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The minimum value of $n$ is $1$.\n\n        Similarly, the maximum value must be an integer smaller than $3+\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            6<3+\\sqrt{15}<7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The maximum value of $n$ is $6$.\n\n        Hence\n        $1\\leq n\\leq 6$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA1047","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the range of possible values for $p$ such that $$e^{2x}+2\\ln p = 4e^x$$ has at least one real solution.","markScheme":"The equation is a hidden quadratic. Let $k=e^x$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^2 - 4k + 2 \\ln p &= 0  \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the discriminant we can say a quadratic equation has at least one real solution if $ b^2-4ac\\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, we have at least one real solution for $k$ if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-4)^2-4(2\\ln p) &\\geq 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[7pt]\n16-8\\ln p &\\geq 0 \\\\[7pt]\n8\\ln p &\\leq 16  \\\\[7pt]\n\\ln p &\\leq 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-2.33em}\\boxed{0 < p \\leq e^2} \\hspace{3em} \\text{($\\ln p > 0$)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\\\\[-11pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1179","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1179\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the following equation for $a$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=a}^{a+35} \\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"First we will unpack the sigma notation.\n\nThe smallest value of $k$ is $\\textcolor{red}{a}$. This value is the increased by $1$ for each subsequent term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=a}^{a+35} \\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)&=\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a}}\\right)+\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+1}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+1}}\\right)+\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{1.2em}\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+2}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+2}}\\right)+...\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis continues until we reach the largest value of $k$ which is $a+35$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    +...\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+34}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+34}}\\right)+\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+35}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+35}}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we simplify the fractions and then apply the following log law\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{a}\\tfrac{x}{y}&=\\log_{a}x-\\log_{a}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=a}^{a+35} \\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)&=\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{a+1}{a}\\right)+\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{a+2}{a+1}\\right)+...\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{a+36}{a+35}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+1)}}-\\textcolor{blue}{\\log_{13}{a}}+\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+2)}}-\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+1)}}+\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{1.2em}-\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+2)}}+...+\\textcolor{blue}{\\log_{13}{(a+36)}}-\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+35)}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice how the terms cancel each other out, leaving only the two $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$ terms. Recall, from the question, this is equal to $1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    -\\log_{13}{a}+\\log_{13}{(a+36)}&=1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can now solve the equation by rewriting the L.H.S. as a single logarithm\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{13}{\\left(\\frac{a+36}{a}\\right)}&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the definition of a logarithm, we rewrite the equation into exponential form\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{a+36}{a}&=13^1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow we can solve for $a$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    a+36&=13a\\\\[6pt]\n    12a&=36\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}a=3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA121","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA121\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $a = \\log_5b$, where $b > 0$. Write down each of the following\nexpressions\\\nin terms of $a$.\n\n1.  $\\log_5b^4$ [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_5 (25b)$ [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_{25}b$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_5b^4 &= 4\\log_5b \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4a\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} \\log_5 (25b) &= \\log_5 \\left(5^2\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot b\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_5 5 + \\log_5 b \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2 + a\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.3em} \\log_{25}b &= \\dfrac{\\log_5 b}{\\log_5 25} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{\\log_5 b}{2\\log_5 5} \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{a}{2}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA1291","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1291\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Show that $12\\log_x{2}=\\dfrac{12}{\\log_2{x}}$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Hence solve the equation $\\log_2{x}=8-12\\log_x{2}$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the change of base formula we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{L.H.S.}&=12\\log_{x}2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=12\\left(\\frac{\\log_22}{\\log_2x}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    As $\\log_n{n}=1$, we can replace $\\log_2{2}$ with 1 and simplify\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=12\\left(\\frac{1}{\\log_2x}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{12}{\\log_2x}\\hspace{1em}\\text{ ...as required}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We can replace $12\\log_x2$ with the R.H.S. of the identity from part (a)\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_2x&=8-12\\log_x2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\log_2x&=8-\\frac{12}{\\log_2x}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We now multiply each side by $\\log_2x$ and then rearrange such that the R.H.S. is equal to $0$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    (\\log_2x)^2&=8\\log_2x-12\\\\[6pt]\n\n    (\\log_2x)^2-8\\log_2x+12&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This is a hidden quadratic equation.  If we replace $\\log_2$ with a variable, say $a$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a^2-8a+12&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Let's solve this by factorising.  \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    (a-2)(a-6)&=0    \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the solutions are\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a-2&=0&\\text{and}&&a-6=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a&=2&&& a=6\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall $a=\\log_2x$, therefore the solutions become\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_2x&=2&&&\\log_2x=6\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Converting each to exponential form, we get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x&=2^2&&&x=2^6\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}x=4&\\hspace{0.3em}&&x=64\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1315","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1315\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nIt is given that $\\log_k{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}=\\dfrac{1}{5}$, where $k>0$.\n\nFind the value of\n\n1.    $\\log_k{16}$; [2 marks]\n\n2.    $\\log_{k^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{16}\\right)}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Let's begin by looking at the given relationship and expression\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_k{\\left(\\textcolor{red}{\\dfrac{1}{4}}\\right)}&=\\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_k{\\textcolor{red}{16}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Notice that $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $16$ share the same base of $4$, this means we can rewrite $\\log_k{16}$ in the terms of $\\log_k{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}$.\n\n    \n\n    To do that we first write down\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    16&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{a}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    where $a$ is to be determined.\n\n    Using index laws we can simplify the L.H.S. and the R.H.S. of above to get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    4^2&=4^{-a}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $a=-2$.\n\n    Using that we can rewrite the given expression in terms of $\\log_k{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_k{16}&= \\log_k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{-2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the logarithmic power law we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &= -2\\log_k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=-2 \\times \\frac{1}{5}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Note that the base of the expression is now $k^2$, hence we need to use the change of base formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_{a}x&=\\frac{\\log_{b}x}{\\log_{b}a}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    to change the base to $k$ so it matches the given relation\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_{k^2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}&=\\frac{\\log_{k}\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}{\\log_{k}k^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that $\\frac{1}{16}=16^{-1}$ and $\\log_{k}k^2=2$ hence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\frac{\\log_{k}\\left(16\\right)^{-1}}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the logarithmic power law and our answer from part (a) we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{k}\\left(16\\right)\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\times -\\frac{2}{5}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{1}{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA143","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA143\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $9^x + 2\\cdot3^{x+1} = 1$.","markScheme":"If we substitute $y = 3^x$ in the equation $9^x + 2\\cdot3^{x+1} = 1$, we\nhave\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(3^x\\right)^2 + 6\\cdot3^x - 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\ny^2 + 6y - 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the quadratic formula, we\nget\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{13em} y &= \\dfrac{-6 + \\sqrt{6^2 - 4(1)(-1)}}{2(1)} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $y = 3^x > 0$}] \\\\[4pt]\n&= \\dfrac{-6 + 2\\sqrt{10}}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n&= -3 + \\sqrt{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the properties of\nlogarithms, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^x &= -3 + \\sqrt{10} \\hspace{2.6em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.3em} x = \\log_3(-3 + \\sqrt{10})\n$$"},{"ref":"AA193","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA193\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider $f(x) = \\log_k(8x-2x^2)$, for $0 < x < 4$, where $k > 0$.\n\nThe equation $f(x) = 3$ has exactly one solution. Find the value of $k$.","markScheme":"If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = 3$ in the form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$,\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} \\log_k(8x-2x^2) &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n8x-2x^2 &= k^3 \\\\[6pt]\n0 &= 2x^2 - 8x + k^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the quadratic function\n$q(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + k^3$ has two equal roots.\n\nTherefore we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.1em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(-8)^2 - 4(2)(k^3) &= 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n64 - 8k^3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n8 - k^3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^3 &= 8 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3.3em} k=2\n$$"},{"ref":"AA284","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA284\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve $\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\sin x) - \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos x) = 1$, for\n$0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\" alt=\"eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\">\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms and the right triangle above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.1em} \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\sin x) - \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos x) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\Big) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\tan x) &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\\tan x &= \\sqrt{3} \\\\[10pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi \\hspace{2.3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\\\[6pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA299","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA299\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Show that $\\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) = \\log_4 \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve\n    $\\log_4(\\sqrt{10}\\cos x) = \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7)$, for\n    $0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) &= \\dfrac{\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7)}{\\log_4 16} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7)}{2\\log_4 4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\log_4 \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\hspace{2.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle formula for cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.75em} \\log_4(\\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x) &= \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) \\\\[14pt]\n    \\log_4(\\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x) &= \\log_4 \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{3em} \\\\[14pt]\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\\\[14pt]\n    10\\cos^2 x &= \\cos 2x + 7 \\\\[14pt]\n    10\\cos^2 x &= (2\\cos^2x - 1) + 7  \\\\[14pt]\n    8\\cos^2 x &= 6 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\cos x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\hspace{1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA646","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA646\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Show that $\\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} = \\log_4 (3-\\cos 2x)$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve\n    $\\log_4 (3\\sin x) \\hspace{0.15em}+\\hspace{0.15em} \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}$,\n    for\n    $0 \\hspace{-0.05em}<\\hspace{-0.05em} x \\hspace{-0.05em}<\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} &= \\dfrac{\\log_4 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}}{\\log_4 2} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log_4 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}}{\\frac{1}{2}\\log_4 4} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2 \\log_4 \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\log_4 4 = 1$}] \\\\[18pt]\n    &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\dfrac{1}{4} &= \\textstyle \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{3.5em} \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\log_4 4 &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    \\textstyle \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\log_4\\sqrt{2} &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{since $\\textstyle 4^{1/4} = \\sqrt{2}$}\\hspace{0.05em}] \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle \\log_4 (3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x)\\hspace{-0.1em} &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 3-\\cos 2x  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $0 < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 3 - (1 - 2\\sin^2 x) \\hspace{1.3em} [\\text{by double angle identity}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 2 + 2\\sin^2 x \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle 2\\sin^2 x - 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + 2 &= 0 \\hspace{2.1em} [\\text{rearranging terms to form quadratic}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2} \\pm \\sqrt{18-4\\cdot2\\cdot2}}{4} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\sin x \\neq \\sqrt{2}$}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{2}$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA751","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA751\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write the expression $4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8$ in the form of $\\ln k$, where\n    $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve $4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8 = -\\ln (2x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8 &= \\ln \\big(2^4\\big) - \\ln 8 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\ln 16 - \\ln 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ln\\Big(\\dfrac{16}{8}\\Big) \\\\[9pt]\n    & = \\ln 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Replacing the left hand side of the equation with $\\ln 2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8 &= -\\ln (2x) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\ln 2  &= -\\ln(2x) \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[12pt]\n    -\\ln 2 &= \\ln(2x) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\ln \\big(2^{-1}\\big) &= \\ln(2x) \\\\[12pt]\n    2x &= 2^{-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    2x &= \\dfrac 1 2 \\\\[13pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em} x = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA820","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA820\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nSolve the simultaneous equations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 2\\log_5 x &= \\log_5 7y \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_7 (6x-1) &= 1 + \\log_7 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we rewrite the given equations as\nfollows.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_5 5 + \\log_5 x^2 &= \\log_5 7y \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_5 5x^2 &= \\log_5 7y \\\\[6pt]\n5x^2 &= 7y \\hspace{5em}{(1)} \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_7 (6x-1) &= 1 + \\log_7 y \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{2.75em} \\log_7 (6x-1) &= \\log_7 7 + \\log_7 y \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_7 (6x-1) &= \\log_7 7y \\\\[6pt]\n6x - 1 &= 7y \\hspace{5em}{(2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the equations (1) and (2),\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^2 &= 6x - 1 \\\\[6pt]\n5x^2 - 6x + 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(5x - 1)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{1}{5} \\text{ or } x = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = \\dfrac{1}{5}$ into the equation (1), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)^2 &= 7y \\hspace{2.5em}\\\\[12pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{5} &= 7y \\\\[12pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{1}{35}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting $x = 1$ into the\nequation (1), we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(1)^2  &= 7y \\\\[12pt]\n5 &= 7y \\\\[12pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\hspace{-2.15em}x = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\, y = \\dfrac{1}{35} \\\\[12pt]\n& \\hspace{-1.9em}x = 1, \\,\\, y = \\dfrac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA984","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA984\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe equation $e^{2x} - 12e^x = -32$ has two solutions, $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\n1. Find the value of $x_1$ and the value of $x_2$.[5 marks]\n\nA second equation, $2\\left(\\log_{\\,9}x\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 6\\log_{\\,9}x - 2\\log_{\\,3}x = -6$, also has two solutions, $x_3$ and $x_4$. \n\n2. 1.  Show that this second equation can be expressed as\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right)^2 -5\\log_{\\,3}x + 6 = 0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. Hence find the value of $x_3$ and the value of $x_4$. [7 marks]\n\n3. Given that $x_1 + x_2 = a(x_3 + x_4)$, find the value of $a$. Give your answer in the form $b\\ln c$, where $b,c \\in \\mathbb{R}$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The equation can be written as a quadratic in $e^x$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(e^x\\right)^2 - 12e^x + 32 = 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The equation can then be factorised:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(e^x - 4\\right)\\left(e^x - 8\\right) = 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    From the first bracket, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    e^x - 4 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^x &= 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    x &= \\ln 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 2\\ln 2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    And from the second bracket,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    e^x - 8 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^x &= 8 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    x &= \\ln 8 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 3\\ln 2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence, $$\\boxed{\\text{$x_1 = 2\\ln 2$ and $x_2 = 3\\ln 2$.}}$$\n\n2. 1. We are asked to write the second equation in terms of $\\log_{\\,3}x$, and currently the equation is expressed in terms of $\\log_{\\,3}x$ and $\\log_{\\,9}x$. Using the formula for changing the base, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\log_{\\,9}x &= \\dfrac{\\log_{\\,3}x}{\\log_{\\,3}9} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{\\log_{\\,3}x}{\\log_{\\,3}3^2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{\\log_{\\,3}x}{2\\log_{\\,3}3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\log_{\\,9}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_{\\,3}x\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Substituting this expression into our original equation, we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2\\left(\\log_{\\,9}x\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 6\\log_{\\,9}x - 2\\log_{\\,3}x &= -6 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 6\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 2\\log_{\\,3}x &= -6 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-10em}\\boxed{\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right)^2 - 5\\log_{\\,3}x + 6 = 0}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. The equation can be factorised as follows:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left(\\log_{\\,3}x - 2\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x - 3\\right) = 0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        From the first bracket, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x - 2 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x &= 2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        x &= 9\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And from the second bracket,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x - 3 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x &= 3 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        x &= 27\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence, $$\\boxed{\\text{$x_3 = 9$ and $x_4 = 27$.}}$$\n\n3. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x_1 + x_2 &= a(x_3 + x_4) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2\\ln 2 + 3\\ln 2 &= a(9+27) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5\\ln 2 &= 36a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{a = \\dfrac{5}{36}\\ln 2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Financial and Growth Models","questions":[{"ref":"AA1053","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1053\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn the $1$st of January  $2024$ Fabian bought a new car for \\$23,000. He knows from research that the car will depreciate at a rate of $8\\%$ for every complete year.\n\n1.  Find the value ($V_f$) of Fabian's car on the $1$st of January $2030$.[3 marks]\n\nFabian's colleague Sven also bought a new car on $1$st of January $2024$. Sven knows from research what the value (in \\$) of his new car will be on the $1$st of January for the following years.\n\n$$\\\\[1em]$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/q1053.png\" alt=\"q1053\">\n\n2.  Assuming the value of Sven's car can be approximately modelled by the equation $V_s=ax+b$, find out the difference in value of Fabian and Sven's cars on $1$st of January $2030$, according to the model. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can use a geometric sequence to model the value of Fabian's car ($V_f$).\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    The common ratio, $r$, will be $1-0.08=0.92$, and the first term, $u_1$, will be $23\\,000$. There are $6$ complete years between $1$st of January $2024$ and $1$st of January $2030$. We are looking for $u_7$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_f &= u_1 \\times r^{7-1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=23\\,000\\times 0.92^6\\\\[6pt]\n        &=13\\,946.16...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$13\\,900 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can find a linear model using the G.D.C. Such that $a=-3\\,228.84...$ and $b=6\\,574\\,789.23... $, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s&=-3228.84... x+6574789.23... \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can predict the value ($V_s$) with $x=2030$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s&=-3228.84...\\times 2030+6574789.23...\\\\\n        &=20\\,231.53...\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore the difference in value of the two cars is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s-V_f&=20\\,231.53...- 13\\,946.16...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$6\\,290 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA108","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA108\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn $1$st of January $2022$, Grace invests $$\\$P$$ in an account that pays\na nominal annual interest rate of $6$ %,\n**compounded quarterly**.\n\nThe amount of money in Grace's account **at the end of each year**\nfollows a geometric sequence with common ratio, $\\alpha$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\alpha$, giving your answer to four significant\n    figures. [3 marks]\n\nGrace makes no further deposits or withdrawals from the account.\n\n2.  Find the year in which the amount of money in Grace's\n    account will become **triple** the amount she invested. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/98fa6420aaf44673cb775b3bfb7054ff59483e48.svg\" alt=\"98fa6420aaf44673cb775b3bfb7054ff59483e48.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    P\\alpha &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{4\\times 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the value of $\\alpha$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\alpha &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{400}\\bigg)^4 \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 1.061\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/22a91089c8626c046d977bf7f58b143ef817f639.svg\" alt=\"22a91089c8626c046d977bf7f58b143ef817f639.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} 3P &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{4\\times n} \\\\[5pt]\n    3 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{400}\\bigg)^{4n} \\\\[12pt]\n    n &\\approx 18.4 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence Grace will have triple the amount she invested during\n     $2040$"},{"ref":"AA109","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA109\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn Gary's $50$th birthday, he invests $$\\$P$$ in an account that pays a\nnominal annual interest rate of $5$ %,\n**compounded monthly**.\n\nThe amount of money in Gary's account **at the end of each year**\nfollows a geometric sequence with common ratio, $\\alpha$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\alpha$, giving your answer to four significant\n    figures. [3 marks]\n\nGary makes no further deposits or withdrawals from the account.\n\n2.  Find the age Gary will be when the amount of money in his\n    account will be **double** the amount he invested. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/41a3f8a185c5c4fff3e200802b215cea41fb13e6.svg\" alt=\"41a3f8a185c5c4fff3e200802b215cea41fb13e6.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    P\\alpha &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the value of $\\alpha$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\alpha &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{1200}\\bigg)^{12} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 1.051\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/3a174f6b31ae114bd45f642a60b4cf2b3ffba841.svg\" alt=\"3a174f6b31ae114bd45f642a60b4cf2b3ffba841.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} 2P &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times n} \\\\[5pt]\n    2 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[12pt]\n    n &\\approx 13.9 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence Gary will have double the amount he invested at age\n     $63$"},{"ref":"AA110","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA110\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nBill takes out a bank loan of $$\\$100\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to buy a premium\nelectric car, at an annual interest rate of\n$5.49$%. The interest is calculated at the end\nof each year and added to the amount outstanding.\n\n1.  Find the amount of money Bill would owe the bank after $10$ years.\n    Give your answer to the nearest dollar. [3 marks]\n\nTo pay off the loan, Bill makes quarterly deposits of $$\\$P$$ at the end\nof every quarter in a savings account, paying a nominal annual interest\nrate of $3.2$%. He makes his first deposit at\nthe end of the first quarter after taking out the loan.\n\n2.  Show that the total value of Bill's savings after $10$\n    years is $P\\bigg[\\dfrac{1.008^{40}-1}{1.008-1}\\bigg]$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Given that Bill's aim is to own the electric car after $10$\n    years, find the value for $P$ to the nearest dollar. [3 marks]\n\nMelinda visits a different bank and makes a single deposit of\n$$\\$\\hspace{0.05em}Q$$, the annual $\\text{interest}$ rate being\n$3.5$%.\n\n4.\n    1.  Melinda wishes to withdraw $$\\$8000$$ at the end of each year for\n        a period of $n$ years. Show that an expression for the minimum\n        value of $Q$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\dfrac{8000}{1.035} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^2} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^3} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^n}.\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the minimum value of $Q$ that would\n        permit Melinda to withdraw annual amounts of $$\\$8000$$\n        indefinitely. Give your answer to the nearest dollar.\n        [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/8d43689548a3d16e00609e684b76b38f90c0c20a.svg\" alt=\"8d43689548a3d16e00609e684b76b38f90c0c20a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 100\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5.49}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times10} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\$170\\hspace{0.15em}653 \\hspace{8em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The number of time periods in $10$ years is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    n &= (4)(10) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The total value of Bill's savings after $10$\n    years is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} &= P + P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.2}{100\\times4}\\bigg) + P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.2}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^2 + \\dots + P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.2}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{39} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= P + P(1.008) + P(1.008)^2 + \\cdots + P(1.008)^{39} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = P$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 1.008$.\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n - 1)}{r - 1}$ with $\\text{\\(n = 40\\)}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} &= S_{40} \\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}P\\bigg(\\dfrac{1.008^{40} - 1}{1.008 - 1}\\bigg) \\hspace{8em}{(2)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(1) = (2)$ for $P$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.45em} 170\\hspace{0.15em}653 &= P\\bigg(\\dfrac{1.008^{40} - 1}{1.008 - 1}\\bigg)  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    P \\approx \\$3637\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/7fa99aa4ecafd8974b214fde355c840e109f39ef.svg\" alt=\"7fa99aa4ecafd8974b214fde355c840e109f39ef.svg\">\n\n        Hence the amount of money in Melinda's account after $n$ years\n        is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= Q\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times n} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= Q(1.035)^n\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we deduce\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        Q(1.035)^n &= 8000 + 8000(1.035) + 8000(1.035)^2 + \\cdots + 8000(1.035)^{n-1} \\\\[12pt]\n        Q &= \\dfrac{8000\\big[1 + 1.035 + 1.035^2 + \\cdots + 1.035^{n-1}\\big]}{1.035^n} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        Q = \\dfrac{8000}{1.035} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^2} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^3} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^n} \\hspace{4em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have an infinite geometric sequence with the first term\n        $u_1 = \\dfrac{8000}{1.035}$ and the common ratio\n        $r = \\dfrac{1}{1.035}$.\n        Hence, using the sum of an infinite geometric\n        sequence $\\text{formula}$ $\\text{\\(S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}\\)}$,\n        we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} Q &= \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\bigg[\\dfrac{8000}{1.035}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{1.035}\\bigg]^k \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\frac{8000}{1.035}}{1 - \\frac{1}{1.035}} \\\\[15pt]\n        &= \\$228\\hspace{0.15em}571\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA117","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA117\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIt is known that the number of trees in a small forest will decrease by\n$5$ % each year unless some new trees are\nplanted. At the end of each year, $600$ new trees are planted to the\nforest. At the start of $2021$, there are $8200$ trees in the forest.\n\n1.  Show that there will be roughly $9060$ trees in the forest at the\n    start of $2026$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the approximate number of trees in the forest at the start of\n    $2041$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of trees in the forest at the start of $2022$ will be\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T}_1 &= 8200\\times0.95 + 600 \\hspace{2.8em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The number of trees in the forest at the start of $2023$ will be\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.65em} \\mathrm{T}_2 &= \\mathrm{T}_1\\times0.95 + 600 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\left(8200\\times0.95 + 600\\right)0.95 + 600 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8200\\times0.95^2 + 600(0.95 + 1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the number\n    of trees in the forest at the start of $2026$ will be\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T}_5 &= 8200\\times0.95^5 + 600\\hspace{0.05em}(0.95^4 + 0.95^3 + 0.95^2 + 0.95 + 1) \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 9060\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The approximate number of trees in the forest at the start of $2041$\n    will be\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T}_{20} &= 8200\\times0.95^{20} + 600\\bigg[\\sum_{k=0}^{19}0.95^k\\bigg] \\\\[2pt]\n    &= 8200\\times0.95^{20} + 600\\bigg[\\dfrac{1 - 0.95^{20}}{1 - 0.95}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 10\\hspace{0.15em}638\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1175","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1175\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nJuanita wants to borrow some money to buy an apartment.\n\nShe finds an offer allowing her to borrow \\$480\\,000 over $10$ years with an interest rate of $r\\%$ P.A. compounded monthly. She repays the loan with a fixed amount $p$ every month.\n\nJuanita takes the loan out at the beginning of the month. At the end of the month, the interest is added __and then__ she makes the monthly payment of $p$.\n\nThis continues until after $10$ complete years, she has repaid the loan in its entirety.\n\nJuanita wants to analyse three different scenarios in which she could repay the loan.\n\n1.  In the first scenario her monthly payment is $$p=\\,\\$5\\,000$$.\n\n    If $k=1+\\dfrac{r}{1200}$\n    1.  Write down the number of payments that will be made over the entire $10$ year term of the loan.\n\n    2.  Show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    96k^{120}&=\\frac{k^{120}-1}{k-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    3.  Hence, or otherwise, find $r$.[6 marks]\n\n2.  In the second scenario Juanita uses the same values for $p$ and $r$ as part (a).  She makes the monthly payments of $p$ for $7$ years and $4$ months.\n\n    She then makes a final payment to clear the remaining balance of the loan.\n    1.  Find the number of payments she makes __before__  the final payment.\n\n    2.  Hence, find the final payment required to clear the remaining balance to $4$ significant figures. [3 marks]\n\n3.  In the third scenario Juanita pays $p$ per month for $5$ complete years and then she increases her monthly loan repayment to $2p$ for the remaining $5$ years.\n\n    Find the value of $p$, to the nearest dollar, for the third scenario. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  There are $12$ payments per year for $10$ years\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=10 \\times 12\\\\[6pt]\n        &=120 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    2.  Note: Multiplying by $k$ represents an increase of $r\\%$.\n\n        We can build up an expression which represents the remaining value, $FV$, of the loan. After the first month, this remaining value is increased by $r\\%$ and then the payment \\$$5\\,000$ is subtracted\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=480\\,000\\times k -5\\,000\\\\[6pt]\n            &=480\\,000k -5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We then continue into the second month, this process is repeated. The remaining value is increased by $r\\%$ and the payment \\$$5\\,000$ is subtracted\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=(480\\,000k -5\\,000)\\times k -5\\,000\\\\[6pt]\n            &=480\\,000k^2 -5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Similarly for the third month\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=(480\\,000k^2 -5\\,000k-5\\,000)\\times k -5\\,000\\\\[6pt]\n            &=480\\,000k^3 -5\\,000k^2-5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        This continues for all $120$ payments and can be shown using the following series\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=480\\,000k^{120} -5\\,000k^{119}-5\\,000k^{118}-5\\,000k^{117}-...-5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        As the loan has been repaid in full we can replace $FV$ with $0$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            0&=480\\,000k^{120} -5\\,000k^{119}-5\\,000k^{118}-5\\,000k^{117}-...-5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can then rearrange the equation above to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            480\\,000k^{120}&=5\\,000k^{119}+5\\,000k^{118}+5\\,000k^{117}+...+5\\,000k+5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Recognise that the R.H.S. of the above is a geometric series with a first term of $5\\,000$ and a common ratio of $k$. Hence we can use the sum of a geometric sequence formula to write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            480\\,000k^{120}&=5\\,000\\left(\\frac{k^{120}-1}{k-1}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n            &\\hspace{-2.8em}96k^{120}=\\dfrac{k^{120}-1}{k-1}\\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Using a G.D.C. we can solve the equation found above.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                96k^{120}&=\\frac{k^{120}-1}{k-1} \\\\[6pt]\n                k&=1.0038412... \\hspace{3.3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                1.0038412...&=1+\\tfrac{r}{1200} \\\\[6pt]\n                r&=4.609419...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n                &=4.61\\% \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  1.  She makes $7$ full years of payments plus an additional $4$ months, which gives\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                &=7 \\times 12 + 4\\\\[6pt]\n                &=88\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $\\text{She makes }88\\text{ payments before the final payment.}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  You could use the Financial Solver on the G.D.C. with the following values\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/AA1175_table.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1175_table\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Solving for $FV$ gives a remaining value of $150\\,286.301...$.\n\n        Hence the final payment is $$\\boxed{\\$150\\,300}$$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Juanita pays $p$ per month for $60$ months, she then pays $2p$ per month for the remaining $60$ months.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Lets consider the first $60$ payments of  $p$ using a similar method to part (a) where $k=1+\\tfrac{4.61}{1200} = 1.0038...$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        FV&=((480\\,000 \\times k - p)\\times k - p)...\\\\[6pt]\n        FV&=480\\,000k^{60}-pk^{59}-pk^{58}...-pk-p \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now let's consider the next $60$ payments of $2p$ which will clear the remaining balance, which means the future value is \\$$0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=((480\\,000k^{60}-pk^{59}-pk^{58}...-p)\\times k - 2p)\\times k - 2p)...\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=480\\,000k^{120}-pk^{119}...-pk^{60}-2pk^{59}-2pk^{58}-...-2pk-2p\\\\[6pt]\n        480\\,000k^{120}&=pk^{119}...+pk^{60}+2pk^{59}+2pk^{58}+...+2pk+2p \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    This equation has a single unknown ($p$). However in its current form we cannot enter it into the calculator as there are too many terms!\n\n    We need to find a way to rewrite the sum in a simpler form.\n\n    We can do this by summing the RHS of the equation in two parts. We will let the first $60$ terms be part $A$ and the last $60$ terms be part $B$\n\n    To find the sum of part $A$ we can use the formula directly, pasrt $A$ has parameters $u_1=2p$, $r=1.0038...$ and $n=60$, therefore\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_A&=2p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's reconsider the original equation and replace part $A$ with the simpler form found above\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        480\\,000k^{120}&=\\underbrace{pk^{119}...+pk^{60}}_{\\mathrm{B}}+\\underbrace{2pk^{59}+2pk^{58}+...+2pk+2p}_{\\mathrm{A}}\\\\[6pt]\n        480\\,000k^{120}&=\\underbrace{pk^{119}...+pk^{60}}_{\\mathrm{B}}+\\underbrace{2p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1}}_{\\mathrm{A}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Part $B$ is more complicated. We can't simply sum $120$ terms as we only want to sum the last $60$ terms of the sequence.\n\n    Lets find the sum of the full $120$ terms and then subtract the sum of the first $60$ terms.\n\n    Part $B$ has parameters $u_1=p$, $r=1.0038...$ and $n=120$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_B&=p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{120}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} - p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's now rewrite the original equation and replace $S_B$ with the formula above\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        480\\,000(1.0038...)^{120}&=\\underbrace{p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{120}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} - p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1}}_{\\mathrm{B}}+\\underbrace{2p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1}}_{\\mathrm{A}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now solve this on the G.D.C.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        p&=3465.61...\\\\[6pt]\n        p&=\\$3466 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1332","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1332\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn $1$st January $2021$, Nyla buys a yacht.\n\nThe price of the yacht depreciates at $7\\%$ per annum.\n\nOn $1$st January $2025$, she sells the yacht for \\$23\\,564 .\n\n1.    Find the price of the yacht when Nyla bought it. [3 marks]\n\nNyla wants to buy a speedboat.\n\nThe speedboat is priced at \\$45\\,000 on $1$st January $2021$, and it depreciates at $0.2\\%$ per month.\n\nOn $1$st June $2025$, Nyla buys the speedboat.\n\nIn addition to the money she received for selling the yacht she was required to add \\$$K$ to buy the speedboat.\n\n2.    Find the value of $K$ to the nearest dollar. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can determine the price of the boat by using the compound depreciation formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}&=\\mathrm{PV}\\left(1-\\frac{r}{100}\\right)^{n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}&=\\text{ Future Value}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{PV}&=\\text{ Present Value}\\\\[6pt]\n    r&=\\text{ annual rate of depreciation, as a percentage}\\\\[6pt]\n    n&=\\text{ number of compounding periods}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In this case we are told the future value and are expected to find the present value.\n\n    This means we have $\\mathrm{FV}=$\\$23\\,564, $k=1$, $r=7$, and $n=4$.\n\n    Hence we can substitute these values into the formula to solve for the initial value (PV).\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    23\\,564&=\\mathrm{PV}\\times \\left(1-\\frac{7}{100}\\right)^4\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1A1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    23\\,564&=\\mathrm{PV}(0.93)^4\\\\[6pt]\n    31\\,500.4...&=\\mathrm{PV}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{PV}&=\\$31\\,500\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. We are told that Nyla will use the money from the sale of the boat and an additional amount $K$ to buy the speedboat.\n\n    Hence, letting $\\mathrm{FV}_S$ represent the future value of the speedboat we can write the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    23\\,564+K&=\\mathrm{FV}_S\\\\[6pt]\n    K&=\\mathrm{FV}_S-23\\,564\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To determine $\\mathrm{FV}_S$ at the time when Nyla buys the speedboat we can use the formula in part (a).\n\n    We know that $\\mathrm{PV}=$ \\$45\\,000 and $r=0.2$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}&=45\\,000\\left(1-\\frac{0.2}{100}\\right)^{n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The value of $n$ is the number of compounding periods so we need to determine how many months are between $1$ January $2021$ and $1$ June $2025$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    n&=4\\times12+5\\\\[6pt]\n    &=53\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting $n=53$ we can solve for the value of the speedboat.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}_S&=45\\,000\\left(1-\\frac{0.2}{100}\\right)^{53}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\$40\\,469.813...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can use this value to solve for $K$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    K&=\\mathrm{FV}_S-23\\,564\\\\[6pt]\n    &=40\\,469.813-23\\,564\\\\[6pt]\n    &=16\\,905.813...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\$16\\,906\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA630","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA630\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe Australian Koala Foundation estimates that there are about\n$45\\hspace{0.15em}000$ koalas left in the wild in $2019$. A year before,\nin $2018$, the population of koalas was estimated as\n$50\\hspace{0.15em}000$. Assuming the population of koalas continues to\ndecrease by the same percentage each year, find:\n\n1.  the exact population of koalas in $2022$; [3 marks]\n\n2.  the number of years it will take for the koala population to reduce\n    to half of its number in $2018$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have a geometric sequence with\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2018:\\hspace{0.5em} u_1 &= 50\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[6pt]\n    2019:\\hspace{0.5em} u_2 &= 45\\hspace{0.15em}000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The\n    common ratio of the sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{45\\hspace{0.15em}000}{50\\hspace{0.15em}000} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 5$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.5em} 2022:\\hspace{0.5em} u_5 &= u_1r^{5-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 50\\hspace{0.15em}000(0.9)^{5-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 32\\hspace{0.15em}805\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $u_n = 25\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $n$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r^{n-1} &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    50\\hspace{0.15em}000(0.9)^{n-1} &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.5em} n \\approx 7.58 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[2pt]\n    $$\n\n    Because our starting year, 2018, is considered to be year 1 ($n=1$), it will take 6.58 years from then for the population to halve from $50\\,000$ to $25\\,000$.\n\n    Hence it will take\n     $6.58 \\text{ years}$\n    for the koala population to reduce to half of its number in $2018$."},{"ref":"AA635","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA635\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**In this question give all answers correct to the nearest whole\nnumber.**\n\nA population of goats on an island starts at $232$. The population is\nexpected to increase by $15$ % each year.\n\n1.  Find the expected population size after:\n    1.  $10$ years;\n\n    2.  $20$ years. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the number of years it will take for the population to reach\n    $15\\hspace{0.15em}000$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/537894a1e06c34454736a91f01647c46ca2f4fa3.svg\" alt=\"537894a1e06c34454736a91f01647c46ca2f4fa3.svg\">\n\n    Hence the population size is expected to increase according to a\n    geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 232(1.15)$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 1.15$.\n\n    1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 10$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} u_{10} &= u_1r^{10-1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= [232(1.15)](1.15)^{10-1} \\\\[7pt]\n        &\\approx 939\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 20$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} u_{20} &= u_1r^{20-1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= [232(1.15)](1.15)^{20-1} \\\\[7pt]\n        &\\approx 3797\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $u_n = 15\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $n$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r^{n-1} &= 15\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.5em} [232(1.15)](1.15)^{n-1} &= 15\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[7pt]\n    n &\\approx 29.8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence it will take\n     $30 \\text{ years}$\n    for the population of goats to reach $15\\hspace{0.15em}000$."},{"ref":"AA657","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA657\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn $1$st of January $2021$, Fiona decides to take out a bank loan to\npurchase a new Tesla electric car. Fiona takes out a loan of $$\\$P$$ with\na bank that offers a nominal annual interest rate of\n$2.6\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$, **compounded monthly**.\n\nThe size of Fiona's loan **at the end of each year** follows a geometric\nsequence with common ratio, $\\alpha$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\alpha$, giving your answer to five significant\n    figures. [3 marks]\n\nThe bank lets the size of Fiona's loan increase until it becomes\n**triple** the size of the original loan. Once this happens, the bank\ndemands that Fiona pays the entire amount back to close the loan.\n\n2.  Find the year during which Fiona will need to pay back the\n    loan. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/5f2f8a1ae6bc63ae761ba90ac49b1bb0bd1bd144.svg\" alt=\"5f2f8a1ae6bc63ae761ba90ac49b1bb0bd1bd144.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we deduce\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    P\\alpha &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the value of $\\alpha$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} \\alpha &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{1200}\\bigg)^{12} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 1.0263\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/a4fdabe8dee8262e3ad5b7a5f6550e756265ae9a.svg\" alt=\"a4fdabe8dee8262e3ad5b7a5f6550e756265ae9a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.75em} 3P &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times n} \\\\[5pt]\n    3 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[12pt]\n    n &\\approx 42.3 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence Fiona will need to pay back the loan during\n     $2063$"},{"ref":"AA702","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA702\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJeremy invests $$\\$8000$$ into a savings account that pays an annual\ninterest rate of $5.5$ %, **compounded\nannually**.\n\n1.  Write down a formula which calculates that total value of the\n    investment after $n$ years. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the amount of money in the savings account after:\n    1.  $1$ year;\n\n    2.  $3$ years. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Jeremy wants to use the money to put down a $$\\$10\\hspace{0.15em}000$$\n    deposit on an apartment. Determine if Jeremy will be able to do this\n    within a $5$-year timeframe.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/eaedd006beb5ba238d9ad76a3a6f6101e049be71.svg\" alt=\"eaedd006beb5ba238d9ad76a3a6f6101e049be71.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 8000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5.5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times n} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055)^n \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Substituting $n = 1$ in the formula $(1)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{FV} &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055) \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\$8440\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Substituting $n = 3$ in the formula $(1)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{FV} &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055)^3 \\\\[8pt]\n        &=9393.9...\\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx \\$9390\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Substituting $n = 5$ in the formula $(1)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} \\mathrm{FV} &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055)^5 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx \\$10\\hspace{0.15em}456\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $\\text{Yes, Jeremy will be able to do this within a \\(5\\)-year timeframe.}$"},{"ref":"AA703","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA703\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nMaria invests $$\\$25\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ into a savings account that pays\na nominal annual interest rate of $4.25$%,\n**compounded monthly**.\n\n1.  Calculate the amount of money in the savings account after $3$\n    years. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the number of years it takes for the account to reach\n    $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/987f4c1d6bf2f65199be87193b7aea8bd2861a05.svg\" alt=\"987f4c1d6bf2f65199be87193b7aea8bd2861a05.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times3} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\$28\\hspace{0.15em}400\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/f2cb8aa057458cfd9f39f532ecc8e0e8adc7b0a4.svg\" alt=\"f2cb8aa057458cfd9f39f532ecc8e0e8adc7b0a4.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} 40\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[6pt]\n    1.6 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Sketching the graphs of $y=1.6$ and $y=\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n}$ we get:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/AA703a.PNG\" alt=\"AA703a\">\n\n    Hence it takes\n     $11.1 \\text{ years}$\n    for the account to reach $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$."},{"ref":"AA704","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA704\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**In this question give all answers correct to two decimal places.**\n\nMia deposits $4000$ Australian dollars (AUD) into a bank account. The\nbank pays a nominal annual interest rate of\n$6$ %, **compounded semi-annually**.\n\n1.  Find the amount of **interest** that Mia will earn over the next\n    $2.5$ years. [3 marks]\n\nElla also deposits AUD into a bank account. Her bank pays a nominal\nannual $\\text{interest}$ rate of $4$ %,\n**compounded monthly**. In $2.5$ years, the total amount in Ella's\naccount will be $4000$ AUD.\n\n2.  Find the amount that Ella deposits in the bank account.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/dc425b29b9e196ccc51470e8966f83917d4179b9.svg\" alt=\"dc425b29b9e196ccc51470e8966f83917d4179b9.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 4000\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{6}{100\\times2}\\bigg)^{2\\times2.5} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 4637.10 \\text{ AUD}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the amount of interest earned is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} - \\text{PV} &\\approx 4637.10 - 4000 \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 637.10 \\text{ AUD}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/bc195cd8b953f93121c2542be7b715a98cf4c62e.svg\" alt=\"bc195cd8b953f93121c2542be7b715a98cf4c62e.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} 4000 &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{4}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times2.5} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9em} \\text{PV} \\approx 3619.95 \\text{ AUD} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA705","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA705\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGreg has saved $2000$ British pounds (GBP) over the last six months. He\ndecided to deposit his savings in a bank which offers a nominal annual\ninterest rate of $\\text{\\(8\\)\\hspace{0.05em}\\%}$, **compounded\nmonthly**, for two years.\n\n1.  Calculate the total amount of interest Greg would earn over the two\n    years. Give your answer correct to **two decimal places**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nGreg would earn the same amount of interest, **compounded\nsemi-annually**, for two years if he deposits his savings in a second\nbank.\n\n2.  Calculate the nominal annual interest rate the second bank\n    offers. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/d94b9efaa433da997063a9b0927befd8e7a68df2.svg\" alt=\"d94b9efaa433da997063a9b0927befd8e7a68df2.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{8}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2345.78 \\text{ GBP}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the total amount of interest earned is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} - \\text{PV} &= 2345.78 - 2000 \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 345.78 \\text{ GBP}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/218c768ecef0bd0485a005a319e86a9adb263fd0.svg\" alt=\"218c768ecef0bd0485a005a319e86a9adb263fd0.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} 2345.78 &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100\\times2}\\bigg)^{2\\times 2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8.2em} r \\approx 8.13\\hspace{0.05em}\\% \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA706","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA706\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nEmily deposits $2000$ Australian dollars (AUD) into a bank account. The\nbank pays a nominal annual interest rate of\n$4$ %, **compounded monthly**.\n\n1.  Find the amount of money that Emily will have in her bank account\n    after $5$ years. Give your answer correct to **two decimal places**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nEmily will withdraw the money back from her bank account when the amount\nreaches $3000$ AUD.\n\n2.  Find the time, **in years and months**, until Emily withdraws the\n    money from her bank account. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/adb2e5a5727d896793f414aff36c01c8cf4261d3.svg\" alt=\"adb2e5a5727d896793f414aff36c01c8cf4261d3.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 5} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2441.99 \\text{ AUD}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/07a5668b1bebdc2755dde33ca8b99bce2a25d25a.svg\" alt=\"07a5668b1bebdc2755dde33ca8b99bce2a25d25a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} 3000 &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{3.7em} 1.5 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Sketching the graphs of $y=1.5$ and $y=\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n}$ we get:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/AA706a.PNG\" alt=\"AA706a\">\n\n    $10.15$ years $\\approx 10$ years and 2 months.\n\n    Hence Emily will withdraw the money from her bank account after\n     $10 \\text{ years and }2 \\text{ months.}$"},{"ref":"AA707","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA707\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJulia wants to buy a house that requires a deposit of\n$74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ Australian dollars (AUD).\n\nJulia is going to invest an amount of AUD in an account that pays a\nnominal annual interest rate of $5.5$ %,\n**compounded monthly**.\n\n1.  Find the amount of AUD Julia needs to invest to reach\n    $74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ AUD after $8$ years. Give your answer correct\n    to **the nearest dollar**. [3 marks]\n\nJulia's parents offer to add $5000$ AUD to her initial investment from\npart (a), however, only if she invests her money in a more reliable bank\nthat pays a nominal annual interest rate only of\n$3.5$ %, **compounded quarterly**.\n\n2.  Find the number of years it would take Julia to save the\n    $74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ AUD if she accepts her parents money and\n    follows their advice. Give your answer correct to **the nearest\n    year**. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/3787235f52ce0a9532d1fb499061fa26f27dacb9.svg\" alt=\"3787235f52ce0a9532d1fb499061fa26f27dacb9.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    74\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5.5}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 8} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.1em} \\text{PV} \\approx 47\\hspace{0.15em}707 \\text{ AUD} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/c0f56a142d7776c6858b94a3b97c9a56ff5d69a7.svg\" alt=\"c0f56a142d7776c6858b94a3b97c9a56ff5d69a7.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.6em} 74\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 52\\hspace{0.15em}707\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.5}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{4n} \\\\[8pt]\n    n &\\approx 9.74 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence it would take Julia\n     $10 \\text{ years}$\n    to save the $74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ AUD."},{"ref":"AA709","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA709\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nHannah buys a car for $$\\$24\\hspace{0.15em}900$$. The value of the car\ndepreciates by $16$ %\neach year.\n\n1.  Find the value of the car after $10$ years. [3 marks]\n\nPatrick buys a car for $$\\$12\\hspace{0.15em}000$$. The car depreciates by\na fixed percentage each year, and after $6$ years it is worth $$\\$6200$$.\n\n2.  Find the annual rate of depreciation of the car.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/848a0a2395b69bf10b5ffc75fbbed6c738b8c64e.svg\" alt=\"848a0a2395b69bf10b5ffc75fbbed6c738b8c64e.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{r}{100}\\bigg)^n \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 24\\hspace{0.25em}900\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{16}{100}\\bigg)^{10}  \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 4355.04...\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx \\$4360\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/80839ebacd99f943925e1048218639215d638a8d.svg\" alt=\"80839ebacd99f943925e1048218639215d638a8d.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.9em} 6200 &= 12\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{r}{100}\\bigg)^6  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.7em} r \\approx 10.4\\hspace{0.05em}\\% \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA710","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA710\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAli bought a car for $$\\$18\\hspace{0.15em}000$$. The value of the car\ndepreciates by $10.5$ %\neach year.\n\n1.  Find the value of the car at the end of the first year. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of the car after $4$ years. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the number of years it will take for the car to be worth\n    exactly half its original value. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/86d0641572fd7d8ca13b40e061d1374bca7391bc.svg\" alt=\"86d0641572fd7d8ca13b40e061d1374bca7391bc.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{r}{100}\\bigg)^n \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 18\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{10.5}{100}\\bigg)  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\$16\\hspace{0.15em}110 \\\\[18pt]\n    &\\approx \\$16\\hspace{0.15em}100\\hspace{2em}\\text{correct to 3 s.f.} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/8adbc1baad593f942992356c96995063b6051eee.svg\" alt=\"8adbc1baad593f942992356c96995063b6051eee.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{FV} &= 18\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{10.5}{100}\\bigg)^4  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\$11\\hspace{0.15em}549.5 \\\\[18pt]\n    &\\approx \\$11\\hspace{0.15em}500\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/22cbaefd6c6344e284fd948d4606858070563a4e.svg\" alt=\"22cbaefd6c6344e284fd948d4606858070563a4e.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    9000 &= 18\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{10.5}{100}\\bigg)^n \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8em} n \\approx 6.25 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA839","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA839\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nAlex and Julie each have a goal of saving $$\\$30\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to\nput towards a house deposit. They each have $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to\ninvest.\n\n1.  Alex chooses his local bank and invests his $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$\n    in a savings account that offers an interest rate of $5\\%$ per annum\n    compounded **annually**.\n\n    1.  Find the value of Alex's investment after $7$ years, to the\n        nearest hundred dollars.\n\n    2.  Alex reaches his goal after n years, where n is an integer.\n        Determine the value of n. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Julie chooses a different bank and invests her\n    $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ in a savings account that offers an\n    interest rate of $r\\%$ per annum compounded **monthly**, where $r$\n    is set to two decimal places.\n\n    Find the minimum value of $r$ needed for Julie to reach her goal\n    after $10$ years. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Xavier also wants to reach a savings goal of\n    $$\\$30 \\hspace{0.15em}000$$. He doesn't trust his local bank so he\n    decides to initially put his money into a safety deposit box where it does not\n    earn any interest. His system is to add more money into the safety\n    deposit box at the end of each year. At the end of each year he will add one third of the amount\n    he added at the end of the previous year.\n\n    1.  Show that Xavier will never reach his goal if his initial\n        deposit into the safety deposit box is $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$.\n\n    2.  Find the amount Xavier needs to initially deposit in order to\n        reach his goal after $7$ years. Give your answer to the nearest\n        dollar. [7 marks]\n\n    $\\\\[-2em]$","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/9dbbca0f5e685431fe27e5f6d20cddfa41909806.svg\" alt=\"9dbbca0f5e685431fe27e5f6d20cddfa41909806.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times 7} \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx\\$22\\hspace{0.15em}500\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/bb1b286c0aec001397762ed487c09e553b272c3a.svg\" alt=\"bb1b286c0aec001397762ed487c09e553b272c3a.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n        30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times n} \\\\[8pt]\n        n & =13 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/990e9181ddb4f41f6ff2aaf08aa718b7d5031131.svg\" alt=\"990e9181ddb4f41f6ff2aaf08aa718b7d5031131.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 10} \\\\[6pt]\n    30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{1200}\\bigg)^{120} \\\\[8pt]\n    r&=6.302...\\hspace{5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\\\[8pt]\n    r&=6.31\\,(\\text{2 d.p.})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    **Note:** Even though we generally round down in this case, here we need to round up to 6.31 to ensure Julie reaches her goal.\n\n3.\n    1.  The total amount of money in Xavier's safe after an infinite\n        number of years is expressed by\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000 + 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right) + 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^2 +  16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^{3} + \\dots\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We have an infinite geometric series with\n        the first term $u_1 = 16\\hspace{0.15em}000$ and the common ratio\n        $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\n        Hence, using **the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric sequence (1.8)**,\n        $\\text{\\(S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}\\)}$, we obtain\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &=  \\dfrac{16\\hspace{0.15em}000}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} \\hspace{5.8em}\\\\[8pt]\n        A &=\\$24\\hspace{0.15em}000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence he will never reach the\n        $$\\$30\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ target if his initial deposit is\n        $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$.\n\n    2.  Let $P$ represent the initial deposit. Note that after the initial deposit, there will be a further $7$ deposits, one at the end of each year.\n\n        The amount of money that Xavier saves after $7$ years, $A$, is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &= P + P\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right) + P\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^2 + \\dots + P\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^{7} \\hspace{2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We have a geometric series with the first\n        term $u_1 = P$, common ratio $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $8$ terms in total.\n\n        Hence, using the formula **for the sum of $\\bm{n}$ terms of a finite geometric sequence (1.3)**, $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\n        with $\\text{\\(n = 8\\)}$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{A} &= S_{8} \\\\[6pt]\n        30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\dfrac{P\\left(1-\\left( \\frac 1 3 \\right)^8\\right)}{1-\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\[12pt]\n        P &= \\dfrac{20\\,000}{1-\\left( \\frac 1 3 \\right)^8}\\\\[12pt]\n        P&=\\$20\\hspace{0.15em}003 \\hspace{1em}\\text{ [to the nearest dollar]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA840","marks":"13","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA840\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nGrant wants to save $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ over 5 years to help his\nson pay for his college tuition. He deposits $$\\$20\\hspace{0.15em}000$$\ninto a savings account that has an interest rate of $6\\%$ per annum\ncompounded **monthly** for $5$ years.\n\n1.  Show that Grant will not be able to reach his target. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the minimum amount, to the nearest dollar, that Grant would\n    need to deposit initially for him to reach his target. [3 marks]\n\nGrant only has $$\\$20\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to invest, so he asks his\nsister, Caroline, to help him accelerate the saving process. Caroline is\nhappy to help and offers to contribute part of her income each year. Her\nannual income is $$\\$37 \\hspace{0.15em}500$$ per year. She starts by\ncontributing one fifth of her annual income, and then decreases her\ncontributions by half each year until the target is reached. Caroline's\ncontributions do not yield any interest.\n\n3.  Show that Grant and Caroline together can reach the target\n    in 5 years. [4 marks]\n\nGrant and Caroline agree that Caroline should stop contributing once she\ncontributes enough to complement the deficit of Grant's investment.\n\n4.  Find the **whole** number of years after which Caroline\n    will stop contributing. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/64fb25df045f9e0b7a416f15dde7a9fce22438d5.svg\" alt=\"64fb25df045f9e0b7a416f15dde7a9fce22438d5.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 20\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 5} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx \\$26\\hspace{0.15em}977\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therfore, Grant\n    will not reach his target as the value of his investment after $5$\n    years is $$\\$26\\hspace{0.15em}977$$ which is less than his\n    $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ goal.\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/aaeeec80128922b9372aa176b0353a0a146271f3.svg\" alt=\"aaeeec80128922b9372aa176b0353a0a146271f3.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\hspace{4.25em} 40\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 5} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.5em}\\mathrm{PV} \\approx \\$29\\hspace{0.15em}655 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The amount that Caroline contributes in the first year is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P} &= \\dfrac{1}{5}(37\\hspace{0.15em}500) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$7\\hspace{0.15em}500\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The total\n    amount that Caroline contributes after $5$ years is expressed by\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &= 7500 + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right) + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right)^2 +  + \\dots + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right)^{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have a geometric series with the first term\n    $u_1 = 7500$ and $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Hence, using the formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n - 1)}{r - 1}$ with\n    $\\text{\\(n = 5\\)}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{A} &= S_{5} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\hspace{2.75em} &= 7\\hspace{0.15em}500\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\left( \\frac 1 2 \\right)^5 - 1}{\\frac{1}{2} - 1}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\$14\\hspace{0.15em}531\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, the total amount that Grant and Caroline can save in $5$\n    years is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.5em} \\text{Total amount} &\\approx 26\\hspace{0.15em}977 + 14\\hspace{0.15em}531 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.35em} \\approx \\$41\\hspace{0.15em}508\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, Grant and Caroline together can reach the target as the total\n    amount saved exceeds the target of $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$.\n\n4.  The amount Caroline must contribute to help Grant reach the target\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.75em} C &= 40\\hspace{0.15em}000 - 26\\hspace{0.15em}977 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$13\\hspace{0.15em}023\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    7500 + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right) + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right)^2 +  \\dots \\hspace{1em} &> 13\\hspace{0.15em}023\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have a geometric series with the first term $u_1 = 7500$ and\n    $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n - 1)}{r - 1}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    7500\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\left( \\frac 1 2 \\right)^n - 1}{\\frac{1}{2} - 1}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.2em} &> 13\\hspace{0.15em}023 \\\\[12pt]\n    n &> 2.92358 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, Caroline will stop contributing after\n     $3\\hspace{0.3em} \\text{years}$"}]},{"name":"Proofs","questions":[{"ref":"AA111","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA111\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Show that $(2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 = 16n^3+12n$ for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, prove that the sum of the cubes of any two\n    consecutive odd integers is divisible by four. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n-1)^3 &= (2n)^3 - 3(2n)^2 + 3(2n) - 1 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8n^3 - 12n^2 + 6n - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n+1)^3 &= (2n)^3 + 3(2n)^2 + 3(2n) + 1 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8n^3 + 12n^2 + 6n + 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Summing our expressions for $(2n-1)^3$ and $(2n+1)^3$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 &= 8n^3 - \\cancel{12n^2} + 6n - \\cancel{1} + 8n^3 + \\cancel{12n^2} + 6n + \\cancel{1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 = 16n^3 + 12n \\hspace{2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $2n-1$ and $2n+1$ be two consecutive odd integers for some\n    $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    The sum of the cubes of $2n-1$ and $2n+1$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.4em} (2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 &= 16n^3 + 12n \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4n(4n^2 + 3) \\hspace{2.22em} [\\text{multiple of $4$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the sum of the cubes of any two consecutive odd integers is\n    divisible by four."},{"ref":"AA1245","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1245\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the 3 digit number $m=134$.\n\nThe **digits** of $m$ are 1, 3 and 4.\n\nThe sum of the digits of $m$ is $1+3+4=8$, and we could write the number $m$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    m &= 100 \\times 1 + 10 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow consider a 4 digit number $n$.\n\nProve that if the sum of the digits of $n$ is divisible by 3, then $n$ is also divisible by 3.","markScheme":"Let the digits of $n$ be $p, q, r$ and $s$, where $p,q,r,s\\in\\mathbb{Z^+}$, $1 \\leq p \\leq 9$, and $0 \\leq q,r,s\\leq9$.\n\nWe can therefore rewrite $n$ as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn=1000p+100q+10r+s \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the sum of the digits is divisible by three, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{p+q+r+s}{3} &= k \\hspace{1em}(k\\in\\mathbb{Z^+})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    p+q+r+s=3k\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging to make $s$ the subject, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    s=3k-p-q-r \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation for $n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    n&=1000p+100q+10r+3k-p-q-r \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=999p+99q+9r+3k\\\\[6pt]\n    &=3(333p+33q+3r+k)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=3v \\hspace{1em}[\\text{where } v=333p+33q+3r+k \\text{ and }v \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}]\\\\[6pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\text{if the digits of $n$ are divisible by 3, then $n$ is also divisible by $3$}$$ ... as required.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA834","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA834\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nConsider two consecutive positive integers, $k$ and $k+1$.\n\nShow that the difference of their squares is equal to the sum of the two\nintegers.","markScheme":"The difference of the squares of $k$ and $k+1$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.5em}(k+1)^2 - k^2 &= k^2 + 2k + 1 - k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2k + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n&= k + (k + 1) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{sum of $k$ and $k+1$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the difference of the squares of two consecutive positive integers\nis equal to their sum."},{"ref":"AA835","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA835\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nProve that the sum of three consecutive positive integers is divisible\nby $3$.","markScheme":"Let $n, \\, n + 1$ and $n+2$ be three consecutive positive integers for\nsome $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^{+}}$.\n\nThe sum of the three integers is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8em} n + (n + 1) + (n+2) &= 3n + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 3(n+1) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{multiple of $3$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the sum of three consecutive positive integers is divisible by\n$3$."},{"ref":"AA836","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA836\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe product of three consecutive integers is increased by the middle\ninteger.\n\nProve that the result is a perfect cube.","markScheme":"Let $n-1,\\, n$ and $n+1$ be three consecutive integers for some\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThe product of these three integers is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(n-1) \\times n \\times (n + 1) &=  n \\times (n^2 - 1) \\\\[6pt]\n&= n^3 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf this product is\nincreased by the middle integer, $n$, then the result would be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.25em} (n^3 - n) + n &= n^3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{perfect cube}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the result of increasing the product of three consecutive integers\nby the middle integer is a perfect cube."},{"ref":"AA950","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA950\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Prove that $\\dfrac{5}{x^2} = \\dfrac{5}{x(x-2)}-\\dfrac{10}{x^2(x-2)}$. [3 marks]\n\n2. Determine the set of numbers $x$ for which the proof in part (a) is valid. [2 marks]\n$$","markScheme":"1. By working out the right side of the equation, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{5}{x(x-2)}-\\dfrac{10}{x^2(x-2)} &= \\dfrac{5x}{x^2(x-2)}-\\dfrac{10}{x^2(x-2)} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5x-10}{x^2(x-2)} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5\\ \\cancel{(x-2)}}{x^2\\ \\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5}{x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\boxed{\\text{LHS} = \\text{RHS}}$, as required.\n\n2. We cannot have a denominator equal to zero, and therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2 &\\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 0 \\hspace{3.95em} \\text{[from L.H.S.]} \\\\[8pt]\n        x(x-2) &\\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 0 , 2 \\hspace{3em} \\text{[from R.H.S.]} \\\\[8pt]\n        x^2(x-2) &\\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 0 , 2 \\hspace{3em} \\text{[from R.H.S.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore the proof is valid for all values of $x$ except $x = 0$ and $x=2$.\\\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}, x \\neq 0 \\text{  and  } x\\neq 2$"}]},{"name":"Sequences & Series","questions":[{"ref":"AA001","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA001\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider an arithmetic sequence $2,6,10,14,\\dots$\n\n1.  Find the common difference, $d$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $10$th term in the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the sum of the first $10$ terms in the sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 2$ and $u_2 = 6$.\n    Hence the common difference is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6 - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 10$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} u_{10} &= u_1 + (10-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2 + (10-1)(4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 38\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=10$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{10} &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(u_1 + u_{10}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2 + 38) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 200\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA002","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA002\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn arithmetic sequence has $u_1= 40$, $u_2 = 32$, $u_3 = 24$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference, $d$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_8$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $S_8$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common difference of an arithmetic sequence is found by subtracting any term in the sequence from the next term in the sequence. We could subtract $u_1$ from $u_2$, or subtract $u_2$ from $u_3$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 32 - 40 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    d&=-8\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 8$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} u_8 &= u_1 + (8-1)d   \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 40 + (8-1)(-8) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    u_8&=-16\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=8$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} S_8 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(u_1 + u_8) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(40 + (-16)) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[11pt]\n    S_8 &=96 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA003","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA003\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the infinite geometric sequence $4480$, $-3360$, $2520$,\n$-1890,\\dots$\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $20$th term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the **exact** sum of the infinite sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 4480$ and $u_2 = -3360$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{3360}{4480} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -0.75\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 r^{n-1}$ with $n = 20$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} u_{20} &= u_1 r^{20-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4480)(-0.75)^{20-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx -18.9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3em} S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{4480}{1-(-0.75)} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2560\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA004","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA004\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nAn arithmetic sequence is given by $3$, $5$, $7,\\dots$\n\n1.  Write down the value of the common difference, $d$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find\n    1.  $u_{10}$;\n\n    2.  $S_{10}$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $u_n = 253$, find the value of $n$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 3$ and $u_2 = 5$.\n    Hence the value of $d$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5 - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 10$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.1em} u_{10} &= u_1 + (10-1)d  \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3 + (10-1)(2) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 21\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n        $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n=10$, we find\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         S_{10} &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(u_1 + u_{10}) \\\\[6pt]\n         &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(3 + 21) \\\\[11pt]\n         &= 120\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n3.  Substituting $u_n = 253$, $u_1 = 3$ and $d = 2$ in\n    $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ and solving the resulting equation for $n$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} 253 &= 3 + (n-1)(2) \\\\[6pt]\n    253 &= 2n+1 \\\\[6pt]\n    252 &= 2n\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    n = 126\\hspace{1.1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA005","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA005\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, $u_5 = 24$, $u_{13} = 80$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the first term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the sum of the first $20$ terms in the sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n = 13$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{13} &= u_5 + (13-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    80 &= 24 + (13-5)d \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    56 &= 8d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 7 \\hspace{6.35em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_5 + (1-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 + (1-5)(7) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n    $n=20$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} S_{20} &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(2u_1 + (20-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(2(-4) + (20-1)(7)) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 1250\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA006","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA006\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $u_1 = 32$,\n$u_2 = -16$, $u_3 = 8$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_6$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $S_{\\infty}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The value of $r$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{16}{32} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{3.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 r^{n-1}$ with $n = 6$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_6 &= u_1 r^{6-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 32\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em} -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)^{6-1} \\\\[4pt]\n    &= 32\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{32}\\bigg) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.405em} S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{32}{1-(-1/2)} \\hspace{5.45em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{32}{3/2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{64}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA007","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA007\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, $u_4 = 12$, $u_{11} = -9$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the first term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the sum of the first $11$ terms in the sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_4 + (n-4)d$ with $n = 11$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{11} &= u_4 + (11-4)d \\\\[6pt]\n    -9 &= 12 + (11-4)d \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    -21 &= 7d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = -3 \\hspace{5.5em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_4 + (n-4)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_4 + (1-4)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12 + (1-4)(-3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 21\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=11$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{11} &= \\dfrac{11}{2}(u_1 + u_{11}) \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11}{2}(21 + (-9)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11}{2}(12) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 66\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA008","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA008\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the following sequence of figures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA008.PNG\" alt=\"AA008\">\n\nFigure 1 contains $6$ line segments.\n\n1.  Given that Figure $n$ contains $101$ line segments, show that\n    $n = 20$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total number of line segments in the first $20$ figures.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have an arithmetic sequence with $u_1 = 6$, $u_2 = 11$ and\n    $u_3 = 16$.\n\n    The common difference is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11 - 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we substitute $u_n = 101$, $u_1 = 6$\n    and $d = 5$ in $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ and solve the resulting equation\n    for $n$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9em} 101 &= 6 + (n-1)(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    101 &= 5n+1 \\\\[6pt]\n    100 &= 5n\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    n = 20\\hspace{2.3em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=20$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} S_{20} &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(u_1 + u_{20}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(6 + 101) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 10(107) \\\\[16pt]\n    &= 1070\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA009","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA009\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, the third term is $41$ and the ninth term is\n$23$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the first term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the smallest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_3 = 41$ and $u_9 = 23$.\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with\n    $n = 9$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_9 &= u_3 + (9-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n    23 &= 41 + (9-3)d \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    -18 &= 6d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = -3 \\hspace{5em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} u_1 &= u_3 + (1-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 41 + (1-3)(-3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 47\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &< 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d) &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{0.7em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(47) + (n-1)(-3)) &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{n}{2}(97-3n) &< 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    32.333 &< n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the smallest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 0$ is\n     $n=33$"},{"ref":"AA010","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA010\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $u_1 = 0.8$,\n$u_2 = 2.4$, $u_3 = 7.2$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $S_8$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the least value of $n$ such that $S_n > 35\\hspace{0.15em}000$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The value of $r$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2.4}{0.8} \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$\n    with $n=8$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} S_8 &= \\dfrac{u_1(r^8 - 1)}{r-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.8(3^8 - 1)}{3-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2624\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1} &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\dfrac{0.8(3^n-1)}{3-1} &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[8pt]\n    0.4(3^n-1) &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[13pt]\n    n &> 10.358 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the least value of $n$ such that $S_n > 35\\hspace{0.15em}000$\n    is  $n=11$"},{"ref":"AA011","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA011\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $u_1 = 0.4$,\n$u_2 = 0.6$, $u_3 = 0.9$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the sum of the first ten terms in the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the greatest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 650$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The value of $r$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.6}{0.4} \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 1.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$\n    with $n=10$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} S_{10} &= \\dfrac{u_1(r^{10} - 1)}{r-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.4(1.5^{10} - 1)}{1.5-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 45.3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &< 650 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1} &< 650 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{7.7em} \\dfrac{0.4(1.5^n-1)}{1.5-1} &< 650 \\\\[8pt]\n    0.8(1.5^n-1) &< 650 \\\\[13pt]\n    n &< 16.528 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the greatest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 650$ is\n     $n=16$"},{"ref":"AA012","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA012\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe third term of an arithmetic sequence is equal to $7$ and the sum of\nthe first $8$ terms is $20$.\n\nFind the common difference and the first term.","markScheme":"Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 1$ and\n$n = 8$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_3 + (1-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 7-2d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_8 &= u_3 + (8-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 7+5d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n$S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n=8$, we get\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{4em}  S_8 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(u_1 + u_8) \\\\[6pt]\n 20 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}((7-2d) + (7+5d)) \\\\[6pt]\n 20 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(14 + 3d) \\\\[10pt]\n 20 &= 56 + 12d \\\\[14pt]\n -36 &= 12d \\\\\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n$$\nd = -3 \\hspace{4em}\n$$\nHence\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_3-2d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 7 - 2(-3) \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA013","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA013\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe first term and the common ratio of a geometric series are denoted,\nrespectively, by $u_1$ and $r$, where $u_1,r \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. Given that\nthe fourth term is $64$ and the sum to infinity is $625$, find the value\nof $u_1$ and the value of $r$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_4 &= 64 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\nu_1r^{4-1} &= 64 \\\\[6pt]\nu_1r^3 &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\infty} &= 625 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 625 \\\\[5pt]\nu_1 &= 625(1-r)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1r^3 &= 64 \\\\[6pt]\n625(1-r)r^3 &= 64  \\hspace{6.45em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{14.4em}r = 0.8 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{0.5em} [\\text{since $r\\in \\mathbb{Q}$}]\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.15em} u_1 &= 625(1-r) \\\\[7pt]\n&= 625(1-0.8) \\\\[7pt]\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Solving $625(1-r)r^3 = 64$ on the G.D.C. gives two solutions, $r = 0.8$ and $r = 0.695\\ldots$, both of which satisfy $|r| < 1$. However, only $r = 0.8$ is rational, so the second solution must be rejected since we are given $r \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ in the question."},{"ref":"AA014","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA014\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn a geometric sequence, $u_2 = 6$, $u_5 = 20.25$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the greatest value of $n$ such that $u_n < 200$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_2r^{n-2}$ with $n = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_5 &= u_2r^{5-2} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    20.25 &= 6r^{5-2} \\\\[6pt]\n    r^3 &= 3.375\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.75em} r = 1.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_2r^{n-2}$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_2r^{1-2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6(1.5)^{1-2}  \\hspace{0.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.7em} u_n &< 200 \\\\[6pt]\n    u_1r^{n-1} &< 200 \\\\[6pt]\n    4(1.5)^{n-1} &< 200 \\\\[6pt]\n    n &< 10.648 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the greatest value of $n$ such that $u_n < 200$ is\n     $n=10$"},{"ref":"AA016","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA016\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSarah walks to school each morning. During the first minute, she travels\n$130$ $\\text{metres}$. In each subsequent minute, she travels\n$5$ metres less than the distance she travelled during the previous\nminute. The distance from her home to school is $950$ metres. Sarah\nleaves her house at $8$:$00$ am and must be at\nschool by $8$:$10$ am.\n\nWill Sarah arrive to school on time? Justify your answer.","markScheme":"We have an arithmetic sequence with the first term $u_1 = 130$ and the\ncommon difference $d = -5$.\n\nIf we attempt to solve $S_n = 950$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_n &= 950 \\\\[9pt]\n\\dfrac{n}{2}(2(130)+(n-1)(-5)) &= 950 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{n}{2}(265-5n) &= 950 \\\\[8pt]\nn &\\approx 8.55 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Hence it takes approximately \\(8.55\\) minutes for Sarah to travel \\(950\\) metres.}$\n\n$\\text{Therefore, Sarah \\textbf{will} arrive at school on time.}$"},{"ref":"AA017","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA017\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJack rides his bike to work each morning. During the first minute, he\ntravels $160$ metres. In each subsequent minute, he travels\n$80$ % of the distance travelled during the\nprevious minute.\n\nThe distance from his home to work is $750$ metres. Jack leaves his\nhouse at $8$:$30$ am and must be at work at\n$8$:$40$ am.\n\nWill Jack arrive to work on time? Justify your answer.","markScheme":"We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 160$ and the\ncommon ratio $r = 0.8$.\n\nIf we use the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\nwith $n=10$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{10} &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^{10})}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{160(1-0.8^{10})}{1-0.8} \\\\[12pt]\n&\\approx 714\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Jack only travels \\(714\\) metres by \\(8\\):\\(40\\)\\hspace{0.2em}am. Therefore, Jack \\textbf{will not} arrive to work on time.}$"},{"ref":"AA018","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA018\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe sum of the first three terms of a geometric sequence is $81.3$, and\nthe sum of the infinite sequence is $300$. Find the common ratio.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_3 &= 81.3 \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 81.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\infty} &= 300 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 300 \\\\[5pt]\nu_1 &= 300(1-r)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 81.3 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{300(1-r)(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 81.3 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n300(1-r^3) &= 81.3 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{9.3em} r = 0.9 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA019","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA019\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe first two terms of an infinite geometric sequence, in order, are\n\n$$\n3\\log_3x,\\,\\, 2\\log_3x,\\,\\, \\text{where $x > 0$.}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the sum of the infinite sequence is $9\\log_3 x$.\n    [3 marks]\n\nThe first three terms of an arithmetic sequence, in order, are\n\n$$\n\\log_3x,\\,\\, \\log_3 \\dfrac{x}{3},\\,\\, \\log_3\\dfrac{x}{9},\\,\\, \\text{where $x > 0$.}\n$$\n\n3.  Find the common difference $d$, giving your answer as an\n    integer. [3 marks]\n\nLet $S_6$ be the sum of the first $6$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n\n4.  Show that $S_6 = 6\\log_3 x - 15$. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Given that $S_6$ is equal to one third of the sum of the\n    infinite geometric\\\n    sequence, find $x$, giving your answer in the form\n     $a^p$ where $a,p \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\log_3 x}{3\\log_3 x} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{3\\log_3x}{1 - 2/3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\log_3x}{1/3} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 9\\log_3x \\hspace{5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} d &= u_2 - u_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\log_3 \\dfrac{x}{3} - \\log_3x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (\\cancel{\\log_3x} - \\log_33) - \\cancel{\\log_3x} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n    $n=6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} S_6 &= \\dfrac{6}{2}(2u_1 + (6-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{6}{2}(2(\\log_3 x) + (6-1)(-1)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{6}{2}(2\\log_3 x - 5) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 6\\log_3 x - 15\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  If we solve the equation $S_6 = \\dfrac{1}{3}S_{\\infty}$ for $x$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    6\\log_3 x - 15 &= \\dfrac{1}{3}(9\\log_3x) \\hspace{3.6em} \\\\[8pt]\n    6\\log_3 x - 15 &= 3\\log_3x \\\\[12pt]\n    3\\log_3x &= 15 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\log_3x &= 5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x = 3^5 \\hspace{2.5em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA020","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA020\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe $1$st, $5$th and $13$th terms of an arithmetic sequence, with common\ndifference $d$, $d \\neq 0$, are the first three terms of a geometric\nsequence, with common ratio $r$, $r \\neq 1$. Given that the $1$st term\nof both sequences is $12$, find the value of $d$ and the value of $r$.","markScheme":"The $1$st, $5$th and $13$th terms of the arithmetic sequence are $12$,\n$12 + 4d$ and $12 + 12d$.\n\nThe first three terms of the geometric sequence are $12$, $12r$ and\n$12r^2$ .\n\nHence we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 4d &= 12r  \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n12 + 12d &= 12r^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nDividing the equation $(1)$\nby $4$ and the equation (2) by $12$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + d &= 3r  \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n1 + d &= r^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we subtract the equation $(4)$\nfrom the equation $(3)$ and solve the resulting\n$\\text{equation}$ for $r$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 + d) - (1 + d) &= 3r - r^2 \\\\[6pt]\n2 &= 3r - r^2 \\hspace{4.55em} \\\\[6pt]\nr^2 - 3r + 2 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(r - 1)(r - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{7.9em} r = 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $r \\neq 1$}]\n$$\n\nHence, if we substitute $r = 2$ in the equation (4) and solve the\nresulting equation for $d$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + d &= 2^2 \\\\[6pt]\nd &= 2^2 -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = 3 \\hspace{0.45em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA021","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA021\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe first three terms of an infinite geometric sequence are\n$k-4,\\,\\, 4,\\,\\, k+2$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down an expression for the common ratio, $r$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $k$ satisfies the equation\n        $k^2 - 2k - 24 = 0$.[5 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the possible values for $k$.\n\n    2.  Find the possible values for $r$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  The geometric sequence has an infinite sum.\n\n    1.  Which value of $r$ leads to this sum. Justify your answer.\n\n    2.  Find the sum of the sequence. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The common ratio is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4}{k-4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        or\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_3}{u_2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{k+2}{4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.5em} \\dfrac{4}{k-4} &= \\dfrac{k+2}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n        16 &= (k+2)(k-4) \\\\[10pt]\n        16 &= k^2 - 2k - 8 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        k^2 - 2k - 24 = 0 \\hspace{7.05em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Solving the equation $k^2 - 2k - 24 = 0$ for $k$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        k^2 - 2k - 24 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        (k+4)(k-6) &= 0 \\hspace{7.65em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        k = -4,\\, 6 \\hspace{0.25em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $r = \\dfrac{4}{k-4}$ for $k = -4,\\, 6$, we obtain\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\hspace{1.5em} r &= \\dfrac{4}{(-4)-4} \\\\[6pt]\n         &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n        and\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{4}{6-4} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  The infinite sum exists when $|r| < 1$.\n        We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bigg|\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\bigg| &= \\dfrac{1}{2} < 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence\n         $r = - 1/2$\n        leads to this sum.\n\n    2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n        $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$,\n\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{(-8)}{1 - (-1/2)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{(-8)}{3/2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{16}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA022","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA022\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe first two terms of an infinite geometric sequence are $u_1 = 20$ and\n$u_2 = 16\\sin^2 \\theta$, where $\\text{\\(0 < \\theta < 2\\pi\\)}$, and\n$\\theta \\neq \\pi$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find an expression for $r$ in terms of $\\theta$.\n\n    2.  Find the possible values of $r$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the sum of the infinite sequence is\n    $\\dfrac{100}{3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta}$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the values of $\\theta$ which give the greatest value of the\n    sum. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The common ratio is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{16\\sin^2 \\theta}{20} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4\\sin^2 \\theta}{5}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Since $0 < \\theta < 2\\pi$ and $\\theta \\neq \\pi$, we have\n        $\\sin \\theta \\neq 0$.\n\n        Hence we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        0 < \\sin^2 \\theta &\\leq 1 \\\\[9pt]\n        0 < \\dfrac{4\\sin^2 \\theta}{5} &\\leq \\dfrac{4}{5} \\hspace{2.8em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        0 < r \\leq \\dfrac{4}{5}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the double-angle formula for cosine, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos 2\\theta &= 1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    2\\cos 2\\theta &= 2 - 4\\sin^2\\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta &= 5 - 4\\sin^2\\theta \\hspace{4em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$,\n\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{20}{1 - \\dfrac{4\\sin^2 \\theta}{5}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{100}{5-4\\sin^2 \\theta} \\\\[15pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{100}{3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The greatest value of the sum is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{100}{3 + 2(-1)} &= 100 \\hspace{6.8em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    solving the equation $\\cos 2\\theta = -1$ for $\\theta$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2\\theta &= \\pi,\\, 3\\pi \\hspace{2.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\hspace{1.5em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA023","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA023\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $\\ln x^9$, $\\ln x^3$,\n$\\ln x$, for $x > 0$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, solve $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty 3^{3-k}\\ln x = 27$.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 9\\ln x$ and $u_2 = 3\\ln x$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\ln x}{9\\ln x} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_n &= u_1r^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9\\ln x\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg)^{n-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 3^{3-n}\\ln x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty 3^{3-k}\\ln x \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $S_{\\infty} = 27$ for $x$, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 27 \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n     \\dfrac{9\\ln x}{1 - 1/3} &= 27 \\\\[6pt]\n     \\dfrac{27\\ln x}{3-1} &= 27 \\\\[12pt]\n     \\ln x &= 2 \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    $$\n    x = e^2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA024","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA024\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe seventh term of an arithmetic sequence is equal to $1$ and the sum\nof the first $16$ terms is $52$.\n\nFind the common difference and the first term.","markScheme":"Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_7 + (n-7)d$ with $n = 1$ and\n$n = 16$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_7 + (1-7)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1-6d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{16} &= u_7 + (16-7)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1+9d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n$S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n=16$, we get\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{4em}  S_{16} &= \\dfrac{16}{2}(u_1 + u_{16}) \\\\[6pt]\n 52 &= \\dfrac{16}{2}((1-6d) + (1+9d)) \\\\[6pt]\n 52 &= \\dfrac{16}{2}(2 + 3d) \\\\[10pt]\n 52 &= 16 + 24d \\\\[14pt]\n 36 &= 24d \\\\\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n$$\nd = 1.5 \\hspace{4em}\n$$\nHence\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_7-6d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1 - 6(1.5) \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n&= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA026","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA026\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven a sequence of integers, between $20$ and $300$, which are\ndivisible by $9$.\n\n1.  Find their sum. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Express this sum using sigma notation. [2 marks]\n\nAn arithmetic sequence has first term $-500$ and common difference of\n$8$. The sum of the first $n$ terms of this sequence is negative.\n\n3.  Find the greatest value of $n$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have an arithmetic sequence $27,\\, 36,\\, 45,\\,\\dots,\\, 297$ with\n    $u_1 = 27$ and $d = 9$.\n\n    The number of terms in the sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} n &= \\floor*{\\dfrac{300}{9}} - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    & = 31\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n = 31$, $u_1 = 27$ and\n    $u_n = 297$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(27 + 297) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5022\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Alternatively,\n    using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $n = 31$, $u_1 = 27$ and\n    $d = 9$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.6em} S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(2(27) + (31-1)(9)) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5022\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Since $u_1 = 27$, $d = 9$ and $n = 31$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} S_{31}  &= 27 + (27 + 9) + (27 + 2\\cdot9) + \\cdots + (27 + 30\\cdot9) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^{31}} (27 + (n-1)(9)) \\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}\\text{or} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^{31}} 9(n+2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $u_1 = -500$ and $d = 8$,\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(-500) + (n-1)(8)) &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    n(-500 + (n-1)(4)) &< 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    n &< 126 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    the greatest value of $n$ is\n     $125$"},{"ref":"AA1023","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1023\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is equal to $13$ and the sum of the first $10$ terms is $55$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference and the first term. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Determine the greatest value of $n$ such that the $n$th term is positive. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 4$ and $u_4 = 13$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_4 &= u_1 + 3d \\\\[6pt]\n    13 &= u_1 + 3d \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[1]}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $n=10$ and $S_{10}=55$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{10} &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2u_1 + 9d) \\\\[6pt]\n    55 &= 5(2u_1 + 9d) \\\\[6pt]\n    11 &= 2u_1 + 9d \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[2]}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Solving the system formed by equations [1] and [2] we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2u_1 + 6d &= 26 \\hspace{3em} \\text{(equation \\textcolor{green}{[1]} $\\times$ 2)} \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[3]}}\\\\[6pt]\n    2u_1 + 9d &= 11 \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[4]}}\\\\[6pt]\n    3d &= -15 \\hspace{2.5em}\\text{(equation \\textcolor{green}{[4]} - equation \\textcolor{green}{[3]})}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}d = -5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= 13-3d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 13 - 3(-5) \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.2em}u_1 = 28\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We must have $u_n > 0$. Using the $n$th term formula with $u_1=28$ and $d=-5$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_n &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    u_1 + (n-1)d &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    28 - 5(n-1) &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    28 - 5n + 5 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    33 &> 5n \\\\[6pt]\n    n &< \\dfrac{33}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $n$ must be an integer, and the greatest integer less than $\\dfrac{33}{5}$ is $6$.\n\n    Hence, the greatest value of $n$ such that $u_n$ is positive is $n=6$."},{"ref":"AA1150","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1150\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nA geometric series has a common ratio of $3^x$.\n\n1.  State the domain of $x$ such that the sum to infinity of the series exists. [2 marks]\n\nThe sum to infinity of the series is found to be $54$.\n\n2. Find the value of $x$, given that the first term of the series is $52$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $\\bm{S_{\\infty}}\\bm{=}\\dfrac{\\bm{u_1}}{\\bm{1-r}}\\,,\\,\\bm{|r|<1}$ **(1.8)**.\n\n    That is, the common ratio ($r$) must be between $-1$ and $1$ for the sum to infinity to exist. Therefore,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -1<3^x<1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $3^x$ is larger than zero for all $x$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0<3^x<1\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/3%5Ex.png\" alt=\"3^x\">\n\n    From the sketch of $3^x$ we can see that when $x<0$ then $0<3^x<1$.\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The domain such that the sum to infinity exists is $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{x<0}$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Using the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric series with $S_{\\infty}=54$, $r=3^x$ and $u_1=52$ we can write the equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        54&=\\frac{52}{1-3^x}\\\\[6pt]\n        1-3^x&=\\frac{26}{27}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        3^x&=\\frac{1}{27}\\\\[6pt]\n        3^x&=3^{-3}\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=-3\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1163","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1163\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn arithmetic progression has a common difference of $d$, where $d \\neq 0$.\n\nThe $7^{\\text{th}}$ term is equal to the $3^{\\text{rd}}$ term squared and the $5^{\\text{th}}$ term is $1$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [4 marks]\n\n$S_n$ is the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence.\n\n2.  Find the largest value of $n$ such that $S_n<300$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can use the information given to form an equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        u_7&=(u_3)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the general term of an arithmetic progression, we can write the equation in terms of the first term, $u_1$, and $d$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        u_1+(7-1) \\times d&=(u_1 + (3-1) \\times d)^2\\\\[6pt]\n        u_1+6d&=(u_1 + 2d)^2\\\\[6pt]\n        u_1+6d&=u_1^2+4u_1d+4d^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the given fact $u_5=1$ we can write $u_1$ in terms of $d$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1&=u_1+4d\\\\[6pt]\n    u_1&=1-4d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now write the original equation in terms of $d$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        1-4d+6d&=(1-4d)^2+4(1-4d)d+4d^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now simplify both sides of this equation and then solve the equation graphically.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        1+2d&=(1-4d)^2+4d-16d^2+4d^2\\\\[6pt]\n        1+2d&=(1-4d)^2+4d-12d^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We do this by sketching the L.H.S. and R.H.S. of the above equation, as two seperate functions, using a G.D.C. and then finding the points of intersection\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x)=&=1+2x\\\\[6pt]\n        g(x)&=(1-4x)^2+4x-12x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA1163_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1163_a\">\n\n    Hence we can see that the intersection points tell us that $d=0$ and $d=\\tfrac{3}{2}$.\n    As $d\\neq0$ then the common difference is $d=\\tfrac{3}{2}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the sum $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with $d=\\tfrac{3}{2}$ we can write $S_n$ in terms of $u_1$ and $n$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2u_1+\\tfrac{3}{2}(n-1))\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can find $u_1$ by using the 5$^{th}$ term\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        1&=u_1+4\\times \\tfrac{3}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        u_1&=-5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we can now write $S_n$ in terms of $n$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2(-5)+\\tfrac{3}{2}(n-1)) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the G.D.C. to produce a table of values for $S_n$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/table_2.png\" alt=\"table_2\">\n\n    We can see that for $S_n<300$ the largest value of $n$ is $24$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA119","marks":"13","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA119\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1.  The following diagram shows \\[PQ\\], with length\n    $4$ cm. The line is divided into an infinite\n    number of line segments. The diagram shows the first four\n    segments.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/2ecea1f156846207a208a3fc02a1af87be7132ca.svg\" alt=\"2ecea1f156846207a208a3fc02a1af87be7132ca.svg\">\n\n    The length of the line segments are $m$ cm,\n    $m^2$ cm, $m^3$ cm,\n    $\\dots$, where $0 < m < 1$.\n\n    Show that $m = \\dfrac{4}{5}$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  The following diagram shows \\[RS\\], with length\n    $l$ cm, where $l > 1$. Squares with side\n    lengths $n$ cm, $n^2$ cm,\n    $n^3$ cm, $\\dots$, where $0 < n < 1$, are\n    drawn along \\[RS\\]. This process is carried on indefinitely. The\n    diagram shows the first four squares.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/282d818bda418427e2f4b47fb94d3fce3af0ad9c.svg\" alt=\"282d818bda418427e2f4b47fb94d3fce3af0ad9c.svg\">\n\n    The **total** sum of the areas of all the squares is\n    $\\dfrac{25}{11}$. Find the value of $l$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with $u_1 = m = r$, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{2.5em} 4 &= \\dfrac{m}{1-m} \\hspace{5em}[1]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $[1]$ for $m$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4 - 4m &= m \\\\[12pt]\n    4 &= 5m \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    m = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with $u_1 = n^2 = r$, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{1.9em} \\dfrac{25}{11} &= \\dfrac{n^2}{1-n^2} \\hspace{5em}[2]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $[2]$ for $n$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    25 - 25n^2 &= 11n^2 \\\\[14pt]\n    25 &= 36n^2 \\\\[10pt]\n    n^2 &= \\dfrac{25}{36} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    n &= \\dfrac{5}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the sum of an infinite\n    geometric sequence formula $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$\n    with\n\n    $u_1 = r = n = \\dfrac{5}{6}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} l &= \\dfrac{5/6}{1 - 5/6} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA122","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA122\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe sum of an infinite geometric sequence is $27$. The second term of\nthe sequence is $6$. Find the possible values of $r$.","markScheme":"We have $S_{\\infty} = 27$ and $u_2 = 6$.\n\nUsing the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_2 &= u_1r^{2-1} \\\\[12pt]\n6 &= u_1r \\\\[6pt]\nu_1 &= \\dfrac{6}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the sum of an infinite\ngeometric sequence formula\n$S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27 &= \\dfrac{6/r}{1-r} \\\\[5pt]\n27-27r &= \\dfrac{6}{r} \\\\[10pt]\n9r - 9r^2 &= 2 \\\\[10pt]\n0 &= 9r^2 - 9r + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the quadratic formula to find the roots of the equation, we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.6em} r &= \\dfrac{-(-9)\\pm\\sqrt{(-9)^2 - 4(9)(2)}}{2(9)} \\\\[9pt]\nr &= \\dfrac{6}{18},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{12}{18} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{1}{3},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{2}{3} \\hspace{0.3em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1275","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1275\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA geometric sequence, with common ratio $r$, has a first term of $81$. The sum of the first four terms of the sequence is $195$.\n\n1.  1.  Find $r$.\n\n    2.  Hence, find $G_n$, the sum of the first $n$ terms of the geometric sequence. [3 marks]\n\nThe first three terms of an arithmetic sequence, with a common difference of $d$, are $\\log 96, \\log 48$ and $\\log24$.\n\n2.  Find $d$ in the form $p\\log q$ where $p, q \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.[2 marks]\n\n$A_n$ is the sum of the first $n$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n\n3.  Show that $A_n=\\log{\\left(3^n\\times\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^{11n-n^2}\\right)}$.[5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the maximum value of $n$ such that $\\left|G_n\\right|>\\left|A_n\\right|$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  From the given information we know $u_1=81$ and $S_4=195$. \n\n        \n\n        Using the formula for the sum of a geometric sequence we can form an equation and then solve for $r$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            S_n&=\\frac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            S_4&=\\frac{u_1(1-r^4)}{1-r}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            195&=\\frac{81(1-r^4)}{1-r}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using a G.D.C. we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            r&=0.6666...\\\\[6pt]\n\n            r&=\\dfrac{2}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n    2.  Using the answer from (a) (i) and the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric series we can write the general term for $G_n$ as \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            G_n&=\\frac{81(1-(\\frac{2}{3})^n)}{1-\\frac{2}{3}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            &=\\frac{81(1-(\\frac{2}{3})^n)}{\\frac{1}{3}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            &=81\\left(1-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right)\\times3\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &=243\\left(1-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2.  In an arithmetic sequence, there is a common difference between terms. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    d&=u_2 - u_1\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting the given values for $u_1$ and $u_2$ we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    d&=\\log 48-\\log96\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    d&=\\log\\left(\\frac{48}{96}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    d&=\\log \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    d&=\\log 2^{-1}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    d&=-\\log 2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n3.  The formula for sum of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2u_1+(n-1)d)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $u_1=\\log 96$ and $d=-\\log 2$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2\\log 96+(n-1)(-\\log 2))\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{n}{2}(\\log96^2-(n-1)\\log 2)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Applying logarithm rules we get     \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n    &=\\frac{n}{2}(\\log96^2-\\log2^{(n-1)})\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{n}{2}\\log \\frac{96^2}{2^{(n-1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log\\left(\\frac{96^2}{2^{(n-1)}}\\right)^{\\frac{n}{2}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{96^2}{2^{(n-1)}}\\right)^n}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{96^2}}{\\sqrt{2^{(n-1)}}}\\right)^n\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log{\\left(\\frac{96^n}{(\\sqrt{2})^{n^2-n}}\\right)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Looking at the form of the answer we can attempt to rewrite the denominator using by negating its exponent \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log(96^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n    Let's rewrite $96$ as a product of prime factors\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log((2^5\\times3)^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then we'll rewrite $2^5$ as a base of $\\sqrt{2}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log(((\\sqrt{2})^{10}\\times3)^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can distribute the power of $n$ and then use exponent laws again to simplify to the required form.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log((\\sqrt{2})^{10n}\\times3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\log(3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{10n+n-n^2})\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\log(3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{11n-n^2})\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required.}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the functions from (a) (ii) and (c) we can form an inequality which we can solve for $n$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{red}{\\left|243\\left(1-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right)\\right|}>\\textcolor{blue}{\\left|\\log(3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{11n-n^2})\\right|}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can sketch the $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{L.H.S.}}$ and the $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{R.H.S.}}$ on a G.D.C. to get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA1275.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1275\">\n\n    Hence when\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        n<47.88...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    then $|G_n|>|A_n|$.\n\n    As $n$ represents the term value, we therefore round down to the nearest integer value. \n\n    Hence $n=47$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1324","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1324\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA geometric sequence has a first term of $81$, and the sum of the first $4$ terms is $195$.\n\n1.    Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\nThe fourth term of this geometric sequence is the sixth term of an arithmetic sequence.\n\nLet $S_n$ denote the sum of the first $n$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n\nIt is also known that $S_{20}=S_{11}$.\n\n2.    Find the largest possible value of $S_n$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can use the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric sequence where $S_4=195$, $u_1=81$ and $n=4$, to form an equation.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_n&=u_1\\left(\\frac{r^n-1}{r-1}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    195&=81\\left(\\frac{r^4-1}{r-1}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By graphing the L.H.S. and R.H.S. as two functions\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA1324-a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1324-a\">\n\n    we find\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    r&=0.6666...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=0.667\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\bigg(&=\\frac{2}{3}\\bigg)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note, you could have also solved the equation $81+81r+81r^2+81r^3=195$ on your G.D.C.\n\n2. As we need to find the maximum value of the sum of the arithmetic sequence, we must find the first term of the arithmetic sequence, $a$, and the common difference, $d$.\n\n    We will begin by finding the fourth term of the geometric sequence.\n\n    Using the general term of a geometric sequence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_4&=81 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{4-1}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=24\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can use the result above to form an equation for the sixth term of the arithmetic sequence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    24&=a+(6-1)\\times d\\\\[6pt]\n\n    24&=a+5d \\hspace{2em}[1]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence, $S_n=\\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d)$ we can rewrite the given relation in terms of $a$ and $d$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_{20}&=S_{11}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{20}{2}(2a+19d)&=\\frac{11}{2}(2a+10d)  \\hspace{2em}[2]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We now have two linear equations $[1]$ and $[2]$, which we can solve using our G.D.C.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a&=36&d=&-\\frac{12}{5}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can now find $S_n$ in terms of $n$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\bigg[2(36)+(n-1)\\left(-\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We could simplify this expression. However, as this is a paper $2$ question, it is better practice to not simplify unless you are required to.\n\n    \n\n    We need to find the maximum value of $S_n$ remembering the fact that this represents a sequence and is only valid for positive integer values of $n$.\n\n    \n\n    Using a table of values is a better approach than graphing, to find the maximum value.\n\n    | $n$ | $S_n$ |\n\n    | --- | --- |\n\n    |&nbsp; $13$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $280.8$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $14$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $285.6$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $15$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $\\textcolor{red}{288}$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $16$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $\\textcolor{red}{288}$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $17$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $285.6$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $18 $ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $280.8$ &nbsp;|\n\n    We can see that the $15$th and $16$th terms both give the same maximum value of $S_n$.\n\n    Hence the maximum value of $S_n$ is $288$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that graphing the function $y=\\dfrac{x}{2}\\bigg[2(36)+(x-1)\\left(-\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\bigg]$ on your G.D.C. gives the maximum point $(15.5, 288.3)$. If you gave the answer as $288.3$ then you would not receive the final A$1$ mark as this is not a valid value for the sum of the sequence as $n$ must be a whole number.\n\n    \n\n    This is why, often when working with sequences, it is better practice to use a table of values."},{"ref":"AA1452","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1452\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe $m$th, $n$th and $k$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $a$, $b$ and $c$ respectively, where $a,b,c>0$. The common difference of the arithmetic sequence is $d$.\n\n1. Show that $b-c=(n-k)d$.[2 marks]\n\n2. Hence show that $(b-c)(m-1)+(c-a)(n-1)+(a-b)(k-1)=0$. [2 marks]\n\n    \n\nThe $m$th, $n$th and $k$th terms of a geometric sequence, with first term $v_1>0$ and common ratio $r>0$, are also $a$, $b$ and $c$ respectively.\n\n3. Hence show that $a^{\\,b-c}\\,b^{\\,c-a}\\,c^{\\,a-b}=1$.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using **the formula for the $\\bm{n}$th term of an arithmetic sequence (1.2)**, $u_n = u_1+(n-1)d$, let's first write expressions for $u_m, u_n$ and $u_k$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_m &= u_1+(m-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n\n    u_n &= u_1+(n-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n\n    u_k &= u_1+(k-1)d\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We have that $u_m = a, u_n=b$ and $u_k=c$ and therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a &= u_1+(m-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{[1]}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    b &= u_1+(n-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{[2]}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    c &= u_1+(k-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{[3]}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Subtracting equation **[3]** from equation **[2]**, we can form an expression for $b-c$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    b-c &= \\bigl[u_1+(n-1)d\\bigr]-\\bigl[u_1+(k-1)d\\bigr] \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= (n-1)d-(k-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= d(n-1-(k-1))\\\\[6pt]\n\n    b-c &= \\boxed{(n-k)d}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using the method of part (a), we can form expressions for $a-b$ and $c-a$. This yields\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a-b&=(m-n)d \\\\[6pt]\n\n    c-a&=(k-m)d\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting these into the left-hand side of the given expression, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &(b-c)(m-1)+(c-a)(n-1)+(a-b)(k-1) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    =&(n-k)d(m-1)+(k-m)d(n-1)+(m-n)d(k-1) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    =&d\\left[(n-k)(m-1)+(k-m)(n-1)+(m-n)(k-1)\\right]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Expanding the brackets, we obtain\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{LHS} &= d\\bigl[(mn-n-mk+k) \\\\\n\n    &\\quad+(kn-k-mn+m) \\\\\n\n    &\\quad+(mk-m-nk+n)\\bigr] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\boxed{0} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using **the $\\bm{n}$th term of a geometric sequence (1.3)**, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_n &= u_1r^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    a &= v_1r^{m-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    b &= v_1r^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    c &= v_1r^{k-1}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Substituting into the left-hand side of the given expression, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a^{\\,b-c}\\,b^{\\,c-a}\\,c^{\\,a-b} &= \\left(v_1r^{m-1}\\right)^{b-c}\\left(v_1r^{n-1}\\right)^{c-a}\\left(v_1r^{k-1}\\right)^{a-b} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= v_1^{b-c}r^{(m-1)(b-c)} \\cdot v_1^{c-a}r^{(n-1)(c-a)} \\cdot v_1^{a-b}r^{(k-1)(a-b)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= v_1^{(b-c)+(c-a)+(a-b)} \\cdot r^{(m-1)(b-c)+(n-1)(c-a)+(k-1)(a-b)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= v^0 \\cdot r^{(b-c)(m-1)+(c-a)(n-1)+(a-b)(k-1)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Using our result from part (b), the exponent of $r$ is $0$. Therefore we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a^{\\,b-c}\\,b^{\\,c-a}\\,c^{\\,a-b} &= v_1^{0}\\,r^{0}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\boxed{1} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA199","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA199\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{3\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)}$, for $x > 0$.\n\nThe $k$th maximum point on the graph of $f$ has $x$-coordinate $x_k$,\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.  Given that $x_{k+1} = x_k + d$, find $d$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the value of $n$ such that\n    $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k = 992$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y= f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\" alt=\"97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $x$-coordinates of the points A$(2,20.1)$ and\n    B$(10,20.1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} x_2 &= x_1 + d \\\\[6pt]\n    10 &= 2 + d \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 8\n    $$\n\n2.  We have an arithmetic sequence with the first term $x_1 = 2$ and the\n    common difference $d = 8$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2x_1+(n-1)d)$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k &= 992 \\\\[6pt]\n    S_n &= 992 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{with $x_1 = 2$ \\& $d = 8$}] \\\\[13pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(2)+(n-1)(8)) &= 992 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.1em} n = 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA623","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA623\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOnly one of the following four sequences is arithmetic and only one of\nthem is geometric.\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcccccl}\na_n &=& 1,\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& c_n &=& 1.5,\\,3,\\,4.5,\\,6,\\,\\dots \\\\[12pt]\nb_n &=& \\dfrac{1}{2},\\,\\dfrac{2}{3},\\,\\dfrac{3}{4},\\,\\dfrac{4}{5},\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& d_n &=& 2,\\,1,\\,\\dfrac{1}{2},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,\\dots\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n1.  State which sequence is arithmetic and find the common difference of\n    the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  State which sequence is geometric and find the common ratio of the\n    sequence.[2 marks]\n\n3.  For the **geometric** sequence find the **exact** value of the\n    eighth term. Give your answer as a fraction. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    a_2-a_1 &\\neq& a_3-a_2 &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& c_2-c_1 &=& c_3-c_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    5-1 &\\neq& 10-5 &&& 3-1.5 &=& 4.5-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    4 &\\neq& 5 &&& 1.5 &=& 1.5 \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    b_2-b_1 &\\neq& b_3-b_2 &&& d_2-d_1 &\\neq& d_3-d_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{2}{3}-\\dfrac{1}{2} &\\neq& \\dfrac{3}{4}-\\dfrac{2}{3} &&& 1-2 &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{2}-1 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{6} &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{12} &&& -1 &\\neq& -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $c_n$\n    is arithmetic and\n     $d = 1.5$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& \\dfrac{c_2}{c_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{c_3}{c_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{5}{1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{10}{5} &&& \\dfrac{3}{1.5} &\\neq& \\dfrac{4.5}{3} \\\\[12pt]\n    5 &\\neq& 2 &&& 2 &\\neq& 1.5 \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    \\dfrac{b_2}{b_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{b_3}{b_2} &&& \\dfrac{d_2}{d_1} &=& \\dfrac{d_3}{d_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{2/3}{1/2} &\\neq& \\dfrac{3/4}{2/3} &&& \\dfrac{1}{2} &=& \\dfrac{1/2}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{3} &\\neq& \\dfrac{9}{8} &&& \\dfrac{1}{2} &=& \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $d_n$\n    is geometric and\n     $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n3.  Using the $n$th term formula $d_n = d_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 8$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d_8 &= d_1r^{8-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{64}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA624","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA624\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOnly one of the following four sequences is arithmetic and only one of\nthem is geometric.\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcccccl}\na_n &=& \\dfrac{1}{3},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,\\dfrac{1}{5},\\,\\dfrac{1}{6},\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& c_n &=& 3,\\,1,\\,\\dfrac{1}{3},\\,\\dfrac{1}{9},\\,\\dots \\\\[12pt]\nb_n &=& 2.5,\\,5,\\,7.5,\\,10,\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& d_n &=& 1,\\,3,\\,6,\\,10,\\,\\dots\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n1.  State which sequence is arithmetic and find the common difference of\n    the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  State which sequence is geometric and find the common ratio of the\n    sequence.[2 marks]\n\n3.  For the **geometric** sequence find the **exact** value of the sixth\n    term. Give your answer as a fraction. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    a_2-a_1 &\\neq& a_3-a_2 &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& c_2-c_1 &\\neq& c_3-c_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{4}-\\dfrac{1}{3} &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{5}-\\dfrac{1}{4} &&& 1-3 &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{3}-1 \\\\[12pt]\n    -\\dfrac{1}{12} &\\neq& -\\dfrac{1}{20} &&& -2 &\\neq& -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    b_2-b_1 &=& b_3-b_2 &&& d_2-d_1 &\\neq& d_3-d_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    5-2.5 &=& 7.5-5 &&& 3-1 &\\neq& 6-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    2.5 &=& 2.5 &&& 2 &\\neq& 3\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $b_n$\n    is arithmetic and\n     $d = 2.5$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& \\dfrac{c_2}{c_1} &=& \\dfrac{c_3}{c_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1/4}{1/3} &\\neq& \\dfrac{1/5}{1/4} &&& \\dfrac{1}{3} &=& \\dfrac{1/3}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{4} &\\neq& \\dfrac{4}{5} &&& \\dfrac{1}{3} &=&\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    \\dfrac{b_2}{b_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{b_3}{b_2} &&& \\dfrac{d_2}{d_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{d_3}{d_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{5}{2.5} &\\neq& \\dfrac{7.5}{5} &&& \\dfrac{3}{1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{6}{3} \\\\[12pt]\n    2 &\\neq& 1.5 &&& 3 &\\neq& 2\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $c_n$\n    is geometric and\n     $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n3.  Using the $n$th term formula $c_n = c_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    c_6 &= c_1r^{6-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{6-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{81}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA625","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA625\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA tennis ball is dropped from a height of $h_0\\,\\text{cm}$ and bounces on the ground $n$ times. The height the ball reaches after each\nbounce, $h_1, h_2, h_3, \\dots,h_n,$ form a geometric sequence. The\nheight that the ball reaches after the first bounce, $h_1$, is\n$80$ cm, and after the second bounce, $h_2$, is\n$60$ cm.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio for the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2. Hence, calculate the value of $h_0$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine the height that the ball reaches after the tenth bounce, $h_{10}$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n4.  Find the **total distance** travelled by the ball at the moment it bounces on the ground for the sixth time. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio can be found by taking any term in a geometric sequence, and dividing it by the previous term. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{h_2}{h_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{60}{80} \\\\[12pt]\n    r &= 0.75 \\hspace{2.75em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. In any geometric sequence, the second term, $u_2$, is equal to the first term multiplied by the common ratio. Therefore, we can write\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r &= u_2 \\\\[6pt]\n    u_1 &= \\frac{u_2}{r} \\\\[6pt]\n    h_0 &= \\frac{h_1}{r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\frac{80}{0.75} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 106.6...\\\\[6pt]\n    h_0 &= 107\\,\\text{cm}\\,\\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the $n$th term formula $h_n = h_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 10$ and $h_1=80$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h_{10} &= h_1r^{10-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 80(0.75)^{10-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=6.006...\\\\[7pt]\n    h_{10}&= 6.01\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}\\,\\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  When the ball bounces on the ground for the 6th time, it has travelled **downwards** 6 times, with a total distance of\n\n    $h_0+h_1+h_2+h_3+h_4+h_5$\n\n    and **upwards** 5 times, with a total distance of\n\n    $h_1+h_2+h_3+h_4+h_5$\n\n    this is shown in the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/bounce.png\" alt=\"bounce\">\n\n    We therefore have $h_0$ plus 2 times the sum of the first 5 terms of a geometric sequence with first term $h_1$ and common ratio $r=0.75$. We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Total distance} &= h_0 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{h_1\\left(1-0.75^5\\right)}{1-0.75}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 106.6...+2\\left(\\dfrac{80\\left(1-0.75^5\\right)}{0.25}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 594.7...\\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{Total distance} &= 595\\,\\text{cm}\\,\\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA626","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA626\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe third term, $u_3$, of an arithmetic sequence is $7$. The common\ndifference of\\\nthe sequence, $d$, is $3$.\n\n1.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_{60}$, the $60$th term of sequence. [2 marks]\n\nThe first and fourth terms of this arithmetic sequence are the first two\nterms\\\nof a geometric sequence.\n\n3.  Calculate the sixth term of the **geometric** sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_3 + (1-3)d \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7 + (1-3)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 60$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{60} &= u_3 + (60-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7 + (60-3)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 178\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/ad62ab3f954984b454acd9a772d836d707c86a2f.svg\" alt=\"ad62ab3f954984b454acd9a772d836d707c86a2f.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 6$,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_6 &= u_1r^{6-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1(10)^{6-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 100\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA627","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA627\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fifth term, $u_5$, of a geometric sequence is $125$. The sixth term,\n$u_6$, is $156.25$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the sum of the first $12$ terms of the sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_6}{u_5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{156.25}{125} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 1.25 \\hspace{3.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5r^{n-5}$ with $n = 1$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_5r^{1-5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 125(1.25)^{1-5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 51.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$\n    with $n = 12$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} S_{12} &= \\dfrac{u_1(r^{12}-1)}{r-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{51.2((1.25)^{12}-1)}{1.25-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &=2775.4...\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 2780\\hspace{3em}\\text{rounded to 3 s.f.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA628","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA628\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fourth term, $u_4$, of a geometric sequence is $135$. The fifth\nterm, $u_5$, is $81$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the sum of the first $20$ terms of the sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_5}{u_4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{81}{135} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 0.6 \\hspace{3.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_4r^{n-4}$ with $n = 1$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_4r^{1-4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 135(0.6)^{1-4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 625\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\n    with $n = 20$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} S_{20} &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^{20})}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{625(1-(0.6)^{20})}{1-0.6} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 1560\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA629","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA629\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fifth term, $u_5$, of an arithmetic sequence is $25$. The eleventh\nterm, $u_{11}$, of the same sequence is $49$.\n\n1.  Find $d$, the common difference of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $S_{100}$, the sum of the first $100$ terms of the sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n=11$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{11} &= u_5 + (11-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    49 &= 25 + (11-5)d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5.8em}d = 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n=1$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} u_1 &= u_5 + (1-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25 + (1-5)(4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1+(n-1)d)$\n    with $n = 100$,\n\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} S_{100} &= \\dfrac{100}{2}(2u_1+(100-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{100}{2}(2(9)+(100-1)(4)) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 20\\hspace{0.15em}700\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA631","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA631\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLandmarks are placed along the road from London to Edinburgh and the\ndistance between each landmark is $16.1$ km. The\nfirst landmark placed on the road is $124.7$ km\nfrom London, and the last landmark is near Edinburgh. The length of the\nroad from London to Edinburgh is $667.1$ km.\n\n1.  Find the distance between the fifth landmark and London. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Determine how many landmarks there are along the road. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have an arithmetic sequence with $u_1 = 124.7$ and $d = 16.1$.\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with\n    $n = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} u_5 &= u_1 + (5-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 124.7 + (5-1)(16.1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 189\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the distance between the fifth\n    landmark and London is\n     $189\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}$\n\n2.  If we solve the inequality $u_n < 667.1$ for the largest value of\n    $n$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 + (n-1)d &< 667.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    124.7 + (n-1)(16.1) &< 667.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    n &< 34.7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    n &= 34 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence there are\n     $34$\n    landmarks along the road."},{"ref":"AA632","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA632\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe first term of an arithmetic sequence is $24$ and the common\ndifference is $16$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the $62$nd term of the\n    sequence. [2 marks]\n\nThe first term of a geometric sequence is $8$. The $4$th term of the\ngeometric sequence is equal to the $13$th term of the arithmetic\nsequence given above.\n\n2.  Write down an equation using this information. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the common ratio of the geometric sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 24$ and $d = 16$.\n    Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 62$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} u_{62} &= u_1 + (62-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 + (62-1)(16) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $v_1 = 8$ and $v_4 = u_{13}$.\n    Using the $n$th term formula $v_n = v_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 4$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v_4 &= v_1r^{4-1} \\hspace{1.15em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8r^{4-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8r^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 13$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} u_{13} &= u_1 + (13-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 + (13-1)(16) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 216\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v_4 = u_{13} \\hspace{2.6em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    8r^3 = 216 \\hspace{3.05em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $8r^3 = 216$ for $r$ using G.D.C., we\n    obtain  $r = 3$"},{"ref":"AA633","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA633\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the sum $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_{k = 4}^l (2k-3)$, where $l$ is\na positive integer greater than $4$.\n\n1.  Write down the first three terms of the series. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Write down the number of terms in the series. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Given that $S = 725$, find the value of $l$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $5,\\, 7,\\, 9$\n\n2.\n    $l-3$\n\n3.  From part (a), we have an arithmetic sequence with the first term\n    $u_1 = 5$ and the common difference $d = 2$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $n = l-3$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S &= S_{l-3} \\\\[11pt]\n    \\hspace{4.5em}725 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{l-3}{2}\\bigg](2u_1 + ((l-3)-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    725 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{l-3}{2}\\bigg](2(5) + (l-4)(2)) \\\\[6pt]\n    725 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{l-3}{2}\\bigg](2 + 2l) \\\\[12pt]\n    725 &= (l-3)(l+1) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4.65em} l = 28 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA634","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA634\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $u_n = 5n-1$, for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Using sigma notation, write down an expression for\n        $u_1 + u_2 + u_3 + \\dots + u_{10}$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of the sum from **part (a) (i)**. [4 marks]\n\nA geometric sequence is defined by $v_n = 5\\times 2^{n-1}$, for\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n2.  Find the value of the sum of the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k = 1}^6 \\hspace{0.1em}v_k$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using sigma notation, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        u_1 + u_2 + u_3 + \\dots + u_{10} &= \\sum_{k=1}^{10} u_k \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^{10} (5k-1) \\hspace{8em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  If we rewrite $u_n = 5n-1$ in the form $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} u_n &= 4 + (n-1)(5)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $u_n$ is\n        an arithmetic sequence with the first term $u_1 = 4$ and\\\n        the common difference $d = 5$.\n\n        Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n        $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n        $n=10$\n\n        we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sum_{k=1}^{10} (5k-1) &= S_{10} \\\\[3pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2u_1 + (10-1)d) \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2(4) + (10-1)(5)) \\\\[14pt]\n        &= 265\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.3em} v_1 &= 5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{the first term}] \\\\[9pt]\n    r &= 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{the common ratio}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{v_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$ with\n    $n=6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k = 1}^6 5\\times 2^{k-1} &= S_6 \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{v_1(r^6-1)}{r-1} \\hspace{3.3em}  \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5\\hspace{0.05em}(2^6-1)}{2-1} \\\\[15pt]\n    &= 315\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA636","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA636\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe table shows the first four terms of three sequences: $u_n$, $v_n$,\nand $w_n$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/c39694c1cf7513ffce115791e6b0f1c54c230963.svg\" alt=\"c39694c1cf7513ffce115791e6b0f1c54c230963.svg\">\n\n1.  State which sequence is\n    1.  arithmetic;\n\n    2.  geometric. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the sum of the first $50$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the exact value of the $13$th term of the geometric sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $v_n$\n\n    2.\n        $w_n$\n\n2.  The common difference of the arithmetic sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= v_2 - v_1 \\hspace{0.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 - 12 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2v_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n    $n=50$,\n\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} S_{50} &= \\dfrac{50}{2}(2v_1 + (50-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{50}{2}(2(12) + (50-1)(12)) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 15\\hspace{0.15em}300\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The common ratio of the geometric sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{w_2}{w_1} \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{24}{12} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula\n    $w_n = w_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 13$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.1em} w_{13} &= w_1r^{13-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\\hspace{0.05em}(2)^{13-1} \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 49\\hspace{0.15em}152\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA637","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA637\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA bouncy ball is dropped from a height of $2$ metres onto a concrete floor. After hitting the floor, the ball rebounds back up to $80$\\% of it's previous height, and this pattern continues on repeatedly, until coming to rest.\n\n1.  Show that the total distance travelled by the ball until coming to rest can be expressed by\n\n    $$\n    2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots\n    $$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total distance travelled by the ball.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    | |$n$|$\\hspace{1.5em}0\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}1\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}2\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}3\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n    |:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n    |distance travelled in metres|$n$th fall|2|$2(0.8)$|$2(0.8)^2$|$2(0.8)^3$|\n    | |$n$th rebound|0|$2(0.8)$|$2(0.8)^2$|$2(0.8)^3$|\n\n    $$\\\\[-1.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Combining the fall distances (top row) with the non-zero rebound distances (bottom row) gives the total distance travelled by the ball.\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\boxed{2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots}\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Applying the **sum of infinite geometric series (1.3)**, with $u_1 = 4$ and $r = 0.8$, to find the total distance travelled by the ball.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S &= 2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Adding and subtracting $2$ regroups the leading term as $4$, allowing the series to be rewritten as a single geometric series starting from $n=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S &= \\textcolor{blue}{(-2+2)} + 2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -2 + \\left[4 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots\\right] \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -2 + \\left[\\dfrac{4}{1 - 0.8}\\right] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[12pt]\n        S &= \\boxed{18 \\text{ metres}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA638","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA638\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the infinite geometric sequence $9000$, $-7200$, $5760$,\n$-4608$, ...\n\n1.  Find the common ratio. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $25$th term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the **exact** sum of the infinite sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 9000$ and $u_2 = -7200$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{7200}{9000} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{4}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 25$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{25} &= u_1r^{25-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9000\\left(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^{25-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 42.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{9000}{1-(-4/5)} \\hspace{1.9em} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 5000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA639","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA639\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nPeter is playing on a swing during a school lunch break. The height of\nthe first swing was $2$ m and every subsequent\nswing was $84$ % of the previous one. Peter's\nfriend, Ronald, gives him a push whenever the height falls below\n$1$ m.\n\n1.  Find the height of the third swing. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the number of swings before Ronald gives Peter a push.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the total height of swings if Peter is left to swing until\n    coming\\\n    to rest. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 2$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 0.84$.\n\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 3$,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_3 &= u_1r^{3-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\hspace{0.05em}(0.84)^{3-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 1.41 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the inequality $u_n < 1$ for $n$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r^{n-1} &< 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} 2\\hspace{0.05em}(0.84)^{n-1} &< 1 \\\\[7pt]\n    4.98 &< n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n       Hence\n    the swing needs a push after\n     $5$\n    swings.\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{2}{1-0.84} \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 12.5 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA640","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA640\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe sides of a square are $8$ cm long. A new\nsquare is formed by joining the midpoints of the adjacent sides and two\nof the resulting triangles are shaded as shown. This process is repeated\n$5$ more times to form the right hand diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA640.PNG\" alt=\"AA640\">\n\n1.  Find the total area of the shaded region in the right hand diagram\n    above. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total area of the shaded region if the process is repeated\n    indefinitely.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The side length of the first pair of shaded triangles is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{side length} &= \\dfrac{8}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm} \\hspace{2.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    their total area is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} \\text{area} &= 2\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(4)\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 16\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Similarly, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/e6f45d593aa89a3c2cdb821ce1c78f8d901f645f.svg\" alt=\"e6f45d593aa89a3c2cdb821ce1c78f8d901f645f.svg\">\n\n    Hence the area of successive pairs of shaded triangles form a\n    geometric $\\text{sequence}$ with the first term $u_1 = 16$\n    and the common ratio $r = 1/2$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$ with $n = 6$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} S_6 &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^6)}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{16\\hspace{0.05em}(1-(1/2)^6)}{1-1/2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 31.5 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{16}{1-1/2} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 32 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA668","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA668\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, the sum of the 2nd and 6th term is $32$.\n\nGiven that the sum of the first six terms is $120$, determine the first\nterm and common difference of the sequence.","markScheme":"Let $u_1$ be the first term of the arithmetic sequence and $d$ be the\ncommon difference. The first six terms are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+2d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+3d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+4d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+5d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_2 + u_6= 32\\qquad s_6=120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nthus, we can form two\nequations\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2u_1+6d &= 32 \\\\[5pt]\n6u_1+15d &= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can solve this system of\nlinear equations by using the elimination method or using our G.D.C. We\nget\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1=40 \\qquad d = -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA724","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA724\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA $3$D printer builds a set of $49$ Ei$\\text{f}$fel Tower\nReplicas in different sizes. The height of the largest tower in this set\nis $64$ cm. The heights of successive smaller\ntowers are $95$ % of the preceding larger tower,\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA724a.PNG\" alt=\"AA724a\">\n\n1.  Find the height of the smallest tower in this set. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the **total** height if all $49$ towers were placed one on top\n    of another. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 64$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 0.95$.\n\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n=49$,\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{49} &= u_1r^{49-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 64(0.95)^{49-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 5.46 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\n    with $n=49$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.9em} S_{49} &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^{49})}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{64\\hspace{0.05em}(1-0.95^{49})}{1-0.95} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 1176.33 \\\\[16pt]\n    &\\approx 1180 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA748","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA748\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe sum of the first three terms of a geometric sequence is $92.5$, and\nthe sum of the infinite sequence is $160$. Find the common ratio.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_3 &= 92.5 \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 92.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\infty} &= 160 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 160 \\\\[5pt]\nu_1 &= 160(1-r)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 92.5 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{160(1-r)(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 92.5 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n160(1-r^3) &= 92.5 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{9.3em} r = 0.75 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA832","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA832\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nAn arithmetic sequence has first term $45$ and common difference $-1.5$.\n\n1.  Given that the $k$th term of the sequence is zero, find the value of\n    $k$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $S_n$ denote the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence.\n\n2.  Find the maximum value of $S_n$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The $k$th term in the sequence is given by $u_k = u_1 + (k-1)d$ with\n    $u_1 = 45$ and $d = -1.5$.\n\n    Since $u_k = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    45 + (k-1)(-1.5) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}k = 31 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Since the common difference $d$ is negative, the terms in the\n    sequence are decreasing. From part (a), we found that the $31$st\n    term in the sequence is zero. Hence, any term after this would be\n    negative. Therefore, the sum $S_n$ is maximised when $n = 30$ or\n    $n = 31$ (as $u_{31}=0$).\n\n    Using the formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(u_1 + u_n \\right)$ with\n    $n = 31$, we find that the maximum value of $S_n$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(u_{1} + u_{31}) \\hspace{3em}\\\\[10pt]\n    S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(45 + 0) \\hspace{3em}\\\\[10pt]\n    &= 697.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA923","marks":"18","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA923\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe first three terms of an infinite sequence, in order, are \n\n$$2\\ln x,\\,\\, q\\ln x,\\,\\, \\ln \\sqrt{x}\\,\\,\\,  \\text{ where $\\ x > 0$.} $$\n\nFirst consider the case in which the series is geometric.\n\n1. 1. Find the possible values of $q$.\n\n    2. Hence or otherwise, show that the series is convergent. [3 marks]\n\n2. Given that $q>0$ and $S_\\infty=8\\ln{3}$, find the value of $x$. [3 marks]\n\nNow suppose that the series is arithmetic.\n\n3. 1. Show that $q=\\dfrac{5}{4}$.\n\n    2. Write down the common difference in the form $m\\ln x$, where $m \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. [4 marks]\n\n4. Given that the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence is $\\ln \\sqrt{x^5}$, find the value of $n$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Given that the sequence is geometric, we have for the common ratio\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{q\\ln x}{2\\ln x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{q}{2}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Similarly,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_3}{u_2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{q}{2} &= \\dfrac{\\ln \\sqrt{x}}{q\\ln x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{q}{2} &= \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{2}\\ln {x}}{q\\ln x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{q}{2} &= \\dfrac{1}{2q} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        q^2 &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.49em}\\boxed{q= \\pm 1}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. Given that $r=\\dfrac{q}{2}$, and $q= \\pm 1$, we have $r = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}$. Hence $|r| < 1$.\n\n        $$\\text{In both cases the series converges.}$$\n\n2. Given that $q>0$, $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$. \n\n    Using the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric sequence, we~get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8 \\ln 3 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln x}{1-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8 \\ln 3 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln x}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8 \\ln 3 &= 4\\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    2\\ln 3 &= \\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\ln 3^2 &= \\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\ln 9 &= \\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.54em}\\boxed{x = 9} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. 1. First we find the common difference $d$ as follows\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= u_2 - u_1\\\\[10pt]\n\n        d &= q\\ln x - 2\\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n        d &= (q-2)\\ln x\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Similarly,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= u_3 - u_2\\\\[10pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &= \\ln\\sqrt{x} - q\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &= \\ln x^{1/2} - q\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln x - q\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2} - q\\bigg)\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        q-2 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}-q \\\\[8pt]\n\n        2q &= \\dfrac{1}{2} + 2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        2q &= \\dfrac{5}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{q = \\dfrac{5}{4}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. Given that $d = (q-2)\\ln x$ and $q = \\dfrac{5}{4}$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= \\left(\\dfrac{5}{4}-2\\right)\\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{d = -\\dfrac{3}{4}\\ln x}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n4. Using the formula for the sum of $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_n &= \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\ln \\sqrt{x^5} &= \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(2\\hspace{0.1em}(2\\ln x) + (n-1)\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\ln x \\right)\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\ln x^{5/2} &= \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(\\left(4\\ln x + \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)(n-1)\\ln x\\right) \\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{5}{2}\\ln x &= \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(4 - \\dfrac{3(n-1)}{4}\\right)\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5 &= n\\left(4 - \\dfrac{3(n-1)}{4}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5 &= 4n - \\dfrac{3n(n-1)}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    20 &= 16n - 3n(n-1) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    20 &= 16n - 3n^2 + 3n \\\\[8pt]\n\n    3n^2 -19n +20 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    3n^2 -15n -4n +20 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    3n(n-5) - 4(n-5) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    (3n-4)(n-5) &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The solutions to the quadratic equation are $n=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ and $n=5$.\n\n    Since $n$ must be an integer, we have $n = 5$"},{"ref":"AA986","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA986\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ such that $0 < r < 1$.\n\n1. Show that $u_n - u_{n+1} = u_n(1-r)$.[2 marks]\n\nA geometric sequence has a first term of 150 and a second term of 120.\n\n2. Find the smallest value of $n$ such that $u_n - u_{n+1} < 1$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_n - u_{n+1} &= u_1r^{n-1} - u_1r^{n} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= u_1r^{n-1}(1-r) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-4.5em}\\boxed{u_n - u_{n+1} = u_n(1-r)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. For a geometric sequence, we have $r=\\dfrac{u_2}{u_1}$. Therefore $r = \\dfrac{120}{150}$.\n\n    Hence $u_1 = 150$ and $r=0.8$.\n\n    We are solving the inequality $u_n - u_{n+1} < 1$. Using our result from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_n(1-r) &< 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    u_n(1-0.8) &< 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    150(0.8)^{n-1}(0.2) &< 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    150(0.8)^{n-1} &< 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Sketching the graphs of $y=150(0.8)^{x-1}$ and $y=5$ on the G.D.C. we look for the smallest integer value of $x$ for which $y<5\\,$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/aa986.PNG\" alt=\"aa986\">\n\n    The smallest integer greater than $16.2$ is $17$. Hence\n\n    $$\\boxed{\\text{The smallest value of $n$ such that $u_n - u_{n+1}<1$ is $n=17$.}}$$"}]},{"name":"The Binomial Theorem","questions":[{"ref":"AA057","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA057\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nExpand $(2x - 3)^4$ in descending powers of $x$ and simplify your\nanswer.","markScheme":"Using Pascal triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/The%20Binomial%20Theorem/images/d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\" alt=\"d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\">\n\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} (2x - 3)^4 &= (2x)^4 + 4(2x)^3(-3) + 6(2x)^2(-3)^2 + 4(2x)(-3)^3 + (-3)^4 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 + 4(8x^3)(-3) + 6(4x^2)(9) + 4(2x)(-27) + 81 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 - 96x^3 + 216x^2 - 216x + 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA058","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA058\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nExpand $(2x + 1)^4$ in descending powers of $x$ and simplify your\nanswer.","markScheme":"Using Pascal triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/The%20Binomial%20Theorem/images/d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\" alt=\"d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\">\n\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} (2x + 1)^4 &= (2x)^4 + 4(2x)^3(1) + 6(2x)^2(1)^2 + 4(2x)(1)^3 + (1)^4 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 + 4(8x^3) + 6(4x^2) + 4(2x) + 1 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 + 32x^3 + 24x^2 + 8x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA059","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA059\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe third term, in descending powers of $x$, in the expansion of $(x+p)^8$ is $252x^6$. Find the\npossible values of $p$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} 252x^6 &= \\binom{8}{2}\\big(x^{8-2}\\big)\\big(p^2\\big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $p$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n252x^6 &= 28\\big(x^6\\big)\\big(p^2\\big) \\\\[9pt]\n252 &= 28p^2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{9.05em} p = \\pm 3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA060","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA060\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the expansion of\n$\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{2} + \\dfrac{a}{x}\\right)^6$. The constant term is\n$960$.\n\nFind the possible values of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.5em} 960 &= \\binom{6}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\bigg]^{6-4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{a}{x}\\bigg]^4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $a$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n960 &= 15\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^4}{4}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{a^4}{x^4}\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n960 &= \\dfrac{15a^4}{4} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{7.3em} a = \\pm 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA062","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA062\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $(2x-1)^9$.\n\n1.  Write down the number of terms in this expansion. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the coefficient of the term in $x^2$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of terms in this expansion is\n     $10$\n\n2.  The term in $x^2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{9}{7} \\big[(2x)^{9-7}\\big]\\big[(-1)^7\\big] &= 36\\big[4x^2\\big]\\big[-1\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -144x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^2$\n    is  $-144$"},{"ref":"AA063","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA063\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Write down the quadratic expression $3x^2 + 5x - 2$ in the form\n    $(ax-b)(x+c)$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the coefficient of the term in $x^9$ in\n    the expansion\\\n    of $(3x^2+5x-2)^5$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $(3x - 1)(x + 2)$\n\n2.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (3x^2+5x-2)^5 &= (3x-1)^5(x+2)^5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left[\\sum_{k = 0}^5 \\binom{5}{k}\\big[(3x)^{5-k}\\big]\\big[(-1)^k\\big]\\right]\\left[\\sum_{k=0}^5\\binom{5}{k}\\big(x^{5-k}\\big)\\big(2^k\\big)\\right]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^9$ in the expansion of\n    $(3x^2+5x-2)^5$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a_9 &= \\left[\\binom{5}{0}\\big[3^{5-0}\\big]\\big[(-1)^0\\big]\\right]\\left[\\binom{5}{1}\\big(2^1\\big)\\right] + \\left[\\binom{5}{1}\\big[3^{5-1}\\big]\\big[(-1)^1\\big]\\right]\\left[\\binom{5}{0}\\big(2^0\\big)\\right] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\big[(1)(3^5)(1)\\big]\\big[(5)(2)\\big] + \\big[(5)(3^4)(-1)\\big]\\big[(1)(1)\\big] \\\\[15pt]\n    &= 2025\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA066","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA066\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write down and simplify the expansion of $(3-x)^5$ in descending\n    order of powers of $x$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the exact value of $(2.9)^5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (3-x)^5 &= ((-x) + 3)^5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sum_{k = 0}^5 \\binom{5}{k}\\big[(-x)^{5-k}\\big]\\big[3^k\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -x^5 + 15x^4 - 90x^3 + 270x^2 - 405x + 243\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Hence, substituting $x = 0.1$ in the expression $(1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2.9)^5 &=  -(0.1)^5 + 15(0.1)^4 - 90(0.1)^3 + 270(0.1)^2 - 405(0.1) + 243 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -0.00001 + 0.0015 - 0.09 + 2.7 - 40.5 + 243 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -40.59001 + 245.7015 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 205.11149\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA067","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA067\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn the expansion of $(2x + 1)^n$, the coefficient of the term in $x^2$\nis $40n$, where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. Find $n$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40nx^2 &= \\binom{n}{2}\\big[1^{n-2}\\big]\\big[(2x)^2\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $n$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40nx^2 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}\\bigg]\\big[4x^2\\big] \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[18pt]\n40n &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\bigg]\\big[4\\big] \\\\[18pt]\n20n &= n(n-1) \\\\[18pt]\n0 &= n(n - 21) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nn =21 \\hspace{5.1em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA068","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA068\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn the expansion of $x(2x + 1)^n$, the coefficient of the term in $x^3$\nis $20n$, where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. Find $n$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20nx^3 &= x\\binom{n}{2}\\big[1^{n-2}\\big]\\big[(2x)^2\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $n$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20nx^3 &= x\\bigg[\\dfrac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}\\bigg]\\big[4x^2\\big] \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n20nx^3 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\bigg]\\big[4x^3\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n10n &= n(n-1) \\\\[18pt]\n0 &= n(n - 11) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nn =11 \\hspace{5.7em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA069","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA069\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGiven that $(1 + x)^3(1 + px)^4 = 1 + qx + 93x^2 + \\dots + p^4x^7$, find\nthe possible values of $p$ and $q$.","markScheme":"Using Pascal triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/The%20Binomial%20Theorem/images/d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\" alt=\"d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\">\n\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + qx + 93x^2 + \\dots + p^4x^7 &= (1 + x)^3(1 + px)^4 \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\big(1 + 3x + 3x^2\\hspace{-0.15em} + x^3\\big)\\hspace{-0.15em}\\big(1 + 4px + 6p^2x^2\\hspace{-0.15em} + 4p^3x^3\\hspace{-0.15em} + p^4x^4\\big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nqx &= 4px + 3x \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\nq &= 4p + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} 93x^2 &= 6p^2x^2 + 12px^2 + 3x^2 \\\\[6pt]\n93 &= 6p^2 + 12p + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(2)$ for $p$, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6p^2 + 12p + 3 &= 93 \\\\[6pt]\n6p^2 + 12p - 90 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\np^2 + 2p - 15 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(p + 5)(p - 3) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\np &= -5,\\,3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, substituting $p = -5,\\,3$ in the\nequation $(1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.1em} q &= 4(-5) + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= -17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.3em} q &= 4(3) + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n $p = -5,\\,q=-17$\nand  $p = 3,\\,q=15$"},{"ref":"AA071","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA071\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\bigg(x^3+\\dfrac{2}{x}\\bigg)^8$.\n\n1.  Write down the number of terms in this expansion. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the coefficient of the term in $x^4$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of terms in this expansion is\n     $9$\n\n2.  The term in $x^4$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{5} \\big[x^3\\big]^{8-5} \\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{x}\\bigg]^5&= 56\\big[x^9\\big]\\bigg[\\dfrac{32}{x^5}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1792x^4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^4$\n    is  $1792$"},{"ref":"AA072","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA072\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\bigg(3x + \\dfrac{p}{x}\\bigg)^8$, where\n$p > 0$. The coefficient of the term\n\nin $x^4$ is equal to the coefficient of the term in $x^6$. Find $p$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigg(3x+\\dfrac{p}{x}\\bigg)^8 &= \\sum_{k=0}^8 \\binom{8}{k} \\big[3x\\big]^{8-k}\\bigg[\\dfrac{p}{x}\\bigg]^k \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sum_{k=0}^8 \\binom{8}{k} \\big[3^{8-k}p^k\\big]\\big[x^{8-2k}\\big] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^4$}\\big] &= \\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^6$}\\big] \\hspace{5.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\binom{8}{2}\\big[3^{8-2}p^2\\big] &= \\binom{8}{1}\\big[3^{8-1}p\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n28\\big[3^6p^2\\big] &= 8\\big[3^7p\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n20\\hspace{0.15em}412\\hspace{0.05em}p^2 - 17\\hspace{0.15em}496\\hspace{0.05em}p &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.1em} p = \\dfrac{6}{7}\n \\approx 0.857... \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$\n\n <!-- $$\n \\text{or} \\hspace{5.7em}\n $$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{6.6em} p \\approx 0.857 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$ -->"},{"ref":"AA073","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA073\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the expansion of $px^2(5 + px)^8$, the coefficient of the term in\n$x^6$ is $3402$. Find the value of $p$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3402x^6 &= px^2\\binom{8}{4}\\big[5^{8-4}\\big]\\big[(px)^4\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $p$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3402x^6 &= px^2\\big[70\\big]\\big[5^4\\big]\\big[p^4x^4\\big] \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n3402 &= 43\\hspace{0.15em}750\\hspace{0.05em}p^5 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.3em} p =0.6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1045","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1045\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe coefficient of the variable $x^5$ in the expansion of $(x+2a)^8$, where $a\\in \\mathbb{R}$, is $56$.\n\nFind the value of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (x+2a)^8 &=x^8+\\binom{8}{1}(x)^7(2a)^1+...+\\binom{8}{r}(x)^{(8-r)}(2a)^r+...+(2a)^8 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to identify the $r^{\\text{th}}$ term which includes $x^5$, hence, $r=8-5=3$,\n$$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\binom{8}{3}\\big(x^{5}\\big)\\big(2a\\big)^3 &= 56x^5 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the formula we can evaluate the binomial coefficient\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{3}&=\\frac{8!}{3!5!}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=56\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cancel{56})(\\cancel{x^5})(8a^3) &= \\cancel{56}\\cancel{x^5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[9pt]\n8a^3 &= 1 \\\\[7pt]\na^3 &= \\dfrac{1}{8} \\\\[7pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{a = \\dfrac{1}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1152","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1152\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the expansion of $(a+bx)^6$, the coefficient of the $x^3$ term is $-2$ times the coefficient of the $x^4$ term.\n\nDetermine the value of $\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}$.","markScheme":"From the formula booklet we can find the formula for a general term of the expansion\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (a+b)^n&=...+\\binom{n}{r}a^{n-r}b^r+...\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the general term above, we can write an expression for the $x^3$ term and the $x^4$ term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &\\binom{6}{3}(a)^3(bx)^3& \\binom{6}{4}(a)^2(bx)^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use the information given to write an equation linking the coefficients of the two expressions above.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &\\binom{6}{3}a^3b^3=-2\\times \\binom{6}{4}a^2b^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula we can evaluate the binomial coefficients and simplify\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{6!}{3!3!}a^3b^3&=-2\\times \\frac{6!}{4!2!}a^2b^4\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{6\\times 5 \\times 4\\times \\cancel{3!}}{\\cancel{3!}\\times3\\times 2\\times 1}a^3b^3&=-2\\times \\frac{6\\times 5 \\times  \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}\\times 2 \\times 1}a^2b^4\\\\[6pt]\n    20a^3b^3&=-30a^2b^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe need to find the value of $\\tfrac{a}{b}$ therefore we can rearrange the expression above\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{a^3b^3}{a^2b^4}&=\\frac{-30}{20}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{a}{b}&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{-3}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-1.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA120","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA120\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\bigg(2x^6+\\dfrac{x^2}{q}\\bigg)^{10}$, \n$q \\neq 0$. The coefficient of the term\n\nin $x^{40}$ is twelve times the coefficient of the term in $x^{36}$.\nFind $q$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.5em} \\bigg(2x^6+\\dfrac{x^2}{q}\\bigg)^{10} &= \\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} \\big[2x^6\\big]^{10-k}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2}{q}\\bigg]^k \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} \\big[2^{10-k}q^{-k}\\big]\\big[x^{60-4k}\\big] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^{40}$}\\big] &= 12\\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^{36}$}\\big] \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\binom{10}{5}\\big[2^{10-5}q^{-5}\\big] &= 12\\binom{10}{6}\\big[2^{10-6}q^{-6}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n252\\big[2^5q^{-5}\\big] &= 12(210)\\big[2^4q^{-6}\\big] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{6.9em} q = 5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA123","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA123\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGiven that\n$(5+nx)^2\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{3}{5}x\\bigg)^n\\hspace{-0.25em}=\\hspace{0.05em}25+100x+\\cdots$,\nfind the value of $n$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5+nx)^2\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{3}{5}x\\bigg)^n &= \\big[25 + 10nx + n^2x^2\\big]\\left[\\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom{n}{k}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3x}{5}\\bigg]^k\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe term in $x$ in the expansion of the product $(1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25\\binom{n}{1}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3x}{5}\\bigg]^1 + 10nx\\binom{n}{0}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3x}{5}\\bigg]^0 &= 15nx + 10nx  \\\\[6pt]\n&= 25nx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25n &= 100 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nn = 4 \\hspace{2.1em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1273","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1273\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  1.  Consider the following equation $2\\binom{n}{r}=\\binom{n}{r+1}$.\n\n        Show that it can be written as $3r+2=n$. \n\n    2.  Now consider the following equation $7\\binom{n}{r-1}=2\\binom{n}{r}$.\n\n    \n\n        Show that it can be written as $9r-2=2n$.  [4 marks]\n\nConsider the expansion\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(1+x)^n=1+a_1x+...+a_{k-1}x^{k-1}+a_{k}x^{k}+a_{k+1}x^{k+1}+...+x^n\n\n\\end{aligned} \n\n$$\n\nWhere $a_i \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    \n\nThe coefficients of three consecutive terms of the expansion are such that\n\n$$ \n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n7\\times a_{k-1}&=2\\times a_{k} \\hspace{1em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{1em} 14\\times a_{k}=7\\times a_{k+1}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n2.  Find $n$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the combinations formula $\\binom{n}{r}=\\tfrac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}$ we can rewrite both the LHS and the RHS of the equation\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            2\\left(\\dfrac{\\cancel{n!}}{r!(n-r)!}\\right)&=\\dfrac{\\cancel{n!}}{(r+1)!(n-(r+1))!}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can simplify and rearrange the equation\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{2(r+1)!}{r!}&=\\dfrac{(n-r)!}{(n-r-1)!}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Rewrite the numerators using the concept $n!=n(n-1)!$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{2(r+1)r!}{r!}&=\\dfrac{(n-r)(n-r-1)!}{(n-r-1)!} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We can cancel out the factorial terms\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{2(r+1)\\cancel{r!}}{\\cancel{r!}}&=\\dfrac{(n-r)\\cancel{(n-r-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-r-1)!}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            2(r+1)&=n-r\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &\\hspace{-2.8em} \\boxed{3r+2=n} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As required.\n\n        \n\n    2.  Rewrite the equation using the combinations formula and simplify in a similar way to part (a)\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            7\\left(\\dfrac{n!}{(r-1)!(n-(r-1))!}\\right)&=2\\left(\\dfrac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\dfrac{7}{(r-1)!(n-r+1)!}&=\\dfrac{2}{r!(n-r)!}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\dfrac{7r!}{(r-1)!}&=\\dfrac{2(n-r+1)!}{(n-r)!}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\dfrac{7r\\cancel{(r-1)!}}{\\cancel{(r-1)!}}&=\\dfrac{2(n-r+1)\\cancel{(n-r)!}}{\\cancel{(n-r)!}} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            7r&=2n-2r+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &\\hspace{-2.8em} \\boxed{9r-2=2n} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As required.\n\n2.  We can begin by expanding $(1+x)^n$, using the binomial theorem, in terms of $n$ and $r$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (1+x)^n&=1+\\binom{n}{1}x+\\binom{n}{2}x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &\\textcolor{red}{+\\binom{n}{r-1}x^{r-1}+\\binom{n}{r}x^r+\\binom{n}{r+1}x^{r+1}+...}  \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &+x^n\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The terms in red represent any three consecutive terms in the expansion.\n\n    We are told in the question the way in which the coefficients of three consecutive terms are related.\n\n    $$ \n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n    (1+x)^n=1+a_1x+...+\\textcolor{red}{a_{k-1}x^{k-1}+a_{k}x^{k}+a_{k+1}x^{k+1}}+...+x^n\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence we can use the information given to write the following two equations in terms of binomial coefficients \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &&7\\times a_{k-1}&=2\\times a_{k}& &&14\\times a_{k}&=7\\times a_{k+1}&  \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &&7\\binom{n}{r-1}&=2\\binom{n}{r} &\\hspace{-1em} && 14\\binom{n}{r}&=7\\binom{n}{r+1}& \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &&&&\\hspace{-1em} && 2\\binom{n}{r}&=\\binom{n}{r+1}& \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In part (a) we already rewrote these equations without the combination notation, lets call them $[1]$ and $[2]$. \n\n    We can now solve them simultaneously and find $n$ and $r$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\\n\n        3r+2&=n \\hspace{4.4em} [1]\\\\\n\n        9r-2&=2n \\hspace{4em} [2]\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Multiply $[1]$ by $3$ and subtract equation $[2]$ from this $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{result}}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{red}{9r}-9r+\\textcolor{red}{6}-(-2)&=\\textcolor{red}{3n}-2n\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.6em}\\boxed{n=8} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1328","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1328\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the expansion $(2x+1)^n$, where $n\\in\\mathbb Z^+$, the coefficient of $x^3$ is equal to six times the difference between of the coefficient of $x^2$ and the coefficient of $x$.\n\nFind the value of $n$.","markScheme":"Let's use the binomial theorem from the formula booklet, to start expanding this binomial. \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(a+b)^n&= a^n+\\binom{n}{1}a^{n-1}b +...+ \\binom{n}{r}a^{n-r}b^r +...\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThe given information relates to coefficients of the $x$, $x^2$, and $x^3$ terms so we want to expand in ascending powers of $x$. For that we need to switch the terms of the binomial so that $a=1$ and $b=2x$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(1+2x)^n &= (1)^n + \\binom{n}{1}(1)^{n-1}(2x)+\\binom{n}{2}(1)^{n-2}(2x)^2+\\binom{n}{3}(1)^{n-3}(2x)^3 + ...\\\\[12pt]\n\n&=1+\\binom{n}{1}2x+\\binom{n}{2}4x^2+\\binom{n}{3}8x^3+...\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe are told that six times the difference of the coefficients of the $x^2$ term and $x$ term is equal to the coefficient of the $x^3$ term.\n\nUsing this relationship we can form an equation.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n6\\left[4\\binom{n}{2}-2\\binom{n}{1}\\right]&=8\\binom{n}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n24\\binom{n}{2}-12\\binom{n}{1}&=8\\binom{n}{3}\\\\[12pt]\n\n6\\binom{n}{2}-3\\binom{n}{1}&=2\\binom{n}{3}\\hspace{5em}[1]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow let's use the formula for the binomial coefficient.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\binom{n}{r}&=\\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nEvaluating the formula when $r=1$, $r=2$, and $r=3$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\binom{n}{1}&=\\frac{n!}{1!(n-1)!}=n\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\binom{n}{2}&=\\frac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\binom{n}{3}&=\\frac{n!}{3!(n-3)!}=\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow we substitute these into equation [1] and solve for $n$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n 6\\left(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\right)-3n&=2\\left(\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{6}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n3n(n-1)-3n&=\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3}\\\\[9pt]\n\n9n(n-1)-9n&=n(n-1)(n-2)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nFrom here we can factorise $9n$ on the L.H.S.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n9n(n-1)-9n&=n(n-1)(n-2)\\\\[6pt]\n\n9n(n-1-1)&=n(n-1)(n-2)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n9n(n-2)&=n(n-1)(n-2)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nAs we know that $n\\in\\mathbb Z^+$ we know that $n\\neq0$. Also, because the $x^3$ term exists, $n>2$. Therefore we can divide both sides by $n$ and by $n-2$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n 9\\cancel{n}\\cancel{(n-2)}&=\\cancel{n}(n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n 9&=n-1\\\\[6pt]\n\nn&=10\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA749","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA749\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the expansion of $(x-3)^8$.\n\n1.  Write down the number of terms in this expansion. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the coefficient of the term in $x^6$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of terms in this expansion is\n     $9$\n\n2.  The term in $x^6$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{2} \\big[(x)^{8-2}\\big]\\big[(-3)^2\\big] &= 28\\big[x^6\\big]\\big[9\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 252x^6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^6$\n    is  $252$"},{"ref":"AA988","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA988\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\dfrac{(x+a)^7}{bx}$, where $a > 0$. The coefficient of the term in $x^5$ is $2$, and the coefficient of the term in $x^3$ is $1690$.\n\nFind the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.","markScheme":"Because each term in the expansion of $(x+a)^7$ will be divided by $bx$, we require the terms in $x^6$ and $x^4$. Using the Binomial Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{Term in $x^6$} &= \\binom{7}{1}x^6a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 7x^6a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\text{Term in $x^4$} &= \\binom{7}{3}x^4a^3 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 35x^4a^3\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nDividing both these terms by $bx$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{7x^6a}{bx} &= \\dfrac{7x^5a}{b} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{35x^4a^3}{bx} &= \\dfrac{35x^3a^3}{b}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nGiven that the coefficient of $x^5$ is $2$, and the coefficient of $x^3$ is $1690$, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{7a}{b} &= 2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{35a^3}{b} &= 1690\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nAnd therefore \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    7a &= 2b \\tag*{[1]}\\\\[8pt] \n\n    35a^3 &= 1690b \\tag*{[2]}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nDividing equation [2] by equation [1] we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{35a^3}{7a} &= \\dfrac{1690b}{2b} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5a^2 &= 845 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2 &= 169 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a &= \\pm 13\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nGiven that $a>0$, $\\boxed{a = 13}$. Substituting into equation [1],\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    7a &= 2b \\\\[8pt]\n\n    7(13) &= 2b \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{b = 45.5}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA410","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA410\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe following table shows values of $\\ln x$ and $\\ln y$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20SL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/8c64f77af5ec93279ceb96452d0a87f353ec5871.svg\" alt=\"8c64f77af5ec93279ceb96452d0a87f353ec5871.svg\">\n\nThe relationship between $\\ln x$ and $\\ln y$ can be modelled by the\nregression equation $\\ln y = a \\ln x + b$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Use the regression equation to estimate the value of\n     $y$  when\n     $x = 4.12$. [3 marks]\n\nThe relationship between $x$ and $y$ can be modelled using the formula\n$y = px^q$, where $p\\neq 0$ and $q \\neq 0$, $q\\neq 1$.\n\n3.  Expressing $\\ln y$ in terms of $\\ln x$, find the value of\n    $p$ and the value of $q$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a \\approx -0.564$\n    and  $b \\approx 4.74$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.  Substituting $x = 4.12$ in the equation\n    $\\ln y = -0.564\\ln x + 4.74$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\ln y &= -0.564\\ln (4.12) + 4.74 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= e^{-0.564\\ln (4.12) + 4.74} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    y \\approx 51.5 \\hspace{6em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln y &= -0.564\\ln x + 4.74 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln x^{-0.564} + \\ln e^{4.74} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln\\hspace{-0.15em}\\big([x^{-0.564}][e^{4.74}]\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= [e^{4.74}][x^{-0.564}] \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 114\\hspace{0.05em}x^{-0.564} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n     $p \\approx 114$\n    and\n     $q \\approx -0.564$"},{"ref":"AA987","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA987\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA species of bird, thought to be extinct, was rediscovered on 14 March 1973. Through a careful protection and breeding program, the population has continued to increase since then.\n\nThe Department of conservation proposes that the population of birds $t$ years after 14 March 1973 can be modelled by the function $P(t)=10e^{kt}$, where $0 < k < 1$ and $t \\geq 0$.\n\nOn 14 March 1993 the known population of birds was 50.\n\n1. Use the model to estimate the population of birds on 14 March 2023.[4 marks]\n\nIn fact, the known population of birds on 14 March 2023 was 502. A new model is proposed in which\n\n* The population on March 14 1973 was 10.\n\n* The population on March 14 2023 was 502.\n\n* The population increased at a constant $r\\%$ per year.\n\n2. Find the value of $r$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 14 March 1993 is 20 years after 14 March 1973, therefore $P(20)=50$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(20) &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    50 &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^{20k} &= 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $k\\approx 0.08047$. Hence our model is $P(t)=10e^{0.08047t}$.\n\n    14 March 2023 is 50 years after 14 March 1973, hence we calculate $P(50)$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(50) &= 10e^{0.08047 \\times 50} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-2.4em}\\boxed{P(50)\\approx559}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. If something increases at a constant rate, the compound interest formula applies. Using the formula for compound interest with $PV=10$, $FV=502$, $k=1$ and $n=50$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    FV &= PV \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\right)^{kn} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    502 &= 10 \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100}\\right)^{50}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $\\boxed{r\\approx 8.15}$"}]}]}]};
